{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj95z51SvLGWvek9BDzf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EYUUNZGAbGyMdKXRxT2i2k","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3215,"cache_read_input_tokens":43916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3215},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"865df1a7-1f18-4f9b-b86a-40aa7a478e11","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:54.161Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj95yo9YLnkuNMUq6FZ3","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EYUUNZGAbGyMdKXRxT2i2k":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EYUUNZGAbGyMdKXRxT2i2k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test-game feedback\n2\t\n3\tEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\n4\tthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n5\t`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\n6\tagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\n7\tfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\n8\tunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n9\t\n10\t**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\n11\toriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\n12\tengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\n13\tevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\n14\tchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\n15\thurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n16\t`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\n17\tthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n18\t\n19\t**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\n20\tgame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\n21\tis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n22\t1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n23\t   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n24\t2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n25\t3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n26\t\n27\tThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\n28\treview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n29\t\n30\t**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\n31\tcost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n32\t\n33\t## Tally (1 game)\n34\t| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n35\t|---|---|---|---|\n36\t| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n37\t| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n38\t| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n39\t| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n40\t| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n41\t| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n42\t| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n43\t| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n44\t| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n45\t| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n46\t\n47\t| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n48\t|---|---|---|\n49\t| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n50\t| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n51\t| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n52\t\n53\t## Games\n54\t\n55\t### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n56\t- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n57\t  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n58\t  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n59\t  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n60\t  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n61\t- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n62\t- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n63\t  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n64\t  inside the engine crates.\n65\t- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n66\t  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n67\t  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n68\t- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n69\t  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n70\t  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n71\t- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n72\t  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n73\t  waits for a second game.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"4ff53486-e4eb-4147-a613-40d375545487","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:54.172Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/FEEDBACK.md","content":"# Test-game feedback\n\nEngine feedback from test games: a fresh agent builds a vertical slice of a 1985-2015 classic on\nthis engine, then evaluates the engine. Each game is stored as one self-contained HTML file in\n`michaelcrosato/ai-hub` (`games/`, source zip in `sources/`). The maintainer checks each claim\nagainst the code, fixes what blocks the next game, and waits for patterns before larger work:\nfeatures everyone uses, features no one uses, problems that keep coming back. Newest game first\nunder \"Games\"; the tally is updated with every game.\n\n**Prompt.** The user's template, with the game's name, year and platforms as SOURCE: build an\noriginal spiritual successor as one complete, polished level that doubles as a tech demo; use the\nengine, extend it as needed; deliver one self-contained HTML file; then a one-page candid\nevaluation of the engine (used/not used, engine work you wrote ranked by output saved, engine\nchanges, bugs and wrong docs, source you had to read, fidelity limits, testing, what helped and\nhurt, missing features by impact, the one change). The questions get adjusted as patterns show. Since 2026-10-16 the agent starts from\n`START_HERE.md`'s \"Make a game\" prompt: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` and the starter game, ending with\nthe guide's engine feedback section, which feeds this log.\n\n**Asset store lines** (the store's S3, 2026-10-15; the user pastes them into the template). Each\ngame's answers count toward the store's critical mass (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md` §13): a need\nis met when the game placed, forked or derived a store entry instead of making it from scratch.\n1. Before you make a prop, material or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the\n   engine's asset store and `assets find=words` searches it (`store/README.md`).\n2. Assets you looked for: one line each, the query and the id you used, or `none`.\n3. Up to 5 things you built that another game could reuse: the file and one line each.\n\nThe user flags the store entries each game used on a per-game review page (`scripts/\nreview_page.py` with a `flags` manifest): looks, feels, reads, works or broken (§7).\n\n**Cost** is counted end to end from the agent's transcript (model calls, tokens read, weighted\ncost at cache reads 0.1, cache writes 1.25, visible output 5; hidden reasoning is not recorded).\n\n## Tally (1 game)\n| Asked for | Games | Impact | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A supported second-game path: a `Game` hook for step, frame, view and HUD instead of editing `sim.rs`, `frame.rs`, `build.rs` and `app.rs` | 1 | high | **done** (2026-10-16): `pav_core::games` (step, HUD text, status, bot), the starter game, `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`; drawing and HUD widgets still mean view or app code |\n| Entities, shots, health and AI that don't require rapier (`health.rs`, `projectile.rs`) | 1 | high | waiting |\n| A single-file web build (gzip + inline) | 1 | high | **done**: `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file` |\n| Primitive-part models drawn per frame from game state (props can't be) | 1 | medium | **done**: `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (plan row 4.1) |\n| Stock HUD widgets (bars, radar, off-screen markers, boss bar, banner) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Data-driven sound bank (`pav_audio::sounds_for` is a closed match) | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| Per-scene default camera on `load` | 1 | medium | waiting |\n| HUD visible to headless capture | 1 | medium | **done** (2026-10-19): captures draw the game UI (a hook game's HUD lines too); `ui` reads it as text |\n| Open top-down map data (roads, water, spawns) | 1 | low | waiting |\n| Continuous-play and top-down map captures | 1 | low | waiting |\n\n| Engine part | Used | Skipped |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Sim` (rewind, `state_hash`, snapshot), `pav_render::Scene`, particle presets, `pav_audio` synth, `pav_tools` capture/repl/input, the wasm shell and egui, `Rng`/`InputFrame`/bindings | 1 | 0 |\n| rapier entities, `character.rs`, `nav.rs`, `health.rs`, `projectile.rs`, `vehicle.rs` helicopter, `course.rs`, `zones.rs`, `stealth.rs`, `ai.rs` | 0 | 1 |\n| Rooms/TOML, puppet, moves, clips, the asset and creature studios (props, format 2) | 0 | 1 |\n\n## Games\n\n### Capital Strike, from Jungle Strike (1993; SNES, Genesis) — 2026-10-12\n- **Result:** one mission over Washington. Gun, rockets, homing missiles, flares and a dash;\n  armour, fuel and ammo; winch rescues; a motorcade escort; SAMs, AA guns, tanks, gunships and a\n  three-phase boss; loadouts, difficulties, checkpoints, radar and a procedural soundtrack.\n  `ai-hub/games/Jungle-Strike-Capital-Strike-2026-10-12-model-unknown.html` (9.8 MB); source as a\n  patch series against `6618522` in `ai-hub/sources/`.\n- **Agent:** Sonnet at medium effort, a subagent in its own worktree of this repo.\n- **Cost:** 1.65 h, 170 model calls, 54M tokens read (context grew from 56k to 485k), about 6.5M\n  weighted; 106k tokens of visible output. About 4,270 lines written, 3,965 of them game code\n  inside the engine crates.\n- **Verdict (agent's):** the renderer, wasm shell, audio synth and headless tools carried the\n  visuals and the first wasm build ran; the gameplay half didn't help a non-RPG, non-physics game,\n  so 4,000 lines went into a game layer with no engine facility, and the packaging was all its own.\n- **Checked:** the claims about `vehicle.rs` and `health.rs` hold (rapier-bound; no weapons,\n  damage or fuel), and AGENTS.md now says so. The game didn't touch props, format 2 or the studios:\n  its units are SDF and cube parts drawn in code, because a prop can't be drawn from game state.\n- **Acted on:** the single-file web build (every test game needs one), the AGENTS.md wording, and\n  the asset plan's row 4.1 now includes drawing a library asset from game code. Everything else\n  waits for a second game.\n","numLines":74,"startLine":1,"totalLines":74}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EYUUNZGAbGyMdKXRxT2i2k","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj95z51SvLGWvek9BDzf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FUx8e2K2nUiVhjjMYVMv2m","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/AGENTS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3215,"cache_read_input_tokens":43916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3215},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"7e283fef-ad79-4755-9bdb-be096d50e060","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:54.713Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj95yo9YLnkuNMUq6FZ3","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FUx8e2K2nUiVhjjMYVMv2m":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/AGENTS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FUx8e2K2nUiVhjjMYVMv2m","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n2\t\n3\tMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\n4\tyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n5\t\n6\tShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\n7\tprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n8\t`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\n9\tthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\n10\tread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\n11\ttool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\n12\tneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\n13\tread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\n14\tCLI examples.\n15\t\n16\t## How the doctrine applies here\n17\tAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\n18\tfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n19\t- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n20\t  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n21\t  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n22\t  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n23\t- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n24\t  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n25\t  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n26\t- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n27\t  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n28\t  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n29\t  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n30\t  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n31\t  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n32\t  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n33\t- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n34\t  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n35\t- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n36\t  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n37\t  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n38\t  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n39\t  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n40\t  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n41\t  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n42\t- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n43\t  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n44\t  log it and continue.\n45\t- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n46\t  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n47\t\n48\t## Working rules\n49\tUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n50\t1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n51\t2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n52\t3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n53\t   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n54\t4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n55\t5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n56\t   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n57\t6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n58\t\n59\t## Testing budget (prototype stage)\n60\tEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\n61\tmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\n62\trecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n63\t- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n64\t  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n65\t  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n66\t- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n67\t  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n68\t  errors.\n69\t- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n70\t  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n71\t  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n72\t  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n73\t- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n74\t  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n75\t  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n76\t  are noise.\n77\t- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n78\t  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n79\t  the user on a quick comparison page.\n80\t- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n81\t  a verified benefit.\n82\t- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n83\t  whole context.\n84\t- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n85\t  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n86\t  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n87\t- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n88\t  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n89\t    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n90\t  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n91\t  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n92\t    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n93\t  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n94\t    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n95\t\n96\t## Architecture\n97\tCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n98\t- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n99\t  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n100\t  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n101\t- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n102\t  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n103\t- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n104\t- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n105\t- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n106\t  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n107\t- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n108\t  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n109\t- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n110\t  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n111\t\n112\tData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n113\t`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\n114\tmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\n115\tData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n116\t-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n117\t\n118\tKey rules:\n119\t- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n120\t- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n121\t- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n122\t  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n123\t  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n124\t- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n125\t  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n126\t- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n127\t- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n128\t  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n129\t\n130\t## Build, run, test\n131\t```sh\n132\tscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\n133\tcargo build                        # everything (Linux)\n134\tcargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\n135\tcargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\n136\tcargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\n137\tcargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\n138\tscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\n139\tscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\n140\tscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\n141\tscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\n142\tscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\n143\tscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n144\t```\n145\t`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\n146\tother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\n147\tBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\n148\tsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\n149\tbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\n150\tChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\n151\tin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\n152\tDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\n153\tincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n154\t(or run `cargo clean`).\n155\t\n156\t## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\n157\tEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\n158\tMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\n159\tthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\n160\tand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n161\t```sh\n162\tcargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\n163\tpav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\n164\tpav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\n165\tprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\n166\tprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n167\t```\n168\t- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n169\t  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n170\t  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n171\t- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n172\t  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n173\t- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n174\t  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n175\t- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n176\t  use them for bug reports.\n177\t- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n178\t  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n179\t  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n180\t- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n181\t  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n182\t  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n183\t  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n184\t  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n185\t  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n186\t  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n187\t- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n188\t  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n189\t  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n190\t  assets in game code\").\n191\t- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n192\t  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n193\t- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n194\t  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n195\t  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n196\t  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n197\t\n198\tAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\n199\tIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n200\t\n201\tTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\n202\tspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\n203\troom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\n204\tlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\n205\tloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\n206\tlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\n207\tanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\n208\tasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\n209\tcreature_status`.\n210\t\n211\t### Adding a tool\n212\t1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n213\t   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n214\t2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n215\t   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n216\t3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n217\t4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n218\t5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n219\t6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n220\t\n221\t### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\n222\tCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n223\t(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\n224\tis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\n225\tteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n226\t(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n227\t`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\n228\tfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\n229\thot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\n230\tfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n231\t\n232\t### Scenes\n233\t`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n234\t`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\n235\tstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\n236\tMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\n237\tGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\n238\tCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n239\t\n240\t## Tests\n241\t`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n242\t`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n243\t`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\n244\textend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\n245\tlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n246\t\n247\t## Conventions\n248\t- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n249\t- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n250\t  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n251\t- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n252\t  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n253\t  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n254\t- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n255\t  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n256\t  the PR); don't ask first.\n257\t- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n258\t  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n259\t- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n260\t  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n261\t\n262\t## Code Review Rules\n263\tFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n264\t- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n265\t  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n266\t  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n267\t- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n268\t- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\n269\tLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n270\t(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n271\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"e5009ac7-62be-459b-8c78-bd0d7c1681e7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:54.722Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n\nMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\nyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n\nShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\nprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\nthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\nread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\ntool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\nneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\nread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\nCLI examples.\n\n## How the doctrine applies here\nAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\nfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\nin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\nDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\nincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n(or run `cargo clean`).\n\n## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\nEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\nMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\nthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\nand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\npav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\npav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\nprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\nprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n```\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports.\n- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n  assets in game code\").\n- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n\nAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\nIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n\nTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\nspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\nroom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\nlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\nloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\nlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\nanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\nasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\ncreature_status`.\n\n### Adding a tool\n1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n\n### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\nCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\nis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\nteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\nfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\nhot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\nfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n\n### Scenes\n`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\nstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\nMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\nGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\nCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\nextend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\nlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n\n## Conventions\n- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n  the PR); don't ask first.\n- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n\n## Code Review Rules\nFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\nLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n","numLines":271,"startLine":1,"totalLines":271}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FUx8e2K2nUiVhjjMYVMv2m","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
