{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DN6SssSanxdtqEQXxccMhL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Device-independent input for one simulation tick. The host (window, agent, replay) fills\n2\t//! it; the simulation never sees devices. Directions are already in world space.\n3\t\n4\tuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\n5\tuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n6\t\n7\tpub mod buttons {\n8\t    pub const JUMP: u32 = 1 << 0;\n9\t    pub const CROUCH: u32 = 1 << 1;\n10\t    pub const INTERACT: u32 = 1 << 2;\n11\t    pub const USE: u32 = 1 << 3;\n12\t    pub const FOCUS: u32 = 1 << 4;\n13\t    pub const SPRINT: u32 = 1 << 5;\n14\t    pub const PRIMARY: u32 = 1 << 6;\n15\t    pub const SECONDARY: u32 = 1 << 7;\n16\t    pub const CRAWL: u32 = 1 << 8;\n17\t    /// Shardfall: skill slots 3-6 (slots 1-2 are PRIMARY / SECONDARY), dodge and potion.\n18\t    pub const SKILL3: u32 = 1 << 9;\n19\t    pub const SKILL4: u32 = 1 << 10;\n20\t    pub const SKILL5: u32 = 1 << 11;\n21\t    pub const SKILL6: u32 = 1 << 12;\n22\t    pub const DODGE: u32 = 1 << 13;\n23\t    pub const POTION: u32 = 1 << 14;\n24\t\n25\t    pub const NAMES: &[(&str, u32)] = &[\n26\t        (\"jump\", JUMP),\n27\t        (\"crouch\", CROUCH),\n28\t        (\"interact\", INTERACT),\n29\t        (\"use\", USE),\n30\t        (\"focus\", FOCUS),\n31\t        (\"sprint\", SPRINT),\n32\t        (\"primary\", PRIMARY),\n33\t        (\"secondary\", SECONDARY),\n34\t        (\"crawl\", CRAWL),\n35\t        (\"skill3\", SKILL3),\n36\t        (\"skill4\", SKILL4),\n37\t        (\"skill5\", SKILL5),\n38\t        (\"skill6\", SKILL6),\n39\t        (\"dodge\", DODGE),\n40\t        (\"potion\", POTION),\n41\t    ];\n42\t\n43\t    pub fn from_name(n: &str) -> Option<u32> {\n44\t        NAMES.iter().find(|(k, _)| k.eq_ignore_ascii_case(n)).map(|(_, v)| *v)\n45\t    }\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n49\tpub struct InputFrame {\n50\t    /// Desired movement on the ground plane: x = world +X, y = world +Z. Length <= 1.\n51\t    pub move_dir: Vec2,\n52\t    /// Vertical intent for climbing/flying/swimming (-1..1).\n53\t    #[serde(default)]\n54\t    pub vertical: f32,\n55\t    /// World-space aim point (mouse on the ground plane, or stick projection).\n56\t    #[serde(default)]\n57\t    pub aim: Option<Vec3>,\n58\t    /// Buttons held this tick.\n59\t    #[serde(default)]\n60\t    pub held: u32,\n61\t    /// Buttons that went down since the previous tick (never lost, even for short taps).\n62\t    #[serde(default)]\n63\t    pub pressed: u32,\n64\t    /// A menu action this tick (Shardfall: equip, sell, travel...), queued one per tick.\n65\t    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]\n66\t    pub cmd: Option<crate::arpg::GameCmd>,\n67\t}\n68\t\n69\timpl InputFrame {\n70\t    pub fn down(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n71\t        self.held & b != 0\n72\t    }\n73\t    pub fn just(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n74\t        self.pressed & b != 0\n75\t    }\n76\t}\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"59109ed4-19d1-441b-bf81-61d502a1845a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:06.472Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/crates/pav_core/src/input.rs","content":"//! Device-independent input for one simulation tick. The host (window, agent, replay) fills\n//! it; the simulation never sees devices. Directions are already in world space.\n\nuse glam::{Vec2, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\npub mod buttons {\n    pub const JUMP: u32 = 1 << 0;\n    pub const CROUCH: u32 = 1 << 1;\n    pub const INTERACT: u32 = 1 << 2;\n    pub const USE: u32 = 1 << 3;\n    pub const FOCUS: u32 = 1 << 4;\n    pub const SPRINT: u32 = 1 << 5;\n    pub const PRIMARY: u32 = 1 << 6;\n    pub const SECONDARY: u32 = 1 << 7;\n    pub const CRAWL: u32 = 1 << 8;\n    /// Shardfall: skill slots 3-6 (slots 1-2 are PRIMARY / SECONDARY), dodge and potion.\n    pub const SKILL3: u32 = 1 << 9;\n    pub const SKILL4: u32 = 1 << 10;\n    pub const SKILL5: u32 = 1 << 11;\n    pub const SKILL6: u32 = 1 << 12;\n    pub const DODGE: u32 = 1 << 13;\n    pub const POTION: u32 = 1 << 14;\n\n    pub const NAMES: &[(&str, u32)] = &[\n        (\"jump\", JUMP),\n        (\"crouch\", CROUCH),\n        (\"interact\", INTERACT),\n        (\"use\", USE),\n        (\"focus\", FOCUS),\n        (\"sprint\", SPRINT),\n        (\"primary\", PRIMARY),\n        (\"secondary\", SECONDARY),\n        (\"crawl\", CRAWL),\n        (\"skill3\", SKILL3),\n        (\"skill4\", SKILL4),\n        (\"skill5\", SKILL5),\n        (\"skill6\", SKILL6),\n        (\"dodge\", DODGE),\n        (\"potion\", POTION),\n    ];\n\n    pub fn from_name(n: &str) -> Option<u32> {\n        NAMES.iter().find(|(k, _)| k.eq_ignore_ascii_case(n)).map(|(_, v)| *v)\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct InputFrame {\n    /// Desired movement on the ground plane: x = world +X, y = world +Z. Length <= 1.\n    pub move_dir: Vec2,\n    /// Vertical intent for climbing/flying/swimming (-1..1).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub vertical: f32,\n    /// World-space aim point (mouse on the ground plane, or stick projection).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub aim: Option<Vec3>,\n    /// Buttons held this tick.\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub held: u32,\n    /// Buttons that went down since the previous tick (never lost, even for short taps).\n    #[serde(default)]\n    pub pressed: u32,\n    /// A menu action this tick (Shardfall: equip, sell, travel...), queued one per tick.\n    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]\n    pub cmd: Option<crate::arpg::GameCmd>,\n}\n\nimpl InputFrame {\n    pub fn down(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n        self.held & b != 0\n    }\n    pub fn just(&self, b: u32) -> bool {\n        self.pressed & b != 0\n    }\n}\n","numLines":77,"startLine":1,"totalLines":77}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DN6SssSanxdtqEQXxccMhL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NtD4NAV1Ke5yS4t7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MUm3Aipii2jMH4qNuWyDbg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/crates/pav_app/src/input.rs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18388,"cache_read_input_tokens":47131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18388},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"a56e5c6e-0f87-4431-8bae-d2742ffb4d9c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:07.260Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96NdaN8NnwMtqFgvkm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MUm3Aipii2jMH4qNuWyDbg":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/crates/pav_app/src/input.rs"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MUm3Aipii2jMH4qNuWyDbg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Devices -> actions. Keyboard keys are bound by physical position (WASD stays WASD on AZERTY).\n2\t//! The fixed system layer (pause, rewind, menus) is handled in `app.rs` and never rebinds.\n3\t\n4\tuse std::collections::HashSet;\n5\t\n6\tuse glam::Vec2;\n7\tuse pav_core::input::buttons;\n8\tuse winit::event::{ElementState, MouseButton};\n9\tuse winit::keyboard::KeyCode;\n10\t\n11\tpub use pav_ui::Device;\n12\t\n13\t/// Game-action bindings (defaults; rooms may override in the future).\n14\tpub struct KeyBinds {\n15\t    pub up: &'static [KeyCode],\n16\t    pub down: &'static [KeyCode],\n17\t    pub left: &'static [KeyCode],\n18\t    pub right: &'static [KeyCode],\n19\t    pub buttons: &'static [(u32, &'static [KeyCode])],\n20\t}\n21\t\n22\tpub const KEYS: KeyBinds = KeyBinds {\n23\t    up: &[KeyCode::KeyW, KeyCode::ArrowUp],\n24\t    down: &[KeyCode::KeyS, KeyCode::ArrowDown],\n25\t    left: &[KeyCode::KeyA, KeyCode::ArrowLeft],\n26\t    right: &[KeyCode::KeyD, KeyCode::ArrowRight],\n27\t    buttons: &[\n28\t        (buttons::JUMP, &[KeyCode::Space]),\n29\t        (buttons::CROUCH, &[KeyCode::KeyC, KeyCode::ControlLeft]),\n30\t        (buttons::CRAWL, &[KeyCode::KeyZ]),\n31\t        (buttons::USE, &[KeyCode::KeyF]),\n32\t        (buttons::FOCUS, &[KeyCode::ShiftLeft]),\n33\t        (buttons::INTERACT, &[KeyCode::KeyE]),\n34\t    ],\n35\t};\n36\t\n37\t/// Physical key names usable in room files (`[keys]`).\n38\tpub fn key_from_name(n: &str) -> Option<KeyCode> {\n39\t    use KeyCode::*;\n40\t    let letters = [\n41\t        KeyA, KeyB, KeyC, KeyD, KeyE, KeyF, KeyG, KeyH, KeyI, KeyJ, KeyK, KeyL, KeyM, KeyN, KeyO, KeyP, KeyQ, KeyR, KeyS, KeyT,\n42\t        KeyU, KeyV, KeyW, KeyX, KeyY, KeyZ,\n43\t    ];\n44\t    if let Some(c) = n.strip_prefix(\"Key\").and_then(|l| l.chars().next()).filter(|c| c.is_ascii_uppercase() && n.len() == 4) {\n45\t        return Some(letters[(c as u8 - b'A') as usize]);\n46\t    }\n47\t    let digits = [Digit0, Digit1, Digit2, Digit3, Digit4, Digit5, Digit6, Digit7, Digit8, Digit9];\n48\t    if let Some(d) = n.strip_prefix(\"Digit\").and_then(|d| d.parse::<usize>().ok()).filter(|d| *d < 10) {\n49\t        return Some(digits[d]);\n50\t    }\n51\t    Some(match n {\n52\t        \"Space\" => Space,\n53\t        \"Enter\" => Enter,\n54\t        \"Tab\" => Tab,\n55\t        \"ShiftLeft\" => ShiftLeft,\n56\t        \"ShiftRight\" => ShiftRight,\n57\t        \"ControlLeft\" => ControlLeft,\n58\t        \"ControlRight\" => ControlRight,\n59\t        \"AltLeft\" => AltLeft,\n60\t        \"AltRight\" => AltRight,\n61\t        \"ArrowUp\" => ArrowUp,\n62\t        \"ArrowDown\" => ArrowDown,\n63\t        \"ArrowLeft\" => ArrowLeft,\n64\t        \"ArrowRight\" => ArrowRight,\n65\t        \"Comma\" => Comma,\n66\t        \"Period\" => Period,\n67\t        \"Slash\" => Slash,\n68\t        \"Semicolon\" => Semicolon,\n69\t        \"Quote\" => Quote,\n70\t        \"BracketLeft\" => BracketLeft,\n71\t        \"BracketRight\" => BracketRight,\n72\t        \"Minus\" => Minus,\n73\t        \"Equal\" => Equal,\n74\t        \"CapsLock\" => CapsLock,\n75\t        _ => return None,\n76\t    })\n77\t}\n78\t\n79\t/// Current keyboard bindings: defaults, optionally overridden by the room.\n80\t#[derive(Clone)]\n81\tpub struct Bindings {\n82\t    pub up: Vec<KeyCode>,\n83\t    pub down: Vec<KeyCode>,\n84\t    pub left: Vec<KeyCode>,\n85\t    pub right: Vec<KeyCode>,\n86\t    pub buttons: Vec<(u32, Vec<KeyCode>)>,\n87\t}\n88\t\n89\timpl Default for Bindings {\n90\t    fn default() -> Self {\n91\t        Self {\n92\t            up: KEYS.up.to_vec(),\n93\t            down: KEYS.down.to_vec(),\n94\t            left: KEYS.left.to_vec(),\n95\t            right: KEYS.right.to_vec(),\n96\t            buttons: KEYS.buttons.iter().map(|(b, k)| (*b, k.to_vec())).collect(),\n97\t        }\n98\t    }\n99\t}\n100\t\n101\timpl Bindings {\n102\t    /// Shardfall: WASD to move, mouse buttons and Q E R F for the six skill slots, Space to\n103\t    /// dodge, 1 for a potion, Shift to attack in place.\n104\t    pub fn game() -> Self {\n105\t        use KeyCode::*;\n106\t        Self {\n107\t            up: vec![KeyW, ArrowUp],\n108\t            down: vec![KeyS, ArrowDown],\n109\t            left: vec![KeyA, ArrowLeft],\n110\t            right: vec![KeyD, ArrowRight],\n111\t            buttons: vec![\n112\t                (buttons::SKILL3, vec![KeyQ]),\n113\t                (buttons::SKILL4, vec![KeyE]),\n114\t                (buttons::SKILL5, vec![KeyR]),\n115\t                (buttons::SKILL6, vec![KeyF]),\n116\t                (buttons::DODGE, vec![Space]),\n117\t                (buttons::POTION, vec![Digit1]),\n118\t                (buttons::FOCUS, vec![ShiftLeft]),\n119\t                (buttons::INTERACT, vec![KeyG]),\n120\t            ],\n121\t        }\n122\t    }\n123\t\n124\t    /// Defaults with a room's `[keys]` overrides applied. Returns problems found.\n125\t    pub fn with_overrides(map: &std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Vec<String>>) -> (Self, Vec<String>) {\n126\t        let mut b = Self::default();\n127\t        let mut errs = Vec::new();\n128\t        for (action, names) in map {\n129\t            let keys: Vec<KeyCode> = names\n130\t                .iter()\n131\t                .filter_map(|n| {\n132\t                    let k = key_from_name(n);\n133\t                    if k.is_none() {\n134\t                        errs.push(format!(\"unknown key '{n}'\"));\n135\t                    }\n136\t                    k\n137\t                })\n138\t                .collect();\n139\t            match action.as_str() {\n140\t                \"move_up\" => b.up = keys,\n141\t                \"move_down\" => b.down = keys,\n142\t                \"move_left\" => b.left = keys,\n143\t                \"move_right\" => b.right = keys,\n144\t                other => match pav_core::input::buttons::from_name(other) {\n145\t                    Some(bit) => match b.buttons.iter_mut().find(|(x, _)| *x == bit) {\n146\t                        Some(e) => e.1 = keys,\n147\t                        None => b.buttons.push((bit, keys)),\n148\t                    },\n149\t                    None => errs.push(format!(\"unknown action '{other}'\")),\n150\t                },\n151\t            }\n152\t        }\n153\t        (b, errs)\n154\t    }\n155\t}\n156\t\n157\t/// Shardfall's controls.\n158\tpub fn game_guide(device: Device) -> &'static [(&'static str, &'static str)] {\n159\t    match device {\n160\t        Device::KeyboardMouse => &[\n161\t            (\"Move\", \"W A S D\"),\n162\t            (\"Aim\", \"mouse\"),\n163\t            (\"Skills\", \"LMB · RMB · Q · E · R · F (hold to repeat)\"),\n164\t            (\"Dodge roll\", \"Space (cancels any attack)\"),\n165\t            (\"Potion\", \"1\"),\n166\t            (\"Attack in place\", \"hold Shift\"),\n167\t            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"G\"),\n168\t            (\"Inventory · passives · character · skills · map\", \"I · P · C · K · M\"),\n169\t            (\"Town portal\", \"T\"),\n170\t            (\"Pick up\", \"walk over it, or click its name\"),\n171\t            (\"Camera\", \"middle-drag rotate · wheel zoom\"),\n172\t            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Backspace\"),\n173\t            (\"Menu / tuning\", \"Esc / F1\"),\n174\t        ],\n175\t        Device::Gamepad => &[\n176\t            (\"Move / aim\", \"left stick / right stick\"),\n177\t            (\"Skills\", \"X · Y · B · RB · LB · RT\"),\n178\t            (\"Dodge roll\", \"A\"),\n179\t            (\"Potion\", \"D-pad up\"),\n180\t            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"D-pad right\"),\n181\t            (\"Map\", \"D-pad left\"),\n182\t            (\"Inventory, character, skills, passives\", \"D-pad down, then LB / RB\"),\n183\t            (\"In menus\", \"stick: cursor · A click · X right-click · hold Y: shift · right stick scroll · B close\"),\n184\t            (\"Menu\", \"Start\"),\n185\t        ],\n186\t        Device::Touch => &[\n187\t            (\"Move\", \"put a thumb down anywhere and drag\"),\n188\t            (\"Attack\", \"automatic: the nearest foe in reach\"),\n189\t            (\"Skills\", \"tap: at the nearest foe · drag: aim, let go to cast · hold: keep casting\"),\n190\t            (\"Dodge roll · potion\", \"the big button · the flask\"),\n191\t            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"the button that appears\"),\n192\t            (\"Bag, hero, skills, passives\", \"the bag (top right)\"),\n193\t            (\"Messages and the minimap\", \"swipe them away\"),\n194\t            (\"Close a window\", \"tap the game outside it\"),\n195\t        ],\n196\t    }\n197\t}\n198\t\n199\t/// Human-readable control guide per device.\n200\tpub fn guide(device: Device) -> &'static [(&'static str, &'static str)] {\n201\t    match device {\n202\t        Device::KeyboardMouse => &[\n203\t            (\"Move\", \"W A S D\"),\n204\t            (\"Jump\", \"Space (hold = higher)\"),\n205\t            (\"Crouch\", \"C / Ctrl (hold)\"),\n206\t            (\"Crawl\", \"Z (toggle)\"),\n207\t            (\"Throw bomb\", \"F or left click (at the cursor)\"),\n208\t            (\"Walk slowly\", \"Shift (instant model)\"),\n209\t            (\"Climb ladder\", \"walk into it\"),\n210\t            (\"Grab a ledge\", \"jump at it, keep pushing toward it\"),\n211\t            (\"Swim\", \"Space up · C down\"),\n212\t            (\"Dodge roll\", \"C while running (committed model)\"),\n213\t            (\"Camera\", \"right-drag rotate · wheel zoom · 1–8 presets\"),\n214\t            (\"Vehicles\", \"E get in / out\"),\n215\t            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Backspace\"),\n216\t            (\"Rooms\", \"F2 any room · F4 leave · F5 reset\"),\n217\t            (\"Menu / tuning\", \"Esc / F1\"),\n218\t        ],\n219\t        Device::Gamepad => &[\n220\t            (\"Move\", \"left stick\"),\n221\t            (\"Aim\", \"right stick\"),\n222\t            (\"Jump\", \"A (hold = higher)\"),\n223\t            (\"Crouch\", \"B (hold)\"),\n224\t            (\"Crawl\", \"Y (toggle)\"),\n225\t            (\"Throw bomb\", \"X or right trigger\"),\n226\t            (\"Walk slowly\", \"left trigger (instant model)\"),\n227\t            (\"Grab a ledge\", \"jump at it, keep pushing toward it\"),\n228\t            (\"Swim\", \"A up · B down\"),\n229\t            (\"Dodge roll\", \"B while running (committed model)\"),\n230\t            (\"Camera\", \"LB / RB rotate · D-pad zoom\"),\n231\t            (\"Vehicles\", \"D-pad right: get in / out\"),\n232\t            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Back/View\"),\n233\t            (\"Menu\", \"Start\"),\n234\t        ],\n235\t        Device::Touch => &[\n236\t            (\"Move\", \"put a thumb down anywhere and drag\"),\n237\t            (\"Jump · bomb · crouch · use\", \"the buttons in the corner\"),\n238\t            (\"Menu\", \"top right\"),\n239\t        ],\n240\t    }\n241\t}\n242\t\n243\t#[derive(Default)]\n244\tpub struct Pad {\n245\t    pub left: Vec2,\n246\t    pub right: Vec2,\n247\t    pub held: u32,\n248\t    pub rotate: f32,\n249\t    pub zoom: f32,\n250\t    pub start_pressed: bool,\n251\t    /// D-pad left (the game's map).\n252\t    pub map_pressed: bool,\n253\t    /// Buttons pressed since the last poll (menus read these as taps).\n254\t    pub taps: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n255\t    /// A (south) and X (west) held: the menu cursor's left and right mouse buttons.\n256\t    pub a_held: bool,\n257\t    pub x_held: bool,\n258\t    /// Y (north) held: Shift for the menu cursor (take a whole path in the passive tree).\n259\t    pub y_held: bool,\n260\t    pub back_held: bool,\n261\t    pub any_activity: bool,\n262\t}\n263\t\n264\tpub struct Input {\n265\t    pub keys: HashSet<KeyCode>,\n266\t    pub mouse: HashSet<MouseButton>,\n267\t    pub cursor: Vec2,\n268\t    pub mouse_active: bool,\n269\t    pub gilrs: Option<gilrs::Gilrs>,\n270\t    /// A scripted gamepad (`--pad-script FILE`): tests and agents drive controller flows.\n271\t    pub pad_script: Option<PadScript>,\n272\t    pub pad: Pad,\n273\t    pub last_device: Device,\n274\t    prev_held: u32,\n275\t    /// Keys / mouse buttons pressed since the last `buttons()` call (short taps are never lost).\n276\t    key_taps: HashSet<KeyCode>,\n277\t    pub mouse_taps: HashSet<MouseButton>,\n278\t    pub bindings: Bindings,\n279\t    /// Game mode: the right mouse button is a skill and the gamepad uses the game layout.\n280\t    pub game: bool,\n281\t}\n282\t\n283\t/// A pad's raw state for one frame.\n284\tstruct PadState {\n285\t    left: Vec2,\n286\t    right: Vec2,\n287\t    held: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n288\t    lt2: bool,\n289\t    rt2: bool,\n290\t}\n291\t\n292\t/// One line of a pad script: hold this for so many frames.\n293\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]\n294\tpub struct PadStep {\n295\t    pub frames: u32,\n296\t    pub left: Vec2,\n297\t    pub right: Vec2,\n298\t    pub hold: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n299\t    pub tap: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n300\t}\n301\t\n302\t/// A scripted gamepad, one step per line:\n303\t/// `<frames> [left=x,y] [right=x,y] [hold=South,North] [tap=DPadDown]` (`#` comments).\n304\t/// Buttons are gilrs names: South (A), East (B), West (X), North (Y), LeftTrigger (LB),\n305\t/// RightTrigger (RB), Start, Select, DPadUp/Down/Left/Right.\n306\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]\n307\tpub struct PadScript {\n308\t    pub steps: Vec<PadStep>,\n309\t    at: usize,\n310\t    frame: u32,\n311\t}\n312\t\n313\timpl PadScript {\n314\t    #[cfg_attr(target_arch = \"wasm32\", allow(dead_code))]\n315\t    pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<PadScript, String> {\n316\t        let button = |n: &str| -> Result<gilrs::Button, String> {\n317\t            use gilrs::Button as B;\n318\t            Ok(match n {\n319\t                \"South\" | \"A\" => B::South,\n320\t                \"East\" | \"B\" => B::East,\n321\t                \"West\" | \"X\" => B::West,\n322\t                \"North\" | \"Y\" => B::North,\n323\t                \"LeftTrigger\" | \"LB\" => B::LeftTrigger,\n324\t                \"RightTrigger\" | \"RB\" => B::RightTrigger,\n325\t                \"Start\" => B::Start,\n326\t                \"Select\" => B::Select,\n327\t                \"DPadUp\" => B::DPadUp,\n328\t                \"DPadDown\" => B::DPadDown,\n329\t                \"DPadLeft\" => B::DPadLeft,\n330\t                \"DPadRight\" => B::DPadRight,\n331\t                o => return Err(format!(\"unknown button '{o}'\")),\n332\t            })\n333\t        };\n334\t        let vec = |v: &str| -> Result<Vec2, String> {\n335\t            let (x, y) = v.split_once(',').ok_or(format!(\"'{v}': want x,y\"))?;\n336\t            Ok(Vec2::new(x.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"'{v}'\"))?, y.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"'{v}'\"))?))\n337\t        };\n338\t        let mut steps = Vec::new();\n339\t        for (n, line) in text.lines().enumerate() {\n340\t            let line = line.split('#').next().unwrap_or(\"\").trim();\n341\t            if line.is_empty() {\n342\t                continue;\n343\t            }\n344\t            let mut words = line.split_whitespace();\n345\t            let frames = words.next().and_then(|w| w.parse().ok()).ok_or(format!(\"line {}: starts with a frame count\", n + 1))?;\n346\t            let mut st = PadStep { frames, ..Default::default() };\n347\t            for w in words {\n348\t                let (k, v) = w.split_once('=').ok_or(format!(\"line {}: '{w}'\", n + 1))?;\n349\t                match k {\n350\t                    \"left\" => st.left = vec(v)?,\n351\t                    \"right\" => st.right = vec(v)?,\n352\t                    \"hold\" => st.hold = v.split(',').map(button).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?,\n353\t                    \"tap\" => st.tap = v.split(',').map(button).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?,\n354\t                    o => return Err(format!(\"line {}: unknown '{o}'\", n + 1)),\n355\t                }\n356\t            }\n357\t            steps.push(st);\n358\t        }\n359\t        Ok(PadScript { steps, at: 0, frame: 0 })\n360\t    }\n361\t\n362\t    /// This frame's step (and whether it's the step's first frame); None when done.\n363\t    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(PadStep, bool)> {\n364\t        let st = self.steps.get(self.at)?.clone();\n365\t        let first = self.frame == 0;\n366\t        self.frame += 1;\n367\t        if self.frame >= st.frames.max(1) {\n368\t            self.at += 1;\n369\t            self.frame = 0;\n370\t        }\n371\t        Some((st, first))\n372\t    }\n373\t}\n374\t\n375\tfn deadzone(v: Vec2, dz: f32) -> Vec2 {\n376\t    let l = v.length();\n377\t    if l < dz { Vec2::ZERO } else { v / l * ((l - dz) / (1.0 - dz)).min(1.0) }\n378\t}\n379\t\n380\timpl Input {\n381\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n382\t        let gilrs = match gilrs::Gilrs::new() {\n383\t            Ok(g) => {\n384\t                for (_, gp) in g.gamepads() {\n385\t                    log::info!(\"gamepad: {} ({:?})\", gp.name(), gp.power_info());\n386\t                }\n387\t                Some(g)\n388\t            }\n389\t            Err(e) => {\n390\t                log::warn!(\"gamepads unavailable: {e}\");\n391\t                None\n392\t            }\n393\t        };\n394\t        Self {\n395\t            keys: HashSet::new(),\n396\t            mouse: HashSet::new(),\n397\t            cursor: Vec2::ZERO,\n398\t            mouse_active: false,\n399\t            gilrs,\n400\t            pad_script: None,\n401\t            pad: Pad::default(),\n402\t            last_device: Device::KeyboardMouse,\n403\t            prev_held: 0,\n404\t            key_taps: HashSet::new(),\n405\t            mouse_taps: HashSet::new(),\n406\t            bindings: Bindings::default(),\n407\t            game: false,\n408\t        }\n409\t    }\n410\t\n411\t    pub fn key(&mut self, code: KeyCode, state: ElementState) {\n412\t        self.last_device = Device::KeyboardMouse;\n413\t        match state {\n414\t            ElementState::Pressed => {\n415\t                self.keys.insert(code);\n416\t                self.key_taps.insert(code);\n417\t            }\n418\t            ElementState::Released => {\n419\t                self.keys.remove(&code);\n420\t            }\n421\t        }\n422\t    }\n423\t\n424\t    pub fn mouse_button(&mut self, b: MouseButton, state: ElementState) {\n425\t        self.last_device = Device::KeyboardMouse;\n426\t        self.mouse_active = true;\n427\t        match state {\n428\t            ElementState::Pressed => {\n429\t                self.mouse.insert(b);\n430\t                self.mouse_taps.insert(b);\n431\t            }\n432\t            ElementState::Released => {\n433\t                self.mouse.remove(&b);\n434\t            }\n435\t        }\n436\t    }\n437\t\n438\t    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\n439\t        self.keys.clear();\n440\t        self.mouse.clear();\n441\t    }\n442\t\n443\t    /// Reads gamepad events and state (first connected pad).\n444\t    pub fn poll_gamepad(&mut self) {\n445\t        use gilrs::{Axis, Button};\n446\t        let mut activity = false;\n447\t        let mut taps = Vec::new();\n448\t        // One pad state from the first connected pad, or from the pad script while it runs.\n449\t        let mut state: Option<PadState> = None;\n450\t        if let Some(g) = &mut self.gilrs {\n451\t            while let Some(ev) = g.next_event() {\n452\t                match ev.event {\n453\t                    gilrs::EventType::ButtonPressed(b, _) => {\n454\t                        taps.push(b);\n455\t                        activity = true;\n456\t                    }\n457\t                    gilrs::EventType::AxisChanged(_, v, _) if v.abs() > 0.4 => activity = true,\n458\t                    gilrs::EventType::Connected => log::info!(\"gamepad connected\"),\n459\t                    gilrs::EventType::Disconnected => log::info!(\"gamepad disconnected\"),\n460\t                    _ => {}\n461\t                }\n462\t            }\n463\t            if let Some((_, gp)) = g.gamepads().next() {\n464\t                const ALL: [Button; 15] = [\n465\t                    Button::South,\n466\t                    Button::East,\n467\t                    Button::North,\n468\t                    Button::West,\n469\t                    Button::LeftTrigger,\n470\t                    Button::RightTrigger,\n471\t                    Button::Select,\n472\t                    Button::Start,\n473\t                    Button::DPadUp,\n474\t                    Button::DPadDown,\n475\t                    Button::DPadLeft,\n476\t                    Button::DPadRight,\n477\t                    Button::LeftThumb,\n478\t                    Button::RightThumb,\n479\t                    Button::Mode,\n480\t                ];\n481\t                let trig = |b: Button| gp.button_data(b).map(|d| d.value()).unwrap_or(0.0) > 0.35;\n482\t                state = Some(PadState {\n483\t                    left: Vec2::new(gp.value(Axis::LeftStickX), gp.value(Axis::LeftStickY)),\n484\t                    right: Vec2::new(gp.value(Axis::RightStickX), gp.value(Axis::RightStickY)),\n485\t                    held: ALL.into_iter().filter(|b| gp.is_pressed(*b)).collect(),\n486\t                    lt2: trig(Button::LeftTrigger2),\n487\t                    rt2: trig(Button::RightTrigger2),\n488\t                });\n489\t            }\n490\t        }\n491\t        if let Some(script) = &mut self.pad_script {\n492\t            if let Some((st, first)) = script.next() {\n493\t                if first {\n494\t                    taps.extend(st.tap.iter().copied());\n495\t                }\n496\t                activity = true;\n497\t                state = Some(PadState { left: st.left, right: st.right, held: st.hold.clone(), lt2: false, rt2: false });\n498\t            }\n499\t        }\n500\t        let start = taps.contains(&Button::Start);\n501\t        let map = taps.contains(&Button::DPadLeft);\n502\t        let mut pad = Pad { start_pressed: start, map_pressed: map, taps, any_activity: activity, ..Default::default() };\n503\t        if let Some(st) = state {\n504\t            let is = |b: Button| st.held.contains(&b);\n505\t            pad.left = deadzone(st.left, 0.18);\n506\t            pad.right = deadzone(st.right, 0.25);\n507\t            let mut held = 0;\n508\t            let layout: &[(Button, u32)] = if self.game {\n509\t                &[\n510\t                    (Button::West, buttons::PRIMARY),\n511\t                    (Button::North, buttons::SECONDARY),\n512\t                    (Button::East, buttons::SKILL3),\n513\t                    (Button::RightTrigger, buttons::SKILL4),\n514\t                    (Button::LeftTrigger, buttons::SKILL5),\n515\t                    (Button::South, buttons::DODGE),\n516\t                    (Button::DPadUp, buttons::POTION),\n517\t                    (Button::DPadRight, buttons::INTERACT),\n518\t                ]\n519\t            } else {\n520\t                &[\n521\t                    (Button::South, buttons::JUMP),\n522\t                    (Button::East, buttons::CROUCH),\n523\t                    (Button::North, buttons::CRAWL),\n524\t                    (Button::West, buttons::USE),\n525\t                    (Button::DPadRight, buttons::INTERACT),\n526\t                ]\n527\t            };\n528\t            for (btn, action) in layout {\n529\t                if is(*btn) {\n530\t                    held |= action;\n531\t                }\n532\t            }\n533\t            if st.rt2 {\n534\t                held |= if self.game { buttons::SKILL6 } else { buttons::USE };\n535\t            }\n536\t            if st.lt2 {\n537\t                held |= buttons::FOCUS;\n538\t            }\n539\t            pad.held = held;\n540\t            pad.rotate = if self.game { 0.0 } else { (is(Button::RightTrigger) as i32 - is(Button::LeftTrigger) as i32) as f32 };\n541\t            // In the game the D-pad is potion / use / map / panels, not zoom.\n542\t            pad.zoom = if self.game { 0.0 } else { (is(Button::DPadDown) as i32 - is(Button::DPadUp) as i32) as f32 };\n543\t            pad.a_held = is(Button::South);\n544\t            pad.x_held = is(Button::West);\n545\t            pad.y_held = is(Button::North);\n546\t            pad.back_held = is(Button::Select);\n547\t            if pad.left.length() > 0.3 || pad.right.length() > 0.3 || held != 0 {\n548\t                pad.any_activity = true;\n549\t            }\n550\t        }\n551\t        if pad.any_activity {\n552\t            self.last_device = Device::Gamepad;\n553\t        }\n554\t        self.pad = pad;\n555\t    }\n556\t\n557\t    /// Screen-space movement (x right, y up) from keys and stick, length <= 1.\n558\t    pub fn move_axis(&self) -> Vec2 {\n559\t        let k = |codes: &[KeyCode]| codes.iter().any(|c| self.keys.contains(c)) as i32 as f32;\n560\t        let b = &self.bindings;\n561\t        let kb = Vec2::new(k(&b.right) - k(&b.left), k(&b.up) - k(&b.down));\n562\t        let v = if kb != Vec2::ZERO { kb.normalize() } else { self.pad.left };\n563\t        v.clamp_length_max(1.0)\n564\t    }\n565\t\n566\t    /// Held game buttons from all devices.\n567\t    pub fn held(&self) -> u32 {\n568\t        let mut b = self.pad.held;\n569\t        for (bit, codes) in &self.bindings.buttons {\n570\t            if codes.iter().any(|c| self.keys.contains(c)) {\n571\t                b |= bit;\n572\t            }\n573\t        }\n574\t        if self.mouse.contains(&MouseButton::Left) {\n575\t            b |= buttons::PRIMARY;\n576\t        }\n577\t        if self.game && self.mouse.contains(&MouseButton::Right) {\n578\t            b |= buttons::SECONDARY;\n579\t        }\n580\t        b\n581\t    }\n582\t\n583\t    /// Returns (held, newly pressed since the last call).\n584\t    pub fn buttons(&mut self) -> (u32, u32) {\n585\t        let held = self.held();\n586\t        let mut pressed = held & !self.prev_held;\n587\t        for (bit, codes) in &self.bindings.buttons {\n588\t            if codes.iter().any(|c| self.key_taps.contains(c)) {\n589\t                pressed |= bit;\n590\t            }\n591\t        }\n592\t        if self.mouse_taps.contains(&MouseButton::Left) {\n593\t            pressed |= buttons::PRIMARY;\n594\t        }\n595\t        if self.game && self.mouse_taps.contains(&MouseButton::Right) {\n596\t            pressed |= buttons::SECONDARY;\n597\t        }\n598\t        self.key_taps.clear();\n599\t        self.mouse_taps.clear();\n600\t        self.prev_held = held;\n601\t        // A tap shorter than a frame still counts as held for that frame.\n602\t        (held | pressed, pressed)\n603\t    }\n604\t\n605\t    /// True if `b` was pressed since the last call (consumes the tap).\n606\t    pub fn take_mouse_tap(&mut self, b: MouseButton) -> bool {\n607\t        self.mouse_taps.remove(&b)\n608\t    }\n609\t\n610\t    pub fn rewind_held(&self) -> bool {\n611\t        self.keys.contains(&KeyCode::Backspace) || self.pad.back_held\n612\t    }\n613\t}\n614\t\n615\t#[cfg(test)]\n616\tmod pad_script_tests {\n617\t    use super::*;\n618\t\n619\t    #[test]\n620\t    fn pad_scripts_parse_and_play() {\n621\t        let mut p = PadScript::parse(\"# open the bag\\n2\\n1 tap=DPadDown\\n3 left=0.5,-1 hold=A,Y\\n\").unwrap();\n622\t        assert_eq!(p.steps.len(), 3);\n623\t        let frames: Vec<(PadStep, bool)> = std::iter::from_fn(|| p.next()).collect();\n624\t        assert_eq!(frames.len(), 6);\n625\t        assert!(frames[2].1 && frames[2].0.tap == vec![gilrs::Button::DPadDown]);\n626\t        assert_eq!(frames[3].0.hold, vec![gilrs::Button::South, gilrs::Button::North]);\n627\t        assert_eq!(frames[5].0.left, Vec2::new(0.5, -1.0));\n628\t        assert!(PadScript::parse(\"x tap=A\").is_err());\n629\t        assert!(PadScript::parse(\"1 tap=Q\").is_err());\n630\t    }\n631\t}\n632\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"7efe51a8-eb30-4a96-a1c2-a20472e0aef0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:07.270Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/crates/pav_app/src/input.rs","content":"//! Devices -> actions. Keyboard keys are bound by physical position (WASD stays WASD on AZERTY).\n//! The fixed system layer (pause, rewind, menus) is handled in `app.rs` and never rebinds.\n\nuse std::collections::HashSet;\n\nuse glam::Vec2;\nuse pav_core::input::buttons;\nuse winit::event::{ElementState, MouseButton};\nuse winit::keyboard::KeyCode;\n\npub use pav_ui::Device;\n\n/// Game-action bindings (defaults; rooms may override in the future).\npub struct KeyBinds {\n    pub up: &'static [KeyCode],\n    pub down: &'static [KeyCode],\n    pub left: &'static [KeyCode],\n    pub right: &'static [KeyCode],\n    pub buttons: &'static [(u32, &'static [KeyCode])],\n}\n\npub const KEYS: KeyBinds = KeyBinds {\n    up: &[KeyCode::KeyW, KeyCode::ArrowUp],\n    down: &[KeyCode::KeyS, KeyCode::ArrowDown],\n    left: &[KeyCode::KeyA, KeyCode::ArrowLeft],\n    right: &[KeyCode::KeyD, KeyCode::ArrowRight],\n    buttons: &[\n        (buttons::JUMP, &[KeyCode::Space]),\n        (buttons::CROUCH, &[KeyCode::KeyC, KeyCode::ControlLeft]),\n        (buttons::CRAWL, &[KeyCode::KeyZ]),\n        (buttons::USE, &[KeyCode::KeyF]),\n        (buttons::FOCUS, &[KeyCode::ShiftLeft]),\n        (buttons::INTERACT, &[KeyCode::KeyE]),\n    ],\n};\n\n/// Physical key names usable in room files (`[keys]`).\npub fn key_from_name(n: &str) -> Option<KeyCode> {\n    use KeyCode::*;\n    let letters = [\n        KeyA, KeyB, KeyC, KeyD, KeyE, KeyF, KeyG, KeyH, KeyI, KeyJ, KeyK, KeyL, KeyM, KeyN, KeyO, KeyP, KeyQ, KeyR, KeyS, KeyT,\n        KeyU, KeyV, KeyW, KeyX, KeyY, KeyZ,\n    ];\n    if let Some(c) = n.strip_prefix(\"Key\").and_then(|l| l.chars().next()).filter(|c| c.is_ascii_uppercase() && n.len() == 4) {\n        return Some(letters[(c as u8 - b'A') as usize]);\n    }\n    let digits = [Digit0, Digit1, Digit2, Digit3, Digit4, Digit5, Digit6, Digit7, Digit8, Digit9];\n    if let Some(d) = n.strip_prefix(\"Digit\").and_then(|d| d.parse::<usize>().ok()).filter(|d| *d < 10) {\n        return Some(digits[d]);\n    }\n    Some(match n {\n        \"Space\" => Space,\n        \"Enter\" => Enter,\n        \"Tab\" => Tab,\n        \"ShiftLeft\" => ShiftLeft,\n        \"ShiftRight\" => ShiftRight,\n        \"ControlLeft\" => ControlLeft,\n        \"ControlRight\" => ControlRight,\n        \"AltLeft\" => AltLeft,\n        \"AltRight\" => AltRight,\n        \"ArrowUp\" => ArrowUp,\n        \"ArrowDown\" => ArrowDown,\n        \"ArrowLeft\" => ArrowLeft,\n        \"ArrowRight\" => ArrowRight,\n        \"Comma\" => Comma,\n        \"Period\" => Period,\n        \"Slash\" => Slash,\n        \"Semicolon\" => Semicolon,\n        \"Quote\" => Quote,\n        \"BracketLeft\" => BracketLeft,\n        \"BracketRight\" => BracketRight,\n        \"Minus\" => Minus,\n        \"Equal\" => Equal,\n        \"CapsLock\" => CapsLock,\n        _ => return None,\n    })\n}\n\n/// Current keyboard bindings: defaults, optionally overridden by the room.\n#[derive(Clone)]\npub struct Bindings {\n    pub up: Vec<KeyCode>,\n    pub down: Vec<KeyCode>,\n    pub left: Vec<KeyCode>,\n    pub right: Vec<KeyCode>,\n    pub buttons: Vec<(u32, Vec<KeyCode>)>,\n}\n\nimpl Default for Bindings {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self {\n            up: KEYS.up.to_vec(),\n            down: KEYS.down.to_vec(),\n            left: KEYS.left.to_vec(),\n            right: KEYS.right.to_vec(),\n            buttons: KEYS.buttons.iter().map(|(b, k)| (*b, k.to_vec())).collect(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Bindings {\n    /// Shardfall: WASD to move, mouse buttons and Q E R F for the six skill slots, Space to\n    /// dodge, 1 for a potion, Shift to attack in place.\n    pub fn game() -> Self {\n        use KeyCode::*;\n        Self {\n            up: vec![KeyW, ArrowUp],\n            down: vec![KeyS, ArrowDown],\n            left: vec![KeyA, ArrowLeft],\n            right: vec![KeyD, ArrowRight],\n            buttons: vec![\n                (buttons::SKILL3, vec![KeyQ]),\n                (buttons::SKILL4, vec![KeyE]),\n                (buttons::SKILL5, vec![KeyR]),\n                (buttons::SKILL6, vec![KeyF]),\n                (buttons::DODGE, vec![Space]),\n                (buttons::POTION, vec![Digit1]),\n                (buttons::FOCUS, vec![ShiftLeft]),\n                (buttons::INTERACT, vec![KeyG]),\n            ],\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Defaults with a room's `[keys]` overrides applied. Returns problems found.\n    pub fn with_overrides(map: &std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Vec<String>>) -> (Self, Vec<String>) {\n        let mut b = Self::default();\n        let mut errs = Vec::new();\n        for (action, names) in map {\n            let keys: Vec<KeyCode> = names\n                .iter()\n                .filter_map(|n| {\n                    let k = key_from_name(n);\n                    if k.is_none() {\n                        errs.push(format!(\"unknown key '{n}'\"));\n                    }\n                    k\n                })\n                .collect();\n            match action.as_str() {\n                \"move_up\" => b.up = keys,\n                \"move_down\" => b.down = keys,\n                \"move_left\" => b.left = keys,\n                \"move_right\" => b.right = keys,\n                other => match pav_core::input::buttons::from_name(other) {\n                    Some(bit) => match b.buttons.iter_mut().find(|(x, _)| *x == bit) {\n                        Some(e) => e.1 = keys,\n                        None => b.buttons.push((bit, keys)),\n                    },\n                    None => errs.push(format!(\"unknown action '{other}'\")),\n                },\n            }\n        }\n        (b, errs)\n    }\n}\n\n/// Shardfall's controls.\npub fn game_guide(device: Device) -> &'static [(&'static str, &'static str)] {\n    match device {\n        Device::KeyboardMouse => &[\n            (\"Move\", \"W A S D\"),\n            (\"Aim\", \"mouse\"),\n            (\"Skills\", \"LMB · RMB · Q · E · R · F (hold to repeat)\"),\n            (\"Dodge roll\", \"Space (cancels any attack)\"),\n            (\"Potion\", \"1\"),\n            (\"Attack in place\", \"hold Shift\"),\n            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"G\"),\n            (\"Inventory · passives · character · skills · map\", \"I · P · C · K · M\"),\n            (\"Town portal\", \"T\"),\n            (\"Pick up\", \"walk over it, or click its name\"),\n            (\"Camera\", \"middle-drag rotate · wheel zoom\"),\n            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Backspace\"),\n            (\"Menu / tuning\", \"Esc / F1\"),\n        ],\n        Device::Gamepad => &[\n            (\"Move / aim\", \"left stick / right stick\"),\n            (\"Skills\", \"X · Y · B · RB · LB · RT\"),\n            (\"Dodge roll\", \"A\"),\n            (\"Potion\", \"D-pad up\"),\n            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"D-pad right\"),\n            (\"Map\", \"D-pad left\"),\n            (\"Inventory, character, skills, passives\", \"D-pad down, then LB / RB\"),\n            (\"In menus\", \"stick: cursor · A click · X right-click · hold Y: shift · right stick scroll · B close\"),\n            (\"Menu\", \"Start\"),\n        ],\n        Device::Touch => &[\n            (\"Move\", \"put a thumb down anywhere and drag\"),\n            (\"Attack\", \"automatic: the nearest foe in reach\"),\n            (\"Skills\", \"tap: at the nearest foe · drag: aim, let go to cast · hold: keep casting\"),\n            (\"Dodge roll · potion\", \"the big button · the flask\"),\n            (\"Use (trade, portal, way down, chests)\", \"the button that appears\"),\n            (\"Bag, hero, skills, passives\", \"the bag (top right)\"),\n            (\"Messages and the minimap\", \"swipe them away\"),\n            (\"Close a window\", \"tap the game outside it\"),\n        ],\n    }\n}\n\n/// Human-readable control guide per device.\npub fn guide(device: Device) -> &'static [(&'static str, &'static str)] {\n    match device {\n        Device::KeyboardMouse => &[\n            (\"Move\", \"W A S D\"),\n            (\"Jump\", \"Space (hold = higher)\"),\n            (\"Crouch\", \"C / Ctrl (hold)\"),\n            (\"Crawl\", \"Z (toggle)\"),\n            (\"Throw bomb\", \"F or left click (at the cursor)\"),\n            (\"Walk slowly\", \"Shift (instant model)\"),\n            (\"Climb ladder\", \"walk into it\"),\n            (\"Grab a ledge\", \"jump at it, keep pushing toward it\"),\n            (\"Swim\", \"Space up · C down\"),\n            (\"Dodge roll\", \"C while running (committed model)\"),\n            (\"Camera\", \"right-drag rotate · wheel zoom · 1–8 presets\"),\n            (\"Vehicles\", \"E get in / out\"),\n            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Backspace\"),\n            (\"Rooms\", \"F2 any room · F4 leave · F5 reset\"),\n            (\"Menu / tuning\", \"Esc / F1\"),\n        ],\n        Device::Gamepad => &[\n            (\"Move\", \"left stick\"),\n            (\"Aim\", \"right stick\"),\n            (\"Jump\", \"A (hold = higher)\"),\n            (\"Crouch\", \"B (hold)\"),\n            (\"Crawl\", \"Y (toggle)\"),\n            (\"Throw bomb\", \"X or right trigger\"),\n            (\"Walk slowly\", \"left trigger (instant model)\"),\n            (\"Grab a ledge\", \"jump at it, keep pushing toward it\"),\n            (\"Swim\", \"A up · B down\"),\n            (\"Dodge roll\", \"B while running (committed model)\"),\n            (\"Camera\", \"LB / RB rotate · D-pad zoom\"),\n            (\"Vehicles\", \"D-pad right: get in / out\"),\n            (\"Rewind\", \"hold Back/View\"),\n            (\"Menu\", \"Start\"),\n        ],\n        Device::Touch => &[\n            (\"Move\", \"put a thumb down anywhere and drag\"),\n            (\"Jump · bomb · crouch · use\", \"the buttons in the corner\"),\n            (\"Menu\", \"top right\"),\n        ],\n    }\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\npub struct Pad {\n    pub left: Vec2,\n    pub right: Vec2,\n    pub held: u32,\n    pub rotate: f32,\n    pub zoom: f32,\n    pub start_pressed: bool,\n    /// D-pad left (the game's map).\n    pub map_pressed: bool,\n    /// Buttons pressed since the last poll (menus read these as taps).\n    pub taps: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n    /// A (south) and X (west) held: the menu cursor's left and right mouse buttons.\n    pub a_held: bool,\n    pub x_held: bool,\n    /// Y (north) held: Shift for the menu cursor (take a whole path in the passive tree).\n    pub y_held: bool,\n    pub back_held: bool,\n    pub any_activity: bool,\n}\n\npub struct Input {\n    pub keys: HashSet<KeyCode>,\n    pub mouse: HashSet<MouseButton>,\n    pub cursor: Vec2,\n    pub mouse_active: bool,\n    pub gilrs: Option<gilrs::Gilrs>,\n    /// A scripted gamepad (`--pad-script FILE`): tests and agents drive controller flows.\n    pub pad_script: Option<PadScript>,\n    pub pad: Pad,\n    pub last_device: Device,\n    prev_held: u32,\n    /// Keys / mouse buttons pressed since the last `buttons()` call (short taps are never lost).\n    key_taps: HashSet<KeyCode>,\n    pub mouse_taps: HashSet<MouseButton>,\n    pub bindings: Bindings,\n    /// Game mode: the right mouse button is a skill and the gamepad uses the game layout.\n    pub game: bool,\n}\n\n/// A pad's raw state for one frame.\nstruct PadState {\n    left: Vec2,\n    right: Vec2,\n    held: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n    lt2: bool,\n    rt2: bool,\n}\n\n/// One line of a pad script: hold this for so many frames.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]\npub struct PadStep {\n    pub frames: u32,\n    pub left: Vec2,\n    pub right: Vec2,\n    pub hold: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n    pub tap: Vec<gilrs::Button>,\n}\n\n/// A scripted gamepad, one step per line:\n/// `<frames> [left=x,y] [right=x,y] [hold=South,North] [tap=DPadDown]` (`#` comments).\n/// Buttons are gilrs names: South (A), East (B), West (X), North (Y), LeftTrigger (LB),\n/// RightTrigger (RB), Start, Select, DPadUp/Down/Left/Right.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]\npub struct PadScript {\n    pub steps: Vec<PadStep>,\n    at: usize,\n    frame: u32,\n}\n\nimpl PadScript {\n    #[cfg_attr(target_arch = \"wasm32\", allow(dead_code))]\n    pub fn parse(text: &str) -> Result<PadScript, String> {\n        let button = |n: &str| -> Result<gilrs::Button, String> {\n            use gilrs::Button as B;\n            Ok(match n {\n                \"South\" | \"A\" => B::South,\n                \"East\" | \"B\" => B::East,\n                \"West\" | \"X\" => B::West,\n                \"North\" | \"Y\" => B::North,\n                \"LeftTrigger\" | \"LB\" => B::LeftTrigger,\n                \"RightTrigger\" | \"RB\" => B::RightTrigger,\n                \"Start\" => B::Start,\n                \"Select\" => B::Select,\n                \"DPadUp\" => B::DPadUp,\n                \"DPadDown\" => B::DPadDown,\n                \"DPadLeft\" => B::DPadLeft,\n                \"DPadRight\" => B::DPadRight,\n                o => return Err(format!(\"unknown button '{o}'\")),\n            })\n        };\n        let vec = |v: &str| -> Result<Vec2, String> {\n            let (x, y) = v.split_once(',').ok_or(format!(\"'{v}': want x,y\"))?;\n            Ok(Vec2::new(x.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"'{v}'\"))?, y.trim().parse().map_err(|_| format!(\"'{v}'\"))?))\n        };\n        let mut steps = Vec::new();\n        for (n, line) in text.lines().enumerate() {\n            let line = line.split('#').next().unwrap_or(\"\").trim();\n            if line.is_empty() {\n                continue;\n            }\n            let mut words = line.split_whitespace();\n            let frames = words.next().and_then(|w| w.parse().ok()).ok_or(format!(\"line {}: starts with a frame count\", n + 1))?;\n            let mut st = PadStep { frames, ..Default::default() };\n            for w in words {\n                let (k, v) = w.split_once('=').ok_or(format!(\"line {}: '{w}'\", n + 1))?;\n                match k {\n                    \"left\" => st.left = vec(v)?,\n                    \"right\" => st.right = vec(v)?,\n                    \"hold\" => st.hold = v.split(',').map(button).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?,\n                    \"tap\" => st.tap = v.split(',').map(button).collect::<Result<_, _>>()?,\n                    o => return Err(format!(\"line {}: unknown '{o}'\", n + 1)),\n                }\n            }\n            steps.push(st);\n        }\n        Ok(PadScript { steps, at: 0, frame: 0 })\n    }\n\n    /// This frame's step (and whether it's the step's first frame); None when done.\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(PadStep, bool)> {\n        let st = self.steps.get(self.at)?.clone();\n        let first = self.frame == 0;\n        self.frame += 1;\n        if self.frame >= st.frames.max(1) {\n            self.at += 1;\n            self.frame = 0;\n        }\n        Some((st, first))\n    }\n}\n\nfn deadzone(v: Vec2, dz: f32) -> Vec2 {\n    let l = v.length();\n    if l < dz { Vec2::ZERO } else { v / l * ((l - dz) / (1.0 - dz)).min(1.0) }\n}\n\nimpl Input {\n    pub fn new() -> Self {\n        let gilrs = match gilrs::Gilrs::new() {\n            Ok(g) => {\n                for (_, gp) in g.gamepads() {\n                    log::info!(\"gamepad: {} ({:?})\", gp.name(), gp.power_info());\n                }\n                Some(g)\n            }\n            Err(e) => {\n                log::warn!(\"gamepads unavailable: {e}\");\n                None\n            }\n        };\n        Self {\n            keys: HashSet::new(),\n            mouse: HashSet::new(),\n            cursor: Vec2::ZERO,\n            mouse_active: false,\n            gilrs,\n            pad_script: None,\n            pad: Pad::default(),\n            last_device: Device::KeyboardMouse,\n            prev_held: 0,\n            key_taps: HashSet::new(),\n            mouse_taps: HashSet::new(),\n            bindings: Bindings::default(),\n            game: false,\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn key(&mut self, code: KeyCode, state: ElementState) {\n        self.last_device = Device::KeyboardMouse;\n        match state {\n            ElementState::Pressed => {\n                self.keys.insert(code);\n                self.key_taps.insert(code);\n            }\n            ElementState::Released => {\n                self.keys.remove(&code);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn mouse_button(&mut self, b: MouseButton, state: ElementState) {\n        self.last_device = Device::KeyboardMouse;\n        self.mouse_active = true;\n        match state {\n            ElementState::Pressed => {\n                self.mouse.insert(b);\n                self.mouse_taps.insert(b);\n            }\n            ElementState::Released => {\n                self.mouse.remove(&b);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    pub fn clear(&mut self) {\n        self.keys.clear();\n        self.mouse.clear();\n    }\n\n    /// Reads gamepad events and state (first connected pad).\n    pub fn poll_gamepad(&mut self) {\n        use gilrs::{Axis, Button};\n        let mut activity = false;\n        let mut taps = Vec::new();\n        // One pad state from the first connected pad, or from the pad script while it runs.\n        let mut state: Option<PadState> = None;\n        if let Some(g) = &mut self.gilrs {\n            while let Some(ev) = g.next_event() {\n                match ev.event {\n                    gilrs::EventType::ButtonPressed(b, _) => {\n                        taps.push(b);\n                        activity = true;\n                    }\n                    gilrs::EventType::AxisChanged(_, v, _) if v.abs() > 0.4 => activity = true,\n                    gilrs::EventType::Connected => log::info!(\"gamepad connected\"),\n                    gilrs::EventType::Disconnected => log::info!(\"gamepad disconnected\"),\n                    _ => {}\n                }\n            }\n            if let Some((_, gp)) = g.gamepads().next() {\n                const ALL: [Button; 15] = [\n                    Button::South,\n                    Button::East,\n                    Button::North,\n                    Button::West,\n                    Button::LeftTrigger,\n                    Button::RightTrigger,\n                    Button::Select,\n                    Button::Start,\n                    Button::DPadUp,\n                    Button::DPadDown,\n                    Button::DPadLeft,\n                    Button::DPadRight,\n                    Button::LeftThumb,\n                    Button::RightThumb,\n                    Button::Mode,\n                ];\n                let trig = |b: Button| gp.button_data(b).map(|d| d.value()).unwrap_or(0.0) > 0.35;\n                state = Some(PadState {\n                    left: Vec2::new(gp.value(Axis::LeftStickX), gp.value(Axis::LeftStickY)),\n                    right: Vec2::new(gp.value(Axis::RightStickX), gp.value(Axis::RightStickY)),\n                    held: ALL.into_iter().filter(|b| gp.is_pressed(*b)).collect(),\n                    lt2: trig(Button::LeftTrigger2),\n                    rt2: trig(Button::RightTrigger2),\n                });\n            }\n        }\n        if let Some(script) = &mut self.pad_script {\n            if let Some((st, first)) = script.next() {\n                if first {\n                    taps.extend(st.tap.iter().copied());\n                }\n                activity = true;\n                state = Some(PadState { left: st.left, right: st.right, held: st.hold.clone(), lt2: false, rt2: false });\n            }\n        }\n        let start = taps.contains(&Button::Start);\n        let map = taps.contains(&Button::DPadLeft);\n        let mut pad = Pad { start_pressed: start, map_pressed: map, taps, any_activity: activity, ..Default::default() };\n        if let Some(st) = state {\n            let is = |b: Button| st.held.contains(&b);\n            pad.left = deadzone(st.left, 0.18);\n            pad.right = deadzone(st.right, 0.25);\n            let mut held = 0;\n            let layout: &[(Button, u32)] = if self.game {\n                &[\n                    (Button::West, buttons::PRIMARY),\n                    (Button::North, buttons::SECONDARY),\n                    (Button::East, buttons::SKILL3),\n                    (Button::RightTrigger, buttons::SKILL4),\n                    (Button::LeftTrigger, buttons::SKILL5),\n                    (Button::South, buttons::DODGE),\n                    (Button::DPadUp, buttons::POTION),\n                    (Button::DPadRight, buttons::INTERACT),\n                ]\n            } else {\n                &[\n                    (Button::South, buttons::JUMP),\n                    (Button::East, buttons::CROUCH),\n                    (Button::North, buttons::CRAWL),\n                    (Button::West, buttons::USE),\n                    (Button::DPadRight, buttons::INTERACT),\n                ]\n            };\n            for (btn, action) in layout {\n                if is(*btn) {\n                    held |= action;\n                }\n            }\n            if st.rt2 {\n                held |= if self.game { buttons::SKILL6 } else { buttons::USE };\n            }\n            if st.lt2 {\n                held |= buttons::FOCUS;\n            }\n            pad.held = held;\n            pad.rotate = if self.game { 0.0 } else { (is(Button::RightTrigger) as i32 - is(Button::LeftTrigger) as i32) as f32 };\n            // In the game the D-pad is potion / use / map / panels, not zoom.\n            pad.zoom = if self.game { 0.0 } else { (is(Button::DPadDown) as i32 - is(Button::DPadUp) as i32) as f32 };\n            pad.a_held = is(Button::South);\n            pad.x_held = is(Button::West);\n            pad.y_held = is(Button::North);\n            pad.back_held = is(Button::Select);\n            if pad.left.length() > 0.3 || pad.right.length() > 0.3 || held != 0 {\n                pad.any_activity = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if pad.any_activity {\n            self.last_device = Device::Gamepad;\n        }\n        self.pad = pad;\n    }\n\n    /// Screen-space movement (x right, y up) from keys and stick, length <= 1.\n    pub fn move_axis(&self) -> Vec2 {\n        let k = |codes: &[KeyCode]| codes.iter().any(|c| self.keys.contains(c)) as i32 as f32;\n        let b = &self.bindings;\n        let kb = Vec2::new(k(&b.right) - k(&b.left), k(&b.up) - k(&b.down));\n        let v = if kb != Vec2::ZERO { kb.normalize() } else { self.pad.left };\n        v.clamp_length_max(1.0)\n    }\n\n    /// Held game buttons from all devices.\n    pub fn held(&self) -> u32 {\n        let mut b = self.pad.held;\n        for (bit, codes) in &self.bindings.buttons {\n            if codes.iter().any(|c| self.keys.contains(c)) {\n                b |= bit;\n            }\n        }\n        if self.mouse.contains(&MouseButton::Left) {\n            b |= buttons::PRIMARY;\n        }\n        if self.game && self.mouse.contains(&MouseButton::Right) {\n            b |= buttons::SECONDARY;\n        }\n        b\n    }\n\n    /// Returns (held, newly pressed since the last call).\n    pub fn buttons(&mut self) -> (u32, u32) {\n        let held = self.held();\n        let mut pressed = held & !self.prev_held;\n        for (bit, codes) in &self.bindings.buttons {\n            if codes.iter().any(|c| self.key_taps.contains(c)) {\n                pressed |= bit;\n            }\n        }\n        if self.mouse_taps.contains(&MouseButton::Left) {\n            pressed |= buttons::PRIMARY;\n        }\n        if self.game && self.mouse_taps.contains(&MouseButton::Right) {\n            pressed |= buttons::SECONDARY;\n        }\n        self.key_taps.clear();\n        self.mouse_taps.clear();\n        self.prev_held = held;\n        // A tap shorter than a frame still counts as held for that frame.\n        (held | pressed, pressed)\n    }\n\n    /// True if `b` was pressed since the last call (consumes the tap).\n    pub fn take_mouse_tap(&mut self, b: MouseButton) -> bool {\n        self.mouse_taps.remove(&b)\n    }\n\n    pub fn rewind_held(&self) -> bool {\n        self.keys.contains(&KeyCode::Backspace) || self.pad.back_held\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod pad_script_tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn pad_scripts_parse_and_play() {\n        let mut p = PadScript::parse(\"# open the bag\\n2\\n1 tap=DPadDown\\n3 left=0.5,-1 hold=A,Y\\n\").unwrap();\n        assert_eq!(p.steps.len(), 3);\n        let frames: Vec<(PadStep, bool)> = std::iter::from_fn(|| p.next()).collect();\n        assert_eq!(frames.len(), 6);\n        assert!(frames[2].1 && frames[2].0.tap == vec![gilrs::Button::DPadDown]);\n        assert_eq!(frames[3].0.hold, vec![gilrs::Button::South, gilrs::Button::North]);\n        assert_eq!(frames[5].0.left, Vec2::new(0.5, -1.0));\n        assert!(PadScript::parse(\"x tap=A\").is_err());\n        assert!(PadScript::parse(\"1 tap=Q\").is_err());\n    }\n}\n","numLines":632,"startLine":1,"totalLines":632}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MUm3Aipii2jMH4qNuWyDbg","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NtD4NAV1Ke5yS4t7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J1CEuqovfuqKF4Qj2wqW4H","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/web/index.html"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18388,"cache_read_input_tokens":47131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18388},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"1cb25ade-96c4-434c-920d-35fab4e395c4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:07.961Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96NdaN8NnwMtqFgvkm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01J1CEuqovfuqKF4Qj2wqW4H":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/web/index.html"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J1CEuqovfuqKF4Qj2wqW4H","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!doctype html>\n2\t<html lang=\"en\">\n3\t<head>\n4\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n5\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\">\n6\t<title>Shardfall</title>\n7\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Play Shardfall, a procedural hack-and-slash adventure with an interactive tech demo pavilion.\">\n8\t<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#15171c\">\n9\t<meta name=\"mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\">\n10\t<meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\">\n11\t<meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style\" content=\"black-translucent\">\n12\t<link rel=\"manifest\" href=\"manifest.webmanifest\">\n13\t<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:,\">\n14\t<style>\n15\t  /* The page is the game: no scrolling, zooming, text selection, callouts or tap flashes. */\n16\t  html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #15171c; overscroll-behavior: none;\n17\t               touch-action: none; -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none; -webkit-touch-callout: none;\n18\t               -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; }\n19\t  /* winit appends the game canvas to <body>; it fills the page and the game follows its size.\n20\t     dvh: the height that is visible while a phone's browser bars come and go. */\n21\t  canvas { display: block; width: 100vw !important; height: 100vh !important; height: 100dvh !important;\n22\t           outline: none; touch-action: none; }\n23\t  #msg { position: fixed; inset: 0; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;\n24\t         font: 18px system-ui, sans-serif; color: #c9ccd4; text-align: center; padding: 24px; pointer-events: none; }\n25\t  /* Phones held upright: a hint to turn sideways that fades by itself. */\n26\t  #turn { display: none; position: fixed; left: 50%; top: 12px; transform: translateX(-50%); padding: 8px 14px;\n27\t          border-radius: 18px; background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6); color: #ffd690; font: 14px system-ui, sans-serif;\n28\t          pointer-events: none; animation: fade 1s 5s forwards; }\n29\t  @media (orientation: portrait) and (pointer: coarse) { #turn { display: block; } }\n30\t  @keyframes fade { to { opacity: 0; } }\n31\t</style>\n32\t</head>\n33\t<body>\n34\t<div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">Loading Shardfall…</div>\n35\t<div id=\"turn\">Turn your phone sideways for the best view</div>\n36\t<script type=\"module\">\n37\t  // Start in a demo room with ?room=NAME, or the pavilion with ?scene=world.\n38\t  // Messages name the game by the page title (a single-file build may retitle it).\n39\t  const name = document.title;\n40\t  const msg = document.getElementById('msg');\n41\t  if (!navigator.gpu) {\n42\t    msg.replaceChildren(`${name} needs WebGPU.`, document.createElement('br'),\n43\t      'Use a recent Chrome, Edge or Safari (iOS 26 or later) with hardware acceleration enabled.');\n44\t  } else {\n45\t    // The game hides this message once its canvas exists, and takes keyboard focus.\n46\t    new MutationObserver((_, obs) => {\n47\t      const c = document.querySelector('canvas');\n48\t      if (c) { c.setAttribute('aria-label', `${name} game`); msg.remove(); c.focus(); obs.disconnect(); }\n49\t    }).observe(document.body, { childList: true });\n50\t    // Phones and tablets: the first tap goes full screen and turns sideways, where the browser\n51\t    // allows it (Android; an iPhone keeps its bars, or plays full screen from the home screen).\n52\t    if (matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches) {\n53\t      addEventListener('pointerup', () => {\n54\t        const el = document.documentElement;\n55\t        if (!document.fullscreenElement && el.requestFullscreen) {\n56\t          el.requestFullscreen({ navigationUI: 'hide' })\n57\t            .then(() => screen.orientation && screen.orientation.lock && screen.orientation.lock('landscape'))\n58\t            .catch(() => {});\n59\t        }\n60\t      }, { once: true, capture: true });\n61\t    }\n62\t    try {\n63\t      // The game is shardfall.js and its .wasm beside this page. A single-file build\n64\t      // (scripts/pack_single_html.py) carries both inside the page and sets window.loadGame.\n65\t      if (window.loadGame) await window.loadGame();\n66\t      else await (await import('./shardfall.js')).default();\n67\t    } catch (error) {\n68\t      console.error(`${name} failed to load:`, error);\n69\t      msg.textContent = `${name} could not load. Please reload the page. If it still fails, check that WebGPU and hardware acceleration are enabled.`;\n70\t      document.body.append(msg);\n71\t    }\n72\t  }\n73\t</script>\n74\t</body>\n75\t</html>\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"7022c7cf-86af-42fb-98bf-47f12a2c0a5b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:07.973Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/web/index.html","content":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\">\n<title>Shardfall</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Play Shardfall, a procedural hack-and-slash adventure with an interactive tech demo pavilion.\">\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#15171c\">\n<meta name=\"mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\">\n<meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-capable\" content=\"yes\">\n<meta name=\"apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style\" content=\"black-translucent\">\n<link rel=\"manifest\" href=\"manifest.webmanifest\">\n<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:,\">\n<style>\n  /* The page is the game: no scrolling, zooming, text selection, callouts or tap flashes. */\n  html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #15171c; overscroll-behavior: none;\n               touch-action: none; -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none; -webkit-touch-callout: none;\n               -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent; }\n  /* winit appends the game canvas to <body>; it fills the page and the game follows its size.\n     dvh: the height that is visible while a phone's browser bars come and go. */\n  canvas { display: block; width: 100vw !important; height: 100vh !important; height: 100dvh !important;\n           outline: none; touch-action: none; }\n  #msg { position: fixed; inset: 0; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;\n         font: 18px system-ui, sans-serif; color: #c9ccd4; text-align: center; padding: 24px; pointer-events: none; }\n  /* Phones held upright: a hint to turn sideways that fades by itself. */\n  #turn { display: none; position: fixed; left: 50%; top: 12px; transform: translateX(-50%); padding: 8px 14px;\n          border-radius: 18px; background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6); color: #ffd690; font: 14px system-ui, sans-serif;\n          pointer-events: none; animation: fade 1s 5s forwards; }\n  @media (orientation: portrait) and (pointer: coarse) { #turn { display: block; } }\n  @keyframes fade { to { opacity: 0; } }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div id=\"msg\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">Loading Shardfall…</div>\n<div id=\"turn\">Turn your phone sideways for the best view</div>\n<script type=\"module\">\n  // Start in a demo room with ?room=NAME, or the pavilion with ?scene=world.\n  // Messages name the game by the page title (a single-file build may retitle it).\n  const name = document.title;\n  const msg = document.getElementById('msg');\n  if (!navigator.gpu) {\n    msg.replaceChildren(`${name} needs WebGPU.`, document.createElement('br'),\n      'Use a recent Chrome, Edge or Safari (iOS 26 or later) with hardware acceleration enabled.');\n  } else {\n    // The game hides this message once its canvas exists, and takes keyboard focus.\n    new MutationObserver((_, obs) => {\n      const c = document.querySelector('canvas');\n      if (c) { c.setAttribute('aria-label', `${name} game`); msg.remove(); c.focus(); obs.disconnect(); }\n    }).observe(document.body, { childList: true });\n    // Phones and tablets: the first tap goes full screen and turns sideways, where the browser\n    // allows it (Android; an iPhone keeps its bars, or plays full screen from the home screen).\n    if (matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches) {\n      addEventListener('pointerup', () => {\n        const el = document.documentElement;\n        if (!document.fullscreenElement && el.requestFullscreen) {\n          el.requestFullscreen({ navigationUI: 'hide' })\n            .then(() => screen.orientation && screen.orientation.lock && screen.orientation.lock('landscape'))\n            .catch(() => {});\n        }\n      }, { once: true, capture: true });\n    }\n    try {\n      // The game is shardfall.js and its .wasm beside this page. A single-file build\n      // (scripts/pack_single_html.py) carries both inside the page and sets window.loadGame.\n      if (window.loadGame) await window.loadGame();\n      else await (await import('./shardfall.js')).default();\n    } catch (error) {\n      console.error(`${name} failed to load:`, error);\n      msg.textContent = `${name} could not load. Please reload the page. If it still fails, check that WebGPU and hardware acceleration are enabled.`;\n      document.body.append(msg);\n    }\n  }\n</script>\n</body>\n</html>\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J1CEuqovfuqKF4Qj2wqW4H","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96NtD4NAV1Ke5yS4t7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011bE3EakJJzQqNEmCpNAFZk","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18388,"cache_read_input_tokens":47131,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18388},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"8e7a2878-207b-4b2b-a30d-62a06452db26","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:08.587Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96NdaN8NnwMtqFgvkm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011bE3EakJJzQqNEmCpNAFZk":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011bE3EakJJzQqNEmCpNAFZk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n2\t\n3\tThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\n4\tthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n5\t\n6\t**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\n7\tnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\n8\tWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n9\t\n10\tFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\n11\tmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n12\t§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\n13\town page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n14\t\n15\t## 1. What the user asked\n16\t| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n17\t|---|---|\n18\t| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n19\t| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n20\t| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n21\t| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n22\t| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n23\t| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n24\t| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n25\t| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n26\t\n27\t## 2. The design in one page\n28\t1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n29\t   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n30\t2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n31\t   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n32\t   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n33\t   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n34\t   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n35\t   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n36\t3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n37\t   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n38\t4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n39\t   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n40\t   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n41\t5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n42\t   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n43\t   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n44\t   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n45\t6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n46\t   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n47\t7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n48\t   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n49\t   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n50\t   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n51\t8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n52\t   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n53\t   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n54\t9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n55\t   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n56\t   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n57\t   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n58\t   leaves (§5.1).\n59\t10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n60\t    what you hit, never the world.\n61\t\n62\t**Rejected:**\n63\t- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n64\t  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n65\t- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n66\t  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n67\t  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n68\t- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n69\t  takes two hops.\n70\t\n71\t## 3. What the code assumes today\n72\t| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n73\t|---|---|---|---|\n74\t| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n75\t| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n76\t| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n77\t| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n78\t| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n79\t| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n80\t| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n81\t| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n82\t| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n83\t| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n84\t| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n85\t| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n86\t| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n87\t| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n88\t| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n89\t| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n90\t| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n91\t| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n92\t\n93\t## 4. Where the code goes\n94\t- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n95\t  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n96\t  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n97\t  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n98\t  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n99\t  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n100\t    from a seed.\n101\t\n102\t  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n103\t  uses it first.\n104\t- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n105\t  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n106\t  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n107\t  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n108\t  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n109\t  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n110\t    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n111\t\n112\t  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n113\t  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n114\t  (§5.2).\n115\t- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n116\t  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n117\t  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n118\t  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n119\t- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n120\t  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.\n121\t- **`pav_view`**: name plates and ping markers, drawn in the 3D scene so captures show them.\n122\t- **`pav_tools`**: one `coop` tool. The MCP tool list is at its 48,000-character cap\n123\t  (`pav_tools/tests/asset_errors.rs` l.101).\n124\t- **Encoding:**\n125\t  - Messages use `postcard` 1.1.3: compact serde, a new dependency.\n126\t  - Snapshots use the existing CBOR (`Sim::save_state`, `ciborium`), deflated with\n127\t    `miniz_oxide` 0.8, already a workspace dependency.\n128\t  - A malformed message is dropped and logged, never a panic.\n129\t\n130\t## 5. How it works\n131\t### 5.1 Session and directory\n132\t- A session is a matchbox full-mesh room at `<signal>/<code>`. The code is six characters of\n133\t  Crockford base32. Every player links to every other: at most six links. Rooms are kept apart\n134\t  by our signaling server (§9), and `Hello` carries the code as a second check.\n135\t- **Handshake:** a joining player sends `Hello{protocol, build, code, name, profile}`. The host\n136\t  answers `Welcome{slot, directory, settings}` or `Reject{reason}`:\n137\t  - The reason is a version mismatch (the `.exe` and the browser build can differ; bump\n138\t    `PROTOCOL` whenever a message changes), a wrong code, or a full session (a fifth player).\n139\t  - Failures show on screen in plain words: host not found, version mismatch, session full.\n140\t- **The host** is the room's creator. It keeps the **directory**:\n141\t  - peers: slot 1-4, colour, name, and a hero profile (character, level, look);\n142\t  - places: `PlaceKey` → owner, standby, members, epoch (the standby is the member who takes\n143\t    over if the owner drops; §5.2);\n144\t  - the session settings: the host's difficulty sliders and the `coop.*` params.\n145\t\n146\t  It broadcasts every change, and every player keeps a copy.\n147\t- **If the host leaves or drops,** copies of the directory may differ (a broadcast can stop\n148\t  partway), so the new host rebuilds it rather than trusting its copy:\n149\t  - The earliest-joined connected player (ties by peer id) becomes host under a new **host\n150\t    term**: the pair (number, host's peer id).\n151\t    - Every control message carries the term, and messages from older terms are dropped.\n152\t    - If a split link ever leaves two hosts, the higher pair wins; the other host steps down\n153\t      and reports.\n154\t  - The new host asks every player for `Report{place, role, place epoch, name, profile,\n155\t    settings version}` and rebuilds the directory from the answers:\n156\t    - if two players claim to own one place, the higher place epoch wins and the other resyncs\n157\t      as a guest;\n158\t    - the settings with the highest version win.\n159\t  - It publishes the rebuilt directory before accepting any change.\n160\t\n161\t  With the embedded signaling server (E1's default), nobody new can join after the host goes,\n162\t  but the session goes on.\n163\t- **`PlaceKey`** is one of `Town`, `Arena`, `Lab` or `Level(depth)`. There is one instance per\n164\t  key per session: whoever goes to depth 7 joins whoever is already there.\n165\t\n166\t### 5.2 Entering, leaving and owning a place\n167\t**Entering.** Travel starts as today: the portal window, `T`, or the exit, through\n168\t`GameCmd::Travel`, gated by the hero's own `max_depth`. Then:\n169\t1. The game sends `Enter(place)` to the host and keeps its loading veil up.\n170\t2. **Empty place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: you, epoch}`. The game builds the place as\n171\t   single player does, and owns it.\n172\t3. **Occupied place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: O}`. The game asks O to `Join`.\n173\t   - O sends `PlaceSnapshot`: its `SimState` minus its hero side and its `Game.net`, deflated,\n174\t     in 16 KB chunks on the bulk channel (about 0.1-0.5 s).\n175\t   - The game **adopts** it: it keeps its own hero side and co-op state, turns O and the other\n176\t     members into remote heroes, and spawns its hero at the place's spawn. It is now a guest.\n177\t4. O adds the newcomer's hero, and every member gets `MemberJoined`.\n178\t\n179\t**Leaving** is travelling away or disconnecting:\n180\t- **A guest leaves:** the owner removes its hero, and members get `MemberLeft`.\n181\t- **Every place with guests has a standby:** its earliest-joined guest, named in the directory;\n182\t  if the standby leaves, the next guest takes the role. The owner keeps the standby promotable:\n183\t  - it sends the standby the place stream without the interest limit (far actors at 2 Hz);\n184\t  - it sends a full snapshot every 5 s, or every 10 s once snapshots pass 100 KB;\n185\t  - this owner-to-standby link has its own budget of 40 KB/s, on top of §5.4's per-player\n186\t    budget.\n187\t- **The owner leaves, others remain:** the standby takes over in three steps, so no change is\n188\t  lost:\n189\t  1. The old owner takes a final snapshot and sends it as `Handoff`. From that tick it stops\n190\t     advancing the place: monsters hold, and claims and uses queue unapplied. So nothing\n191\t     happens after the snapshot that the snapshot lacks.\n192\t  2. The standby adopts it and answers `Ready`.\n193\t  3. The old owner tells the host, which broadcasts `OwnerChanged{epoch+1}` to re-point\n194\t     everyone's streams. The old owner forwards its queued claims and uses to the new owner,\n195\t     then travels behind its loading veil, and forwards any claim that still reaches it.\n196\t\n197\t  Monsters pause for a fraction of a second at a handoff.\n198\t\n199\t  If `Ready` hasn't come within 3 s, the old owner travels anyway and the host treats it as a\n200\t  drop.\n201\t- **The owner drops:** after `owner_timeout` (2 s) with no packets, the host makes the standby\n202\t  the owner. The standby **promotes**: it adopts its latest standby snapshot, applies its fresher\n203\t  stream data on top, and switches the mirrors to full AI. Brains restart, and up to 5 s of\n204\t  far-off changes can be lost: acceptable for a crash.\n205\t- **The last player leaves:** the place is gone. The next visitor builds a new one, as in single\n206\t  player.\n207\t\n208\t**Epochs** drop stale messages from a former owner. If two players enter an empty place\n209\ttogether, the host answers in order and the second waits for the first's snapshot.\n210\t\n211\t**Resync** heals any desync. A guest asks for a fresh snapshot and adopts it, keeping its hero,\n212\twhen any of these happens:\n213\t- a `NetId` named by a stream or an event still has no `Spawned` 2 s after it first appeared.\n214\t  A stream, or a `Cast` on the unordered event channel, can beat its spawn; until then it is\n215\t  buffered, not treated as a desync;\n216\t- a `PlaceState` hash doesn't match;\n217\t- an epoch has a gap.\n218\t\n219\tOne adopt function serves joining, handoff, promotion and resync.\n220\t\n221\t**IDs:** the owner assigns each actor's `NetId`, and each game maps `NetId`s to its own\n222\t`EntityId`s, because games allocate entities independently after they adopt a snapshot. Remote\n223\theroes are `NetId`s by slot.\n224\t\n225\t### 5.3 One tick\n226\t1. The app receives packets and stamps each with the next local tick, giving the `NetInbox`.\n227\t2. `Sim::step_net(&InputFrame, &NetInbox)` runs three steps:\n228\t   - the co-op pre-step applies the inbox: directory, snapshots, events and claims, and\n229\t     streams into the interpolation buffers;\n230\t   - the normal step;\n231\t   - the co-op post-step writes the `NetOutbox`: streams and events.\n232\t3. The app sends the outbox.\n233\t\n234\tReplays record the inbox per tick in a new `net` track in `Replay`. So `record_save` and\n235\t`replay` still reproduce one player's game exactly on the machine that recorded it (doctrine 5;\n236\t`AGENTS.md`'s platform note). Single player calls `step_net` with an empty inbox and behaves\n237\texactly as today.\n238\t\n239\t### 5.4 Streams and events\n240\tFour channels, so a lost or large message never holds up an unrelated one:\n241\t- **0 streams:** unreliable and unordered, each message at most 1,200 bytes.\n242\t- **1 control:** reliable and ordered: the handshake, the directory, owner changes and the\n243\t  handoff's steps.\n244\t- **2 events:** reliable and unordered. Each event carries a per-sender sequence number, and\n245\t  order is restored only where it matters:\n246\t  - a `NetId`'s spawn, death and despawn are kept as tombstones, so a late spawn never revives\n247\t    a dead actor;\n248\t  - each player's claims apply in id order (§5.6).\n249\t- **3 bulk:** reliable and ordered: snapshots and `PlaceState`.\n250\t\n251\t| Message | Ch. | When | From → to | Carries |\n252\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n253\t| Hero stream | 0 | 30 Hz | each player → others in its place | tick, position, velocity, facing, grounded and posture, life and mana fractions, ailment bits, current action and phase |\n254\t| Place stream | 0 | 20 Hz | owner → each guest | owner tick and, per player, the last claim applied (the claim watermark); then per actor in the guest's interest: `NetId`, position, velocity, yaw, life fraction, state bits (casting, stunned, frozen, burning, dead), target |\n255\t| Standby stream, standby snapshot | 0, 3 | far actors 2 Hz; snapshot every 5-10 s | owner → the standby | the place stream without the interest limit; a full snapshot (§5.2; on its own 40 KB/s budget) |\n256\t| `Cast`, `Dodge` | 2 | on event | whoever runs the character → its place | actor, skill, aim, target, start tick, seed |\n257\t| `Spawned`, `Despawned` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, and the new entity and its `Actor` as in the owner's state (CBOR), so summons, waves and brood arrive complete. Every reference inside them to another entity (`Actor.master`, `last_hit`, hit lists, owners, joints) travels as a `NetId` and is remapped on receipt; an unknown one becomes `None` |\n258\t| `Killed` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, owner tick, killer, position, level, rarity, rank, boss |\n259\t| `HitClaim` | 2 | per hit | guest → owner | claim id (rising, per claimant), target, and the attacker's side as its game resolved it: damage by type before the target's mitigation, crit, ailments with strength and duration, knockback, execute, power effects |\n260\t| `UseClaim` | 2 | per use | guest → owner | a world-changing use: a chest, a well, a keg, a Menagerie creature, an arena wave |\n261\t| `HeroHit`, `HeroDied`, `Respawned` | 2 | on event | a player → its place | others' screens: flash, number, fall |\n262\t| `PlaceState` | 3 | on change, ≤ 5 Hz | owner → guests | the shared mechanics state, deflated and sequence-numbered (latest wins) |\n263\t| `PartyStatus` | 2 | 1 Hz | a player → all | life, level, place, dead (for party frames) |\n264\t| `ProfileChanged` | 2 | on change | a player → all | character, level, look |\n265\t| `Emote`, `Ping` | 2 | on use | a player → its place | §8 |\n266\t| `Enter`, `Assign`, `Join`, `Handoff`, `Ready`, `OwnerChanged`, `Report` | 1 (`Handoff` on 3) | on change | the players concerned | the host term and place epoch, plus each step's data (§5.1, §5.2) |\n267\t| `Probe`, `Echo` | 0 | 1 Hz per link | both ways | the link clock |\n268\t\n269\tThe place stream covers actors in the guest's interest: within 45 m of its hero, every boss,\n270\tand anything targeting it. It sends the nearest first and goes round-robin past the size cap.\n271\t\n272\tBudget: at most 40 KB/s per player with four players in one level, not counting the standby's\n273\tlink, which has its own budget (§5.2). Positions start as f32; quantize them only if a\n274\tmeasurement goes over budget.\n275\t\n276\t### 5.5 Who simulates what\n277\t| Thing | Simulated by | Other games |\n278\t|---|---|---|\n279\t| Each hero | its player's game, fully | remote-drive from the hero stream; casts, dodges and emotes from events; its damage ignored |\n280\t| Monsters, bosses, summons, chest keepers, arena waves, Menagerie creatures | the place's owner (AI, life, ailments, deaths) | mirrors: remote-drive from the place stream; casts from events |\n281\t| Projectiles and ground effects | every game that plays the cast | damage only as §5.6 routes it |\n282\t| Shared mechanics: kegs, rift gates, wells, totems, the crumbling floor, the seal and exit, chests and their waves | the owner | `PlaceState` and events (a keg's blast: each game applies it to its own hero) |\n283\t| Static hazards: lava, spike beats, wind, ice, darkness, time bubbles | every game, on its own hero (the owner also on monsters) | not sent |\n284\t| Personal: loot, gold, XP, potions, shrine boons, chest payouts, the passive point for a first boss clear, vendor and buyback, the stash, waypoints (`max_depth`), fog of war (`LevelState.seen`), the kill streak | each player's game only | never sent |\n285\t| Cosmetic: villagers, Biscuit, the Hall of Heroes performers, floaters, gibs, particles, shake | each game locally | never sent |\n286\t\n287\tShrines are personal: each player can use each shrine once. A guest's world-changing use plays\n288\tat once in its own game and goes to the owner as a `UseClaim`.\n289\t\n290\t*Unverified:* how time bubbles and wind act on characters. In C2, check each mechanic in\n291\t`arpg/mechanics.rs` against this table, and record any changes in the stage's `docs/HISTORY.md`\n292\tentry.\n293\t\n294\t### 5.6 Damage routing\n295\tOne check at the top of `Game::hit` returns `Apply`, `Claim` or `Ignore`:\n296\t\n297\t| Target ↓ \\ attacker → | your hero | a monster or hazard | a remote hero |\n298\t|---|---|---|---|\n299\t| your hero | — (no friendly fire) | **Apply**: you decide you were hit | — |\n300\t| a monster or object, in a place you own | **Apply** | **Apply** | **Ignore**: its player claims |\n301\t| a monster or object, in a place where you're a guest | **Claim**: play it now, send `HitClaim` | **Ignore** | **Ignore** |\n302\t| a remote hero | — | **Ignore**: its player decides | — |\n303\t\n304\tThe owner applies a claim through `Game::hit`:\n305\t- **The attacker's side is used as sent:** no re-roll, and no reading of the attacker's powers\n306\t  or build, which the owner doesn't have.\n307\t- **The owner applies its own current defences for the target:** armour, resistances, wards\n308\t  and buffs. The guest predicted with its mirror's defences; the watermark corrects any\n309\t  difference.\n310\t- **Order:** it applies each player's claims in id order, holding any that arrive early. The\n311\t  watermark it advertises is the last claim applied, so it never passes a gap.\n312\t- It credits the claimer.\n313\t\n314\tEffects on the attacker (life and mana on hit, power procs, hit-stop) happen in the attacker's\n315\tgame when the hit lands there. Single player is the \"a place you own\" row.\n316\t\n317\t### 5.7 Lag hiding\n318\t1. **Your own hero has no latency** (§2, point 3).\n319\t2. **Other players' characters show at the owner's time minus an interpolation delay:** 1.5\n320\t   times the stream interval plus the p95 jitter, clamped to 50-200 ms. On loss they\n321\t   extrapolate for up to 150 ms, then hold. Corrections blend over 100 ms; teleports (rift\n322\t   gates, respawn) snap.\n323\t3. **Hits are instant on your screen.** The flash, number and sound, your hit-stop and your\n324\t   life on hit all happen at once. The monster's bar shows predicted life: the owner's latest\n325\t   figure minus your claims above the watermark that came with it, so no claim is subtracted\n326\t   twice. A predicted kill plays the death and drops your loot at once, since loot is personal.\n327\t   If the watermark hasn't passed the killing claim within 1 s, the mirror comes back (rare).\n328\t4. **You dodge what you see.** Monster attacks resolve on your machine against your hero, with\n329\t   the full windup. It is never shortened, which is fair to the victim.\n330\t5. **Interactions** (rift gate, well, keg, chest) play at once in your game; the owner confirms.\n331\t6. **The link clock** sends a probe on each link every second and keeps RTT, jitter (EWMA) and\n332\t   each place's owner-tick estimate.\n333\t7. **Heroes pass through each other**; monsters still collide with heroes.\n334\t8. **The owner is whoever entered first.** If phones or slow browsers owning a place make\n335\t   guests' monsters stutter, hand ownership to a native player in the place. Build this only if\n336\t   it shows up.\n337\t\n338\t## 6. Scaling\n339\t- **n** is the number of players in the place (E2's default). Setting `coop.scale_by = \"session\"`\n340\t  counts everyone in the session instead.\n341\t- **Life** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.life_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.75 gives ×1,\n342\t  ×1.75, ×2.5 and ×3.25.\n343\t  - The owner applies it in `spawn_actor` (`arpg/mod.rs` l.598).\n344\t  - When n changes, every game rescales the actors it holds in the same way, keeping each\n345\t    life fraction (`Actor::recompute`, `arpg/combat.rs` l.295): the owner its monsters, guests\n346\t    their mirrors. So predictions, execute thresholds and damage numbers use the owner's\n347\t    maximum.\n348\t- **Damage** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.damage_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.25 gives ×1,\n349\t  ×1.25, ×1.5 and ×1.75.\n350\t  - It is applied where monster damage is rolled in the victim's game, using that game's n,\n351\t    next to `enemy_damage`.\n352\t  - The places are `roll_damage` (`arpg/skills.rs` l.331), `death_burst` (`arpg/boss.rs`\n353\t    l.350) and powers (`arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348).\n354\t- **Rewards** are per player, as single player gives them. On `Killed`, every player within\n355\t  `coop.share_radius` (40 m) of the kill, or who hit the monster, gets the full XP and rolls\n356\t  their own drops. Distance is judged at the kill's owner tick: each game keeps its hero's\n357\t  positions for the last 2 s, and judges an older kill from its oldest kept position.\n358\t- Ward Totems and kegs keep their own life, as today.\n359\t- The session host's difficulty sliders apply in every game.\n360\t- All four params are a `Tunable` (`coop.*`), so `params`, `set`, presets and the tuning panel\n361\t  get them.\n362\t- **Check (C2):** compare four bots in a place against one bot alone. If kill time or deaths\n363\t  differ by more than 25%, adjust the defaults once and record it. Feel is the user's call.\n364\t\n365\t## 7. Online changes to single-player features\n366\t- **Pause, speed, single step and rewind** (the Esc menu, F6, Backspace, the pad's Select): off in\n367\t  a session. The Esc menu opens over the running game.\n368\t- **Hit-stop:** `Game::time_scale` returns 1 in a session. Your hero freezes for the hit-stop's\n369\t  length, and the struck monster's pose holds on your screen. The boss entrance's 0.35 s freeze\n370\t  becomes a camera beat.\n371\t- **Studios, the F10 editor and the engine demo scenes** (world, rooms, starter): leave the\n372\t  session first. Co-op covers Shardfall's places only.\n373\t- **Character switching** in the Hall of Heroes: allowed in town; it sends `ProfileChanged`.\n374\t- **Death:** as single player: 3 s, then respawn at the place's spawn with full life. Other\n375\t  players see you fall and reappear.\n376\t- **Saves:** each game saves its own hero, as now. Rewind history isn't recorded online.\n377\t\n378\t## 8. Pings, emotes and the co-op UI\n379\t**Pings.** The keys below are free in the game today (`pav_app/src/input.rs` l.111-129,\n380\t`app.rs` l.835-944).\n381\t- **World pings:** `V` (or mouse Back) pings the ground under the cursor, and the context picks\n382\t  the kind:\n383\t  - a monster → **Danger**;\n384\t  - an item, feature or spot → **Look**;\n385\t  - your own hero → **Help**;\n386\t  - anything else → **Go**.\n387\t\n388\t  Hold `V` for a wheel of the four. On a pad, R3 pings at the aim point (hold it for the wheel).\n389\t  On touch, a ping button sits in the top-right row (`touch.rs` l.410-442).\n390\t- **Map pings:** with the big map open (`M`), click while holding `V` (or `Alt`) to ping that\n391\t  spot.\n392\t- **What the others see** (players in the same place only):\n393\t  - a ring and beam in the pinger's colour, with the kind's icon, for 4 s, drawn by `pav_view`\n394\t    so captures show them;\n395\t  - an arrow at the screen edge with the distance, when the ping is off screen;\n396\t  - a blip on the minimap and the big map;\n397\t  - a short synth cue per kind (`pav_audio`).\n398\t- **Limit:** at most 3 pings per 3 s per player; extras are dropped.\n399\t\n400\t**Emotes.**\n401\t- **Eight**, defined as data in `game/emotes.toml` (key, name, clip, loop, icon): wave, cheer,\n402\t  bow, dance, laugh, point, sit and taunt.\n403\t  - Clips come from the store (`assets shelf=clip`, `assets find=<word> kind=clip`), keeping\n404\t    each source's credit and licence: open-licence tiers only, never `anim/local`.\n405\t  - A missing clip is made through the Animation Studio's loop.\n406\t- **Use:** hold `B` for the emote wheel (pad: L3; touch: an emote button). The emote plays on\n407\t  your hero at once and stops when you move, attack or dodge. Other players' games play it on\n408\t  your hero too.\n409\t- *Unverified:* that every clip plays on all three heroes' puppets. Check with `animsheet` in\n410\t  C4.\n411\t\n412\t**UI** (egui in `pav_app`; the `coop` tool reports the same state as text, because captures\n413\tdon't show egui):\n414\t- **Pause menu → Co-op:**\n415\t  - **Host:** the code, a copy button, and \"players n/4\".\n416\t  - **Join:** a code, or `CODE@ip:port` for a host's embedded server.\n417\t  - **Leave.**\n418\t- **Flags and settings:**\n419\t  - flags: `--host`, `--join CODE[@addr]` and `--name NAME`;\n420\t  - `shardfall.toml`: `name`, `signal = \"wss://...\"`, `signal_port = 3536`, `turn`,\n421\t    `turn_user` and `turn_pass`;\n422\t  - the browser: `?join=CODE`.\n423\t\n424\t  The game still boots into town; hosting turns the running game into a session.\n425\t- **Party frames**, up to three: slot colour, name, character, level, a life bar, the place\n426\t  (\"Emberwatch\", \"Depth 7, The Molten Floor\"), a dead mark, and a link-quality dot.\n427\t- **Name plates** over remote heroes (`pav_view`): name and level in the slot colour, with a thin\n428\t  life bar.\n429\t- **Map:** remote heroes as arrows in their colours, and pings.\n430\t- **Toasts** for joins, leaves and lost connections. The text is fixed; there is no chat.\n431\t- **Slot colours:** 1 amber `#f2a33a`, 2 cyan `#3ac8f2`, 3 lime `#8fe04a`, 4 magenta `#e04ad0`.\n432\t\n433\t## 9. Transport and connection\n434\t- **The libraries:** **matchbox_socket 0.12.0** (2025-05-21) and **matchbox_signaling 0.12.0**.\n435\t  - They are the newest versions at least 12 months old on 2026-10-16 (doctrine 9; 0.14 is\n436\t    current). Licence: MIT or Apache-2.0.\n437\t  - The same API runs on native and wasm: full-mesh WebRTC data channels with\n438\t    `ChannelConfig::unreliable()`, `reliable()` and custom configs (reliable and unordered is\n439\t    `ordered: false` with unlimited retransmits), ICE servers (`RtcIceServerConfig`, Google\n440\t    STUN by default), and a pluggable `SignallerBuilder`.\n441\t  - Native-to-browser play is a stated matchbox feature, *unverified* here until C3.\n442\t- **Rooms:** the signaling library's built-in full mesh (`full_mesh_builder`) puts every\n443\t  connected peer in one mesh. The standalone `matchbox_server` keeps rooms apart by URL path,\n444\t  using its own topology on the signaling library; its newest qualifying release is 0.11.0, and\n445\t  it has no 0.12. `pav_net` reuses that pattern on `matchbox_signaling` 0.12, so the host's\n446\t  embedded server and the public `shardfall-signal` binary run the same code.\n447\t- **Native:**\n448\t  - It uses webrtc-rs 0.13, with `ring` 0.17 and `rustls` and no OpenSSL; matchbox's lockfile\n449\t    lists about 300 crates.\n450\t  - One `net` thread runs a tokio runtime. On it run matchbox's message loop (which reaches\n451\t    tokio through `async-compat`) and, on the host, the embedded signaling server (axum on\n452\t    tokio).\n453\t  - The sim thread drains and fills the socket's channels each tick.\n454\t  - Check the Windows cross-build (`scripts/build-windows.sh`) on the first day of C3.\n455\t- **wasm** uses the browser's own WebRTC. The message loop runs under\n456\t  `wasm_bindgen_futures::spawn_local`, and the socket is polled in `pump()`\n457\t  (`pav_app/src/app.rs` l.990). No threads and no blocking calls.\n458\t- **Signaling (E1):**\n459\t  - By default, the host's native game runs `pav_net`'s signaling server on port 3536.\n460\t    Joiners use `CODE@host:3536` on a LAN, through a port forward, or over a VPN such as\n461\t    Tailscale.\n462\t  - A browser page served over https can only reach a `wss://` server.\n463\t    Internet play from the browser waits on E1.\n464\t  - Recommended: `shardfall-signal` in a small container with TLS, its URL in\n465\t    `shardfall.toml` and the web build.\n466\t- **NAT:** STUN only by default. TURN comes from settings if a real session fails to connect;\n467\t  most home networks connect without it.\n468\t- **Sizes:** unreliable messages are at most 1,200 bytes; snapshots go in 16 KB chunks on the\n469\t  bulk channel.\n470\t\n471\t## 10. Tools, tests and targets\n472\t- **`SimNet`:** N players in one process, each with a full `Sim`, over links with seeded latency,\n473\t  jitter, loss and bandwidth. One tick steps every game and the network. Runs are deterministic,\n474\t  so tests and tools repeat exactly.\n475\t- **The `coop` tool** (one tool, help at most 50 tokens):\n476\t  - `coop players=4 latency=80 jitter=15 loss=0.02` starts an in-process session; player 1 is\n477\t    the current session.\n478\t  - `coop peer=2` points the other tools (`input`, `player`, `capture`, `see`, `game_cmd`,\n479\t    `level`) at player 2's game; `step` advances every game.\n480\t  - `coop` with no arguments reports, for each player: place, role, life, RTT, KB/s,\n481\t    interpolation delay, mirror error, unconfirmed claims and resyncs.\n482\t  - `coop bots=true seconds=60` lets bots drive every player, and reports kills, deaths, XP and\n483\t    clear times with the network numbers.\n484\t  - Through `pav live` it reports the real session.\n485\t- **Targets.** These are objective stand-ins for \"feels lag-free\", measured at 100 ms RTT, ±20 ms\n486\t  jitter and 2% loss, with four players in one level:\n487\t\n488\t  | Measure | Target |\n489\t  |---|---|\n490\t  | Own-hero input latency | 0 ticks (true by construction; asserted) |\n491\t  | Mirror error: a guest's mirror vs the owner's actor at the same owner tick | median ≤ 0.15 m, p99 ≤ 0.5 m |\n492\t  | Pops: corrections over 0.5 m in one frame | ≤ 1 per minute per player |\n493\t  | Bandwidth | ≤ 40 KB/s per player |\n494\t  | Hit confirmation (the watermark passing a claim) | median ≤ RTT + 2 ticks, p99 ≤ 2 × RTT |\n495\t  | A guest's tick cost | ≤ the owner's |\n496\t- **Suites** in `crates/pav_core/tests/coop.rs` (new; cargo, no GPU):\n497\t  - the handshake: version mismatch, a full session;\n498\t  - entering, joining, leaving, handoff, owner drop and host drop;\n499\t  - a graceful handoff loses no change; an owner drop with players far apart promotes the\n500\t    standby without stale actors; a host drop in mid-broadcast converges on one directory;\n501\t  - a place stream that beats its `Spawned` is buffered, not resynced;\n502\t  - the claim watermark: predicted life never subtracts a claim twice, and claims that arrive\n503\t    out of order never move the watermark past a gap;\n504\t  - a handoff in mid-fight loses no claim;\n505\t  - a spawned brood's `master` points at the right actor in every game;\n506\t  - travelling between two occupied places leaves no stale `NetId`;\n507\t  - a guest's kill confirmed by the owner, with the guest's own loot;\n508\t  - victim-side hits: a dodge on the guest's screen counts;\n509\t  - scaling, and the live rescale;\n510\t  - ping and emote delivery;\n511\t  - one player's replay reproducing its hash;\n512\t  - every existing suite, unchanged.\n513\t- **`pav_net` tests:** two native peers and the embedded signaling server on 127.0.0.1 exchange\n514\t  messages on all four channels, and two rooms on one server stay apart.\n515\t- **`scripts/coop-local.sh`** (new), all on localhost, with screenshots from each window into\n516\t  `out/coop/`:\n517\t  - a native host under Xvfb and lavapipe, with `--bridge 127.0.0.1:7878`;\n518\t  - a native joiner on `:7879`;\n519\t  - a browser tab in headless Chromium with `?join=` (the flags are in `docs/REFERENCE.md`,\n520\t    \"Browser and phones\").\n521\t- **Windows:** the `.exe` hosting under Wine (`scripts/smoke-windows.sh`) with a Linux joiner.\n522\t- **Cost:** these are tool checks with no model calls, so they may run as often as needed\n523\t  (`AGENTS.md`, \"Testing budget\"). No agent judges; looks and feel go to the user.\n524\t\n525\t## 11. Stages\n526\tEach stage is one PR, merged when its checks pass (`AGENTS.md`, \"Per PR\"), with a dated\n527\t`docs/HISTORY.md` entry and `docs/PROGRESS.md` brought up to date. Single player must behave\n528\texactly as before after every stage.\n529\t\n530\t**C0: several heroes in one place (no network).**\n531\t- **Remote-drive mode** for characters (`Character.remote`): a kinematic target, with the\n532\t  animation fed from velocity, posture and grounded. The `riding` early-out (`character.rs`\n533\t  l.494) is the template, but it skips animation, which this mode keeps.\n534\t- **Remote heroes** as `Team::Hero` actors with their own looks. Generalize `refresh_hero`\n535\t  (`arpg/mod.rs` l.441) to any hero profile.\n536\t- **AI for several heroes:** monsters target the nearest living hero, sticky with hysteresis;\n537\t  `hero_flow` is kept per targeted hero; aggro checks every hero. `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` and the\n538\t  other player-only engine checks take any hero.\n539\t- **Split `Game`:**\n540\t  - The hero side is `hero`, `hero_id` and the hero's `Actor`, `loot`, `gold`, `vendor`,\n541\t    `buyback`, `respawn`, `travel`, `floaters`, `message`, `hitstop`, `shake`, `streak`,\n542\t    `echoes`, `power_clock`, `level_flash`, the loot settings, the bags cache and `roster`.\n543\t  - The rest is the place side.\n544\t  - Add `adopt_place(snapshot, hero_side)`, and have travel use it too.\n545\t- **The damage-routing check** (§5.6) with the single-player role, and the `coop.*` params with\n546\t  the scaling functions.\n547\t- **Exit:**\n548\t  - every suite passes unchanged;\n549\t  - `campaign from=1 to=3` matches `main`;\n550\t  - a new test adds a second, remote-driven hero, and monsters go for the nearer one.\n551\t\n552\t**C1: a session over the simulated network.**\n553\t- `pav_core::net`: `SimNet`, `InterpBuffer` and the link clock.\n554\t- `arpg::coop`:\n555\t  - the handshake (with the code), and the directory with host terms and reports;\n556\t  - entering, joining and leaving, and snapshot adoption (keeping the recipient's co-op\n557\t    state);\n558\t  - the standby and its stream, the three-step handoff, promotion, and resync (buffering\n559\t    early streams);\n560\t  - the hero and place streams, and spawn and despawn with complete actors.\n561\t- `Sim::step_net`, the replay's `net` track, and the `coop` tool.\n562\t- **Exit:** the session suites in `coop.rs` pass, and a four-player `coop` run meets §10's\n563\t  mirror-error, pop and bandwidth targets.\n564\t\n565\t**C2: combat and rewards online.**\n566\t- Hit claims with ids, resolved results and the watermark; prediction; victim-side monster\n567\t  hits; `Killed` with its tick, and personal rewards.\n568\t- `PlaceState` and the mechanics, checking §5.5's table mechanic by mechanic.\n569\t- Scaling with the live rescale in every game, and the online rules (§7).\n570\t- **Exit:**\n571\t  - `coop bots=true` with four players clears levels 1-3 and kills the Hollow King on level 4;\n572\t  - the scaling check (§6) is done;\n573\t  - each class of mechanic has a suite.\n574\t\n575\t**C3: the real network.**\n576\t- `pav_net`: matchbox on native and wasm, and the signaling server with rooms (embedded, and the\n577\t  `shardfall-signal` binary).\n578\t- App wiring: the net thread, the wasm pump, flags and settings, the Co-op window, and handshake\n579\t  errors on screen.\n580\t- `scripts/coop-local.sh`.\n581\t- **Exit:**\n582\t  - two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC, with screenshots in\n583\t    the PR;\n584\t  - the Windows build compiles and hosts under Wine with a Linux joiner.\n585\t\n586\t**C4: presence.**\n587\t- Party frames, name plates, the map, pings (world and map), emotes (data, wheel, clips), pad and\n588\t  touch bindings, and sound cues.\n589\t- **Exit:** captures show name plates and pings, smoke screenshots show the egui parts, and\n590\t  suites cover delivery.\n591\t\n592\t**C5: hardening and docs.**\n593\t- Hardening:\n594\t  - rejoining within 60 s keeps your slot;\n595\t  - host and owner drops are handled under load;\n596\t  - bandwidth caps;\n597\t  - net lines in `shardfall.log`: connect, RTT, owner changes, resyncs.\n598\t- Docs:\n599\t  - `docs/COOP.md` (new) for players: hosting, joining, a port forward or Tailscale;\n600\t  - `docs/REFERENCE.md`: the tool and the tests;\n601\t  - a pointer in `AGENTS.md`, which must stay under 32 KiB;\n602\t  - a section in `docs/GAME.md`;\n603\t  - `docs/PROGRESS.md`.\n604\t- **Exit:** a 30-minute soak with four bots, one owner drop and one host drop: no stuck place,\n605\t  no panic, and fewer than one resync per minute.\n606\t\n607\t**C6: internet play (after E1).** The public signaling server and optional TURN. The web build\n608\tpoints at the server, the user plays a real session, and their notes become follow-up work.\n609\t\n610\t## 12. Rulings\n611\tThese are asked in chat with this plan (2026-10-16; `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`). Each has a default,\n612\twhich the run applies if no answer has come 15 minutes after it reaches the question.\n613\t\n614\t| # | Question | Default |\n615\t|---|---|---|\n616\t| E1 | Where does a public signaling server run? The account, and any small cost, are the user's. | None yet. Hosts run the embedded server, internet play needs a port forward or a VPN, and browser players wait for the answer (C6). |\n617\t| E2 | Scale by the players in the place, or by everyone in the session (the request's literal \"in the game\")? | In the place, so a player fighting alone doesn't face four players' monsters. |\n618\t| E3 | Personal loot, or one shared drop to fight over? | Personal: each player gets single player's drops. |\n619\t\n620\t## 13. Risks\n621\t- **webrtc-rs on Windows (mingw):** `ring` builds C and assembly. Verify this first in C3; if it\n622\t  won't build, escalate rather than drop the browser.\n623\t- **Strict NATs:** some pairs of players won't connect without TURN (C6).\n624\t- **Snapshot size grows with content:** measured at 41-175 KB; budget 300 KB, and leave caches\n625\t  out with `#[serde(skip)]`.\n626\t- **Big snapshots on the browser's main thread:** decoding about 1.5 MB of CBOR may hitch a\n627\t  frame. Measure in C3, and spread the work over frames if needed.\n628\t- **Promotion after a drop is approximate:** brains restart, and up to 5 s of far-off changes\n629\t  can be lost. That's acceptable for a crash; graceful leaves hand over exactly.\n630\t- **The MCP tool list is full:** keep to one `coop` tool with short help, and trim elsewhere if\n631\t  `asset_errors.rs` fails.\n632\t- **The bot's known stall** on level 4's seal (`docs/PROGRESS.md`, \"Open issues\") may block C2's\n633\t  exit; fix it there if it does.\n634\t\n635\t## 14. Running it\n636\t**On hold:** use this section only once the user takes co-op off hold (see the status at the\n637\ttop).\n638\t\n639\tThe expected size is about 8,000-12,000 lines over six PRs (C0-C5); C6 waits on E1.\n640\t1. **Before you start:** answer E1-E3 (§12) if you can, so the run doesn't stop on them.\n641\t2. **Start fresh, don't compact.** Open a new session, or `/clear` this one, then give the\n642\t   `/goal` below. This file carries everything the run needs, and a `/compact` summary of\n643\t   earlier work would add tokens that every later call re-reads. A new cloud session builds\n644\t   `pav` in its start hook.\n645\t3. **Effort: extra (xhigh).** The design decisions are made here; what remains is a long,\n646\t   careful engineering run with tool-checked exits. That suits xhigh. Max would mostly add cost,\n647\t   and medium or high would risk cutting corners on ownership and resync edge cases.\n648\t4. **The goal line:**\n649\t\n650\t```\n651\t/goal Build co-op as docs/COOP_PLAN.md specifies: stages C0-C5 in order, one PR per stage merged when its checks pass, single player unchanged. Done when §10's targets hold in a four-player bot session and two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC.\n652\t```\n653\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"2719e288-a926-431c-b862-612356f43be4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:08.778Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall/docs/COOP_PLAN.md","content":"# Co-op: up to four players, one world\n\nThe plan for online co-op in Shardfall: up to four players, each free to go anywhere, playing\nthe same game as single player, with monsters made tougher instead of more numerous.\n\n**Status: on hold** (the user's call, 2026-10-17). The plan is kept for later, not scrapped;\nnothing is built. Don't start any stage, or run §14's `/goal`, until the user takes it off hold.\nWhen it resumes, re-check §3's facts against `main` first, since the code will have moved.\n\nFacts come from a survey of `main` at `dd5584b`; line numbers are as of then, and *unverified*\nmarks what was not checked. The agent who builds it reads this whole file, then works through\n§11's stages in order. Paths without a crate are under `crates/pav_core/src/`. Players get their\nown page, `docs/COOP.md`, in C5.\n\n## 1. What the user asked\n| The ask | Where this plan meets it |\n|---|---|\n| **Up to four players**, online co-op, \"essentially the same single player experience\" | §2 (1-5), §5.1 (the cap), §5.5, §7 |\n| **No extra monsters:** monster **life and damage scale** with the number of players | §6 |\n| **Lag-free feel:** whatever it takes on every side (host, client, server) | §2 (2-4, 8-9), §5.7, §9, with measured targets in §10 |\n| **No anti-cheat:** every client is trusted | §2 (4), §5.6: claims are never re-checked |\n| **Free roaming:** anyone goes anywhere they can reach on their own, never tied to another player, never waiting (\"a super mini-MMO\") | §2 (1-2), §5.2: one shared instance per place, with travel gated only by the hero's own progress |\n| **No voice or text chat.** Emotes with animations, pings and map pings are in | §8 |\n| A short `/goal` to build it, the effort level, and whether to `/compact` or `/goal` first | §14 |\n| Implied by `AGENTS.md`: the Windows `.exe`, Linux and the browser build play together | §9, §11 (C3) |\n\n## 2. The design in one page\n1. **One game, one place, as today.** Each player's game simulates only the place its hero is\n   in (town, a level, the arena, the lab). One `Sim` holds one place, and travel rebuilds it.\n2. **Place owners.** The first player into a place builds it as single player does and **owns**\n   it: their game runs its monsters, bosses and shared mechanics. Players who arrive later are\n   **guests** whose games mirror the owner's. One guest is the place's **standby**. When the\n   owner leaves, the standby takes over from an exact snapshot, confirmed before the owner goes;\n   if the owner drops, the standby promotes from a snapshot at most 5 s old. A player alone in a\n   place is playing single player, with zero lag.\n3. **Your hero is yours.** Every game fully simulates its own hero: movement, skills, life,\n   mana, potions, items, death. Input never waits on the network.\n4. **The attacker decides it hit; the victim decides it was hit.** Your hits resolve on your\n   screen against what you see and go to the owner as claims. A monster's attack resolves on\n   its target's screen against where that hero really is. Nothing is re-checked (§5.6).\n5. **Personal rewards, shared world.** Loot, gold, XP, boons, chest payouts, vendors, the stash\n   and waypoints are per player and never cross the network: on a kill, each player nearby\n   rolls their own drops, as in single player. Monsters, bosses, seals and the level's\n   mechanics are shared (§5.5).\n6. **Scaling:** life ×(1 + 0.75(n−1)) and damage ×(1 + 0.25(n−1)), where n is the number of\n   players in the place (ruling E2; §6).\n7. **Joining a place means receiving the owner's exact state.** The owner sends its `SimState`\n   snapshot, the CBOR that `Sim::save_state` already writes, minus its own hero. Measured\n   deflated with gzip: 41 KB for town, 81 KB for level 3, 109 KB for level 9 and 175 KB for\n   level 14. Machines therefore never have to compute identical results.\n8. **One replication pattern.** Every character simulated on another machine is a **movement\n   stream** (interpolated) plus **action events** (casts, dodges, emotes, deaths), played\n   through the engine's own animation and skills.\n9. **Transport:** WebRTC data channels through matchbox, on native and in the browser: a full\n   mesh with an unreliable channel for streams and reliable channels for control, events and\n   bulk transfers (§5.4). Tests and tools use an in-process simulated network. The session host\n   keeps only the directory of who owns which place, and hands it on under a new term if it\n   leaves (§5.1).\n10. **Online rules:** no pause, rewind or speed control. Hit-stop freezes only your hero and\n    what you hit, never the world.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- *One host simulating every place* (host-authoritative). Every guest would feel latency on\n  every monster, even when alone, and the host's game would run up to five places at once.\n- *Lockstep or rollback* (for example GGRS). Both need bit-identical simulation on Windows,\n  Linux and wasm, which isn't true here: rapier's `enhanced-determinism` is off and the code\n  uses `std` trig. Every player would also simulate every place.\n- *A WebSocket relay.* On TCP, one lost packet stalls everything behind it, and every message\n  takes two hops.\n\n## 3. What the code assumes today\n| Area | Today | Where | Co-op change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The player | `SimState.player`; one `InputFrame` per tick, given only to the player | `sim.rs` `spawn_player` l.412, `step_inner` l.521 (input l.553) | Stays the local hero. Remote heroes use a new remote-drive mode (C0). |\n| Shardfall's hero | `Game.hero` (the profile) and `hero_id`; live life and mana are in `Actor` | `arpg/mod.rs` l.123-126, `resume_game` l.324 | `Game` splits into a hero side and a place side (C0). |\n| Monster AI | `Game::pre` takes one hero: wake at 12 m, pack at 9 m, casts at the hero's feet; one flow field cached per hero cell | `arpg/mod.rs` `pre` l.629-729, `arpg/brain.rs` l.11-12 and `think` l.56, `arpg/mechanics.rs` `hero_flow` l.248 | Target the nearest living hero (sticky); one flow field per targeted hero. |\n| Engine checks | one `player_feet` for every engine brain; `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` tiles react only to the player | `sim.rs` l.545, `ai.rs`, `destruct.rs` l.78 | Any hero (the Crumbling Halls need it). |\n| Time | hit-stop slows the whole sim to 0.06 | `Game::time_scale`, `arpg/mod.rs` l.271; `sim.rs` l.524 | Per entity online (§7). |\n| Pause, rewind, speed | the app's `TimeControl`; the Esc menu pauses; Backspace rewinds | `pav_app/src/simhost.rs` l.20-38; `app.rs` l.764, l.1000 | Off online. |\n| Travel | rebuilds the whole `SimState` within one tick, carrying the `Game`; `resume_game` wipes actors, loot and the level; the layout seed comes from the sim's RNG, so a level is new on every visit | `arpg/mod.rs` `game_travel` l.404; `arpg/world.rs` l.513 | Kept. Co-op decides whether to build fresh (owner) or adopt the owner's snapshot (guest). |\n| Damage | `Game::hit` for everything; `targets()` filters by `Team::hostile`, so there is no friendly fire | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1077; `arpg/skills.rs` `fire_cast` l.455, `targets` l.416 | One routing check at the top of `Game::hit` (§5.6). |\n| Rewards | `kill()` gives XP to the one `g.hero`; `drop_loot` uses that hero's find stats | `arpg/mod.rs` l.1309; `arpg/loot.rs` l.50 | Each game runs its own reward on a `Killed` event. |\n| Scaling hooks | `spawn_actor` sets life (l.598) and damage (l.611); `enemy_damage` is also applied in `roll_damage`, the boss's `death_burst` and powers | `arpg/mod.rs` l.578; `arpg/skills.rs` l.331; `arpg/boss.rs` l.350; `arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348 | Life scales on the owner, damage on the victim (§6). |\n| Where you can go | `GameCmd::Travel` is gated by the hero's own `max_depth`; `use_exit` refuses while the level is sealed | `arpg/cmd.rs` l.296; `arpg/mechanics.rs` l.911 | Unchanged: \"anywhere they can reach on their own\". |\n| HUD and map | the map takes the hero to be the first `Team::Hero` actor | `pav_app/src/arpg_ui.rs` `level_hud` l.562 (l.641) | Use `hero_id`; draw remote heroes and pings. |\n| View | `RenderFrame.player` and `focus` are single; every entity carries its own puppet look | `sim.rs` `frame` l.941; `pav_view/src/build.rs` l.1104 | Unchanged (the local hero); four heroes already render. |\n| Saves | one hero and roster per machine or browser | `pav_app/src/save.rs` | Unchanged: no save crosses the network. |\n| Bots and tools | `autoplay` and `campaign` drive `g.hero` in one `Session` | `arpg/bot.rs`; `pav_tools/src/agent_tools.rs` l.227 | Per player, through the `coop` tool (§10). |\n| App | no title screen (boots into `town`); the pause menu is the only menu | `pav_app/src/settings.rs` l.172; `ui.rs` `MenuAction` l.25, `pause_menu` l.167 | The co-op entries go in the pause menu. |\n| Networking | none; no async runtime in use (tokio is only in `Cargo.lock`) | every `Cargo.toml` | A new crate, `pav_net` (§4). |\n| Several sims | already work in one process (travel builds a second `Sim`); content caches are process-global | `arpg/mod.rs` l.409 | Enough for the in-process test network. |\n\n## 4. Where the code goes\n- **`pav_core::net`** (new; engine API):\n  - `PeerId`, `NetId` and `Channel` (unreliable or reliable).\n  - A tick's `NetInbox` and `NetOutbox`.\n  - `InterpBuffer`: interpolates, extrapolates up to a limit, then holds.\n  - A link clock: RTT, jitter and the remote-tick estimate.\n  - `SimNet`: an in-process network whose links have latency, jitter, loss and bandwidth drawn\n    from a seed.\n\n  It is pure: no IO, no threads, safe on wasm. It is written for any game on the hook; Shardfall\n  uses it first.\n- **`pav_core::arpg::coop`** (new; the game): messages, the directory, owner and guest logic,\n  the hero and place streams, damage routing, scaling, pings and emotes. Its state is\n  `Game.net: Option<Box<CoopState>>`, inside `SimState`, so a player's own snapshots and\n  replays carry it. It has two parts:\n  - the **session part** (directory, peers, host term, link clocks) lasts the whole session;\n  - the **place part** (role, place epoch, `NetId` map, interpolation buffers, pending events\n    and claims, watermarks) belongs to one place.\n\n  Place snapshots sent to other players carry neither part. Adopting a snapshot keeps the\n  recipient's session part and builds a fresh place part from the snapshot; so does any travel\n  (§5.2).\n- **`pav_net`** (new crate; internals, doctrine 8): the WebRTC transport (matchbox, on native and\n  wasm) and the signaling server with rooms (native only; §9), both embedded in the host's game\n  and as a small `shardfall-signal` binary. It handles bytes only and knows nothing of the game,\n  so `pav_core` stays free of IO.\n- **`pav_app`**: flags and settings, the net pump, the Co-op window, party frames, ping and emote\n  input and wheels, and pad and touch bindings.\n- **`pav_view`**: name plates and ping markers, drawn in the 3D scene so captures show them.\n- **`pav_tools`**: one `coop` tool. The MCP tool list is at its 48,000-character cap\n  (`pav_tools/tests/asset_errors.rs` l.101).\n- **Encoding:**\n  - Messages use `postcard` 1.1.3: compact serde, a new dependency.\n  - Snapshots use the existing CBOR (`Sim::save_state`, `ciborium`), deflated with\n    `miniz_oxide` 0.8, already a workspace dependency.\n  - A malformed message is dropped and logged, never a panic.\n\n## 5. How it works\n### 5.1 Session and directory\n- A session is a matchbox full-mesh room at `<signal>/<code>`. The code is six characters of\n  Crockford base32. Every player links to every other: at most six links. Rooms are kept apart\n  by our signaling server (§9), and `Hello` carries the code as a second check.\n- **Handshake:** a joining player sends `Hello{protocol, build, code, name, profile}`. The host\n  answers `Welcome{slot, directory, settings}` or `Reject{reason}`:\n  - The reason is a version mismatch (the `.exe` and the browser build can differ; bump\n    `PROTOCOL` whenever a message changes), a wrong code, or a full session (a fifth player).\n  - Failures show on screen in plain words: host not found, version mismatch, session full.\n- **The host** is the room's creator. It keeps the **directory**:\n  - peers: slot 1-4, colour, name, and a hero profile (character, level, look);\n  - places: `PlaceKey` → owner, standby, members, epoch (the standby is the member who takes\n    over if the owner drops; §5.2);\n  - the session settings: the host's difficulty sliders and the `coop.*` params.\n\n  It broadcasts every change, and every player keeps a copy.\n- **If the host leaves or drops,** copies of the directory may differ (a broadcast can stop\n  partway), so the new host rebuilds it rather than trusting its copy:\n  - The earliest-joined connected player (ties by peer id) becomes host under a new **host\n    term**: the pair (number, host's peer id).\n    - Every control message carries the term, and messages from older terms are dropped.\n    - If a split link ever leaves two hosts, the higher pair wins; the other host steps down\n      and reports.\n  - The new host asks every player for `Report{place, role, place epoch, name, profile,\n    settings version}` and rebuilds the directory from the answers:\n    - if two players claim to own one place, the higher place epoch wins and the other resyncs\n      as a guest;\n    - the settings with the highest version win.\n  - It publishes the rebuilt directory before accepting any change.\n\n  With the embedded signaling server (E1's default), nobody new can join after the host goes,\n  but the session goes on.\n- **`PlaceKey`** is one of `Town`, `Arena`, `Lab` or `Level(depth)`. There is one instance per\n  key per session: whoever goes to depth 7 joins whoever is already there.\n\n### 5.2 Entering, leaving and owning a place\n**Entering.** Travel starts as today: the portal window, `T`, or the exit, through\n`GameCmd::Travel`, gated by the hero's own `max_depth`. Then:\n1. The game sends `Enter(place)` to the host and keeps its loading veil up.\n2. **Empty place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: you, epoch}`. The game builds the place as\n   single player does, and owns it.\n3. **Occupied place:** the host answers `Assign{owner: O}`. The game asks O to `Join`.\n   - O sends `PlaceSnapshot`: its `SimState` minus its hero side and its `Game.net`, deflated,\n     in 16 KB chunks on the bulk channel (about 0.1-0.5 s).\n   - The game **adopts** it: it keeps its own hero side and co-op state, turns O and the other\n     members into remote heroes, and spawns its hero at the place's spawn. It is now a guest.\n4. O adds the newcomer's hero, and every member gets `MemberJoined`.\n\n**Leaving** is travelling away or disconnecting:\n- **A guest leaves:** the owner removes its hero, and members get `MemberLeft`.\n- **Every place with guests has a standby:** its earliest-joined guest, named in the directory;\n  if the standby leaves, the next guest takes the role. The owner keeps the standby promotable:\n  - it sends the standby the place stream without the interest limit (far actors at 2 Hz);\n  - it sends a full snapshot every 5 s, or every 10 s once snapshots pass 100 KB;\n  - this owner-to-standby link has its own budget of 40 KB/s, on top of §5.4's per-player\n    budget.\n- **The owner leaves, others remain:** the standby takes over in three steps, so no change is\n  lost:\n  1. The old owner takes a final snapshot and sends it as `Handoff`. From that tick it stops\n     advancing the place: monsters hold, and claims and uses queue unapplied. So nothing\n     happens after the snapshot that the snapshot lacks.\n  2. The standby adopts it and answers `Ready`.\n  3. The old owner tells the host, which broadcasts `OwnerChanged{epoch+1}` to re-point\n     everyone's streams. The old owner forwards its queued claims and uses to the new owner,\n     then travels behind its loading veil, and forwards any claim that still reaches it.\n\n  Monsters pause for a fraction of a second at a handoff.\n\n  If `Ready` hasn't come within 3 s, the old owner travels anyway and the host treats it as a\n  drop.\n- **The owner drops:** after `owner_timeout` (2 s) with no packets, the host makes the standby\n  the owner. The standby **promotes**: it adopts its latest standby snapshot, applies its fresher\n  stream data on top, and switches the mirrors to full AI. Brains restart, and up to 5 s of\n  far-off changes can be lost: acceptable for a crash.\n- **The last player leaves:** the place is gone. The next visitor builds a new one, as in single\n  player.\n\n**Epochs** drop stale messages from a former owner. If two players enter an empty place\ntogether, the host answers in order and the second waits for the first's snapshot.\n\n**Resync** heals any desync. A guest asks for a fresh snapshot and adopts it, keeping its hero,\nwhen any of these happens:\n- a `NetId` named by a stream or an event still has no `Spawned` 2 s after it first appeared.\n  A stream, or a `Cast` on the unordered event channel, can beat its spawn; until then it is\n  buffered, not treated as a desync;\n- a `PlaceState` hash doesn't match;\n- an epoch has a gap.\n\nOne adopt function serves joining, handoff, promotion and resync.\n\n**IDs:** the owner assigns each actor's `NetId`, and each game maps `NetId`s to its own\n`EntityId`s, because games allocate entities independently after they adopt a snapshot. Remote\nheroes are `NetId`s by slot.\n\n### 5.3 One tick\n1. The app receives packets and stamps each with the next local tick, giving the `NetInbox`.\n2. `Sim::step_net(&InputFrame, &NetInbox)` runs three steps:\n   - the co-op pre-step applies the inbox: directory, snapshots, events and claims, and\n     streams into the interpolation buffers;\n   - the normal step;\n   - the co-op post-step writes the `NetOutbox`: streams and events.\n3. The app sends the outbox.\n\nReplays record the inbox per tick in a new `net` track in `Replay`. So `record_save` and\n`replay` still reproduce one player's game exactly on the machine that recorded it (doctrine 5;\n`AGENTS.md`'s platform note). Single player calls `step_net` with an empty inbox and behaves\nexactly as today.\n\n### 5.4 Streams and events\nFour channels, so a lost or large message never holds up an unrelated one:\n- **0 streams:** unreliable and unordered, each message at most 1,200 bytes.\n- **1 control:** reliable and ordered: the handshake, the directory, owner changes and the\n  handoff's steps.\n- **2 events:** reliable and unordered. Each event carries a per-sender sequence number, and\n  order is restored only where it matters:\n  - a `NetId`'s spawn, death and despawn are kept as tombstones, so a late spawn never revives\n    a dead actor;\n  - each player's claims apply in id order (§5.6).\n- **3 bulk:** reliable and ordered: snapshots and `PlaceState`.\n\n| Message | Ch. | When | From → to | Carries |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hero stream | 0 | 30 Hz | each player → others in its place | tick, position, velocity, facing, grounded and posture, life and mana fractions, ailment bits, current action and phase |\n| Place stream | 0 | 20 Hz | owner → each guest | owner tick and, per player, the last claim applied (the claim watermark); then per actor in the guest's interest: `NetId`, position, velocity, yaw, life fraction, state bits (casting, stunned, frozen, burning, dead), target |\n| Standby stream, standby snapshot | 0, 3 | far actors 2 Hz; snapshot every 5-10 s | owner → the standby | the place stream without the interest limit; a full snapshot (§5.2; on its own 40 KB/s budget) |\n| `Cast`, `Dodge` | 2 | on event | whoever runs the character → its place | actor, skill, aim, target, start tick, seed |\n| `Spawned`, `Despawned` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, and the new entity and its `Actor` as in the owner's state (CBOR), so summons, waves and brood arrive complete. Every reference inside them to another entity (`Actor.master`, `last_hit`, hit lists, owners, joints) travels as a `NetId` and is remapped on receipt; an unknown one becomes `None` |\n| `Killed` | 2 | on event | owner → guests | `NetId`, owner tick, killer, position, level, rarity, rank, boss |\n| `HitClaim` | 2 | per hit | guest → owner | claim id (rising, per claimant), target, and the attacker's side as its game resolved it: damage by type before the target's mitigation, crit, ailments with strength and duration, knockback, execute, power effects |\n| `UseClaim` | 2 | per use | guest → owner | a world-changing use: a chest, a well, a keg, a Menagerie creature, an arena wave |\n| `HeroHit`, `HeroDied`, `Respawned` | 2 | on event | a player → its place | others' screens: flash, number, fall |\n| `PlaceState` | 3 | on change, ≤ 5 Hz | owner → guests | the shared mechanics state, deflated and sequence-numbered (latest wins) |\n| `PartyStatus` | 2 | 1 Hz | a player → all | life, level, place, dead (for party frames) |\n| `ProfileChanged` | 2 | on change | a player → all | character, level, look |\n| `Emote`, `Ping` | 2 | on use | a player → its place | §8 |\n| `Enter`, `Assign`, `Join`, `Handoff`, `Ready`, `OwnerChanged`, `Report` | 1 (`Handoff` on 3) | on change | the players concerned | the host term and place epoch, plus each step's data (§5.1, §5.2) |\n| `Probe`, `Echo` | 0 | 1 Hz per link | both ways | the link clock |\n\nThe place stream covers actors in the guest's interest: within 45 m of its hero, every boss,\nand anything targeting it. It sends the nearest first and goes round-robin past the size cap.\n\nBudget: at most 40 KB/s per player with four players in one level, not counting the standby's\nlink, which has its own budget (§5.2). Positions start as f32; quantize them only if a\nmeasurement goes over budget.\n\n### 5.5 Who simulates what\n| Thing | Simulated by | Other games |\n|---|---|---|\n| Each hero | its player's game, fully | remote-drive from the hero stream; casts, dodges and emotes from events; its damage ignored |\n| Monsters, bosses, summons, chest keepers, arena waves, Menagerie creatures | the place's owner (AI, life, ailments, deaths) | mirrors: remote-drive from the place stream; casts from events |\n| Projectiles and ground effects | every game that plays the cast | damage only as §5.6 routes it |\n| Shared mechanics: kegs, rift gates, wells, totems, the crumbling floor, the seal and exit, chests and their waves | the owner | `PlaceState` and events (a keg's blast: each game applies it to its own hero) |\n| Static hazards: lava, spike beats, wind, ice, darkness, time bubbles | every game, on its own hero (the owner also on monsters) | not sent |\n| Personal: loot, gold, XP, potions, shrine boons, chest payouts, the passive point for a first boss clear, vendor and buyback, the stash, waypoints (`max_depth`), fog of war (`LevelState.seen`), the kill streak | each player's game only | never sent |\n| Cosmetic: villagers, Biscuit, the Hall of Heroes performers, floaters, gibs, particles, shake | each game locally | never sent |\n\nShrines are personal: each player can use each shrine once. A guest's world-changing use plays\nat once in its own game and goes to the owner as a `UseClaim`.\n\n*Unverified:* how time bubbles and wind act on characters. In C2, check each mechanic in\n`arpg/mechanics.rs` against this table, and record any changes in the stage's `docs/HISTORY.md`\nentry.\n\n### 5.6 Damage routing\nOne check at the top of `Game::hit` returns `Apply`, `Claim` or `Ignore`:\n\n| Target ↓ \\ attacker → | your hero | a monster or hazard | a remote hero |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| your hero | — (no friendly fire) | **Apply**: you decide you were hit | — |\n| a monster or object, in a place you own | **Apply** | **Apply** | **Ignore**: its player claims |\n| a monster or object, in a place where you're a guest | **Claim**: play it now, send `HitClaim` | **Ignore** | **Ignore** |\n| a remote hero | — | **Ignore**: its player decides | — |\n\nThe owner applies a claim through `Game::hit`:\n- **The attacker's side is used as sent:** no re-roll, and no reading of the attacker's powers\n  or build, which the owner doesn't have.\n- **The owner applies its own current defences for the target:** armour, resistances, wards\n  and buffs. The guest predicted with its mirror's defences; the watermark corrects any\n  difference.\n- **Order:** it applies each player's claims in id order, holding any that arrive early. The\n  watermark it advertises is the last claim applied, so it never passes a gap.\n- It credits the claimer.\n\nEffects on the attacker (life and mana on hit, power procs, hit-stop) happen in the attacker's\ngame when the hit lands there. Single player is the \"a place you own\" row.\n\n### 5.7 Lag hiding\n1. **Your own hero has no latency** (§2, point 3).\n2. **Other players' characters show at the owner's time minus an interpolation delay:** 1.5\n   times the stream interval plus the p95 jitter, clamped to 50-200 ms. On loss they\n   extrapolate for up to 150 ms, then hold. Corrections blend over 100 ms; teleports (rift\n   gates, respawn) snap.\n3. **Hits are instant on your screen.** The flash, number and sound, your hit-stop and your\n   life on hit all happen at once. The monster's bar shows predicted life: the owner's latest\n   figure minus your claims above the watermark that came with it, so no claim is subtracted\n   twice. A predicted kill plays the death and drops your loot at once, since loot is personal.\n   If the watermark hasn't passed the killing claim within 1 s, the mirror comes back (rare).\n4. **You dodge what you see.** Monster attacks resolve on your machine against your hero, with\n   the full windup. It is never shortened, which is fair to the victim.\n5. **Interactions** (rift gate, well, keg, chest) play at once in your game; the owner confirms.\n6. **The link clock** sends a probe on each link every second and keeps RTT, jitter (EWMA) and\n   each place's owner-tick estimate.\n7. **Heroes pass through each other**; monsters still collide with heroes.\n8. **The owner is whoever entered first.** If phones or slow browsers owning a place make\n   guests' monsters stutter, hand ownership to a native player in the place. Build this only if\n   it shows up.\n\n## 6. Scaling\n- **n** is the number of players in the place (E2's default). Setting `coop.scale_by = \"session\"`\n  counts everyone in the session instead.\n- **Life** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.life_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.75 gives ×1,\n  ×1.75, ×2.5 and ×3.25.\n  - The owner applies it in `spawn_actor` (`arpg/mod.rs` l.598).\n  - When n changes, every game rescales the actors it holds in the same way, keeping each\n    life fraction (`Actor::recompute`, `arpg/combat.rs` l.295): the owner its monsters, guests\n    their mirrors. So predictions, execute thresholds and damage numbers use the owner's\n    maximum.\n- **Damage** is multiplied by 1 + `coop.damage_per_player` × (n−1). The default 0.25 gives ×1,\n  ×1.25, ×1.5 and ×1.75.\n  - It is applied where monster damage is rolled in the victim's game, using that game's n,\n    next to `enemy_damage`.\n  - The places are `roll_damage` (`arpg/skills.rs` l.331), `death_burst` (`arpg/boss.rs`\n    l.350) and powers (`arpg/powers.rs` l.251, l.348).\n- **Rewards** are per player, as single player gives them. On `Killed`, every player within\n  `coop.share_radius` (40 m) of the kill, or who hit the monster, gets the full XP and rolls\n  their own drops. Distance is judged at the kill's owner tick: each game keeps its hero's\n  positions for the last 2 s, and judges an older kill from its oldest kept position.\n- Ward Totems and kegs keep their own life, as today.\n- The session host's difficulty sliders apply in every game.\n- All four params are a `Tunable` (`coop.*`), so `params`, `set`, presets and the tuning panel\n  get them.\n- **Check (C2):** compare four bots in a place against one bot alone. If kill time or deaths\n  differ by more than 25%, adjust the defaults once and record it. Feel is the user's call.\n\n## 7. Online changes to single-player features\n- **Pause, speed, single step and rewind** (the Esc menu, F6, Backspace, the pad's Select): off in\n  a session. The Esc menu opens over the running game.\n- **Hit-stop:** `Game::time_scale` returns 1 in a session. Your hero freezes for the hit-stop's\n  length, and the struck monster's pose holds on your screen. The boss entrance's 0.35 s freeze\n  becomes a camera beat.\n- **Studios, the F10 editor and the engine demo scenes** (world, rooms, starter): leave the\n  session first. Co-op covers Shardfall's places only.\n- **Character switching** in the Hall of Heroes: allowed in town; it sends `ProfileChanged`.\n- **Death:** as single player: 3 s, then respawn at the place's spawn with full life. Other\n  players see you fall and reappear.\n- **Saves:** each game saves its own hero, as now. Rewind history isn't recorded online.\n\n## 8. Pings, emotes and the co-op UI\n**Pings.** The keys below are free in the game today (`pav_app/src/input.rs` l.111-129,\n`app.rs` l.835-944).\n- **World pings:** `V` (or mouse Back) pings the ground under the cursor, and the context picks\n  the kind:\n  - a monster → **Danger**;\n  - an item, feature or spot → **Look**;\n  - your own hero → **Help**;\n  - anything else → **Go**.\n\n  Hold `V` for a wheel of the four. On a pad, R3 pings at the aim point (hold it for the wheel).\n  On touch, a ping button sits in the top-right row (`touch.rs` l.410-442).\n- **Map pings:** with the big map open (`M`), click while holding `V` (or `Alt`) to ping that\n  spot.\n- **What the others see** (players in the same place only):\n  - a ring and beam in the pinger's colour, with the kind's icon, for 4 s, drawn by `pav_view`\n    so captures show them;\n  - an arrow at the screen edge with the distance, when the ping is off screen;\n  - a blip on the minimap and the big map;\n  - a short synth cue per kind (`pav_audio`).\n- **Limit:** at most 3 pings per 3 s per player; extras are dropped.\n\n**Emotes.**\n- **Eight**, defined as data in `game/emotes.toml` (key, name, clip, loop, icon): wave, cheer,\n  bow, dance, laugh, point, sit and taunt.\n  - Clips come from the store (`assets shelf=clip`, `assets find=<word> kind=clip`), keeping\n    each source's credit and licence: open-licence tiers only, never `anim/local`.\n  - A missing clip is made through the Animation Studio's loop.\n- **Use:** hold `B` for the emote wheel (pad: L3; touch: an emote button). The emote plays on\n  your hero at once and stops when you move, attack or dodge. Other players' games play it on\n  your hero too.\n- *Unverified:* that every clip plays on all three heroes' puppets. Check with `animsheet` in\n  C4.\n\n**UI** (egui in `pav_app`; the `coop` tool reports the same state as text, because captures\ndon't show egui):\n- **Pause menu → Co-op:**\n  - **Host:** the code, a copy button, and \"players n/4\".\n  - **Join:** a code, or `CODE@ip:port` for a host's embedded server.\n  - **Leave.**\n- **Flags and settings:**\n  - flags: `--host`, `--join CODE[@addr]` and `--name NAME`;\n  - `shardfall.toml`: `name`, `signal = \"wss://...\"`, `signal_port = 3536`, `turn`,\n    `turn_user` and `turn_pass`;\n  - the browser: `?join=CODE`.\n\n  The game still boots into town; hosting turns the running game into a session.\n- **Party frames**, up to three: slot colour, name, character, level, a life bar, the place\n  (\"Emberwatch\", \"Depth 7, The Molten Floor\"), a dead mark, and a link-quality dot.\n- **Name plates** over remote heroes (`pav_view`): name and level in the slot colour, with a thin\n  life bar.\n- **Map:** remote heroes as arrows in their colours, and pings.\n- **Toasts** for joins, leaves and lost connections. The text is fixed; there is no chat.\n- **Slot colours:** 1 amber `#f2a33a`, 2 cyan `#3ac8f2`, 3 lime `#8fe04a`, 4 magenta `#e04ad0`.\n\n## 9. Transport and connection\n- **The libraries:** **matchbox_socket 0.12.0** (2025-05-21) and **matchbox_signaling 0.12.0**.\n  - They are the newest versions at least 12 months old on 2026-10-16 (doctrine 9; 0.14 is\n    current). Licence: MIT or Apache-2.0.\n  - The same API runs on native and wasm: full-mesh WebRTC data channels with\n    `ChannelConfig::unreliable()`, `reliable()` and custom configs (reliable and unordered is\n    `ordered: false` with unlimited retransmits), ICE servers (`RtcIceServerConfig`, Google\n    STUN by default), and a pluggable `SignallerBuilder`.\n  - Native-to-browser play is a stated matchbox feature, *unverified* here until C3.\n- **Rooms:** the signaling library's built-in full mesh (`full_mesh_builder`) puts every\n  connected peer in one mesh. The standalone `matchbox_server` keeps rooms apart by URL path,\n  using its own topology on the signaling library; its newest qualifying release is 0.11.0, and\n  it has no 0.12. `pav_net` reuses that pattern on `matchbox_signaling` 0.12, so the host's\n  embedded server and the public `shardfall-signal` binary run the same code.\n- **Native:**\n  - It uses webrtc-rs 0.13, with `ring` 0.17 and `rustls` and no OpenSSL; matchbox's lockfile\n    lists about 300 crates.\n  - One `net` thread runs a tokio runtime. On it run matchbox's message loop (which reaches\n    tokio through `async-compat`) and, on the host, the embedded signaling server (axum on\n    tokio).\n  - The sim thread drains and fills the socket's channels each tick.\n  - Check the Windows cross-build (`scripts/build-windows.sh`) on the first day of C3.\n- **wasm** uses the browser's own WebRTC. The message loop runs under\n  `wasm_bindgen_futures::spawn_local`, and the socket is polled in `pump()`\n  (`pav_app/src/app.rs` l.990). No threads and no blocking calls.\n- **Signaling (E1):**\n  - By default, the host's native game runs `pav_net`'s signaling server on port 3536.\n    Joiners use `CODE@host:3536` on a LAN, through a port forward, or over a VPN such as\n    Tailscale.\n  - A browser page served over https can only reach a `wss://` server.\n    Internet play from the browser waits on E1.\n  - Recommended: `shardfall-signal` in a small container with TLS, its URL in\n    `shardfall.toml` and the web build.\n- **NAT:** STUN only by default. TURN comes from settings if a real session fails to connect;\n  most home networks connect without it.\n- **Sizes:** unreliable messages are at most 1,200 bytes; snapshots go in 16 KB chunks on the\n  bulk channel.\n\n## 10. Tools, tests and targets\n- **`SimNet`:** N players in one process, each with a full `Sim`, over links with seeded latency,\n  jitter, loss and bandwidth. One tick steps every game and the network. Runs are deterministic,\n  so tests and tools repeat exactly.\n- **The `coop` tool** (one tool, help at most 50 tokens):\n  - `coop players=4 latency=80 jitter=15 loss=0.02` starts an in-process session; player 1 is\n    the current session.\n  - `coop peer=2` points the other tools (`input`, `player`, `capture`, `see`, `game_cmd`,\n    `level`) at player 2's game; `step` advances every game.\n  - `coop` with no arguments reports, for each player: place, role, life, RTT, KB/s,\n    interpolation delay, mirror error, unconfirmed claims and resyncs.\n  - `coop bots=true seconds=60` lets bots drive every player, and reports kills, deaths, XP and\n    clear times with the network numbers.\n  - Through `pav live` it reports the real session.\n- **Targets.** These are objective stand-ins for \"feels lag-free\", measured at 100 ms RTT, ±20 ms\n  jitter and 2% loss, with four players in one level:\n\n  | Measure | Target |\n  |---|---|\n  | Own-hero input latency | 0 ticks (true by construction; asserted) |\n  | Mirror error: a guest's mirror vs the owner's actor at the same owner tick | median ≤ 0.15 m, p99 ≤ 0.5 m |\n  | Pops: corrections over 0.5 m in one frame | ≤ 1 per minute per player |\n  | Bandwidth | ≤ 40 KB/s per player |\n  | Hit confirmation (the watermark passing a claim) | median ≤ RTT + 2 ticks, p99 ≤ 2 × RTT |\n  | A guest's tick cost | ≤ the owner's |\n- **Suites** in `crates/pav_core/tests/coop.rs` (new; cargo, no GPU):\n  - the handshake: version mismatch, a full session;\n  - entering, joining, leaving, handoff, owner drop and host drop;\n  - a graceful handoff loses no change; an owner drop with players far apart promotes the\n    standby without stale actors; a host drop in mid-broadcast converges on one directory;\n  - a place stream that beats its `Spawned` is buffered, not resynced;\n  - the claim watermark: predicted life never subtracts a claim twice, and claims that arrive\n    out of order never move the watermark past a gap;\n  - a handoff in mid-fight loses no claim;\n  - a spawned brood's `master` points at the right actor in every game;\n  - travelling between two occupied places leaves no stale `NetId`;\n  - a guest's kill confirmed by the owner, with the guest's own loot;\n  - victim-side hits: a dodge on the guest's screen counts;\n  - scaling, and the live rescale;\n  - ping and emote delivery;\n  - one player's replay reproducing its hash;\n  - every existing suite, unchanged.\n- **`pav_net` tests:** two native peers and the embedded signaling server on 127.0.0.1 exchange\n  messages on all four channels, and two rooms on one server stay apart.\n- **`scripts/coop-local.sh`** (new), all on localhost, with screenshots from each window into\n  `out/coop/`:\n  - a native host under Xvfb and lavapipe, with `--bridge 127.0.0.1:7878`;\n  - a native joiner on `:7879`;\n  - a browser tab in headless Chromium with `?join=` (the flags are in `docs/REFERENCE.md`,\n    \"Browser and phones\").\n- **Windows:** the `.exe` hosting under Wine (`scripts/smoke-windows.sh`) with a Linux joiner.\n- **Cost:** these are tool checks with no model calls, so they may run as often as needed\n  (`AGENTS.md`, \"Testing budget\"). No agent judges; looks and feel go to the user.\n\n## 11. Stages\nEach stage is one PR, merged when its checks pass (`AGENTS.md`, \"Per PR\"), with a dated\n`docs/HISTORY.md` entry and `docs/PROGRESS.md` brought up to date. Single player must behave\nexactly as before after every stage.\n\n**C0: several heroes in one place (no network).**\n- **Remote-drive mode** for characters (`Character.remote`): a kinematic target, with the\n  animation fed from velocity, posture and grounded. The `riding` early-out (`character.rs`\n  l.494) is the template, but it skips animation, which this mode keeps.\n- **Remote heroes** as `Team::Hero` actors with their own looks. Generalize `refresh_hero`\n  (`arpg/mod.rs` l.441) to any hero profile.\n- **AI for several heroes:** monsters target the nearest living hero, sticky with hysteresis;\n  `hero_flow` is kept per targeted hero; aggro checks every hero. `PLAYER_CRUMBLE` and the\n  other player-only engine checks take any hero.\n- **Split `Game`:**\n  - The hero side is `hero`, `hero_id` and the hero's `Actor`, `loot`, `gold`, `vendor`,\n    `buyback`, `respawn`, `travel`, `floaters`, `message`, `hitstop`, `shake`, `streak`,\n    `echoes`, `power_clock`, `level_flash`, the loot settings, the bags cache and `roster`.\n  - The rest is the place side.\n  - Add `adopt_place(snapshot, hero_side)`, and have travel use it too.\n- **The damage-routing check** (§5.6) with the single-player role, and the `coop.*` params with\n  the scaling functions.\n- **Exit:**\n  - every suite passes unchanged;\n  - `campaign from=1 to=3` matches `main`;\n  - a new test adds a second, remote-driven hero, and monsters go for the nearer one.\n\n**C1: a session over the simulated network.**\n- `pav_core::net`: `SimNet`, `InterpBuffer` and the link clock.\n- `arpg::coop`:\n  - the handshake (with the code), and the directory with host terms and reports;\n  - entering, joining and leaving, and snapshot adoption (keeping the recipient's co-op\n    state);\n  - the standby and its stream, the three-step handoff, promotion, and resync (buffering\n    early streams);\n  - the hero and place streams, and spawn and despawn with complete actors.\n- `Sim::step_net`, the replay's `net` track, and the `coop` tool.\n- **Exit:** the session suites in `coop.rs` pass, and a four-player `coop` run meets §10's\n  mirror-error, pop and bandwidth targets.\n\n**C2: combat and rewards online.**\n- Hit claims with ids, resolved results and the watermark; prediction; victim-side monster\n  hits; `Killed` with its tick, and personal rewards.\n- `PlaceState` and the mechanics, checking §5.5's table mechanic by mechanic.\n- Scaling with the live rescale in every game, and the online rules (§7).\n- **Exit:**\n  - `coop bots=true` with four players clears levels 1-3 and kills the Hollow King on level 4;\n  - the scaling check (§6) is done;\n  - each class of mechanic has a suite.\n\n**C3: the real network.**\n- `pav_net`: matchbox on native and wasm, and the signaling server with rooms (embedded, and the\n  `shardfall-signal` binary).\n- App wiring: the net thread, the wasm pump, flags and settings, the Co-op window, and handshake\n  errors on screen.\n- `scripts/coop-local.sh`.\n- **Exit:**\n  - two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC, with screenshots in\n    the PR;\n  - the Windows build compiles and hosts under Wine with a Linux joiner.\n\n**C4: presence.**\n- Party frames, name plates, the map, pings (world and map), emotes (data, wheel, clips), pad and\n  touch bindings, and sound cues.\n- **Exit:** captures show name plates and pings, smoke screenshots show the egui parts, and\n  suites cover delivery.\n\n**C5: hardening and docs.**\n- Hardening:\n  - rejoining within 60 s keeps your slot;\n  - host and owner drops are handled under load;\n  - bandwidth caps;\n  - net lines in `shardfall.log`: connect, RTT, owner changes, resyncs.\n- Docs:\n  - `docs/COOP.md` (new) for players: hosting, joining, a port forward or Tailscale;\n  - `docs/REFERENCE.md`: the tool and the tests;\n  - a pointer in `AGENTS.md`, which must stay under 32 KiB;\n  - a section in `docs/GAME.md`;\n  - `docs/PROGRESS.md`.\n- **Exit:** a 30-minute soak with four bots, one owner drop and one host drop: no stuck place,\n  no panic, and fewer than one resync per minute.\n\n**C6: internet play (after E1).** The public signaling server and optional TURN. The web build\npoints at the server, the user plays a real session, and their notes become follow-up work.\n\n## 12. Rulings\nThese are asked in chat with this plan (2026-10-16; `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`). Each has a default,\nwhich the run applies if no answer has come 15 minutes after it reaches the question.\n\n| # | Question | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Where does a public signaling server run? The account, and any small cost, are the user's. | None yet. Hosts run the embedded server, internet play needs a port forward or a VPN, and browser players wait for the answer (C6). |\n| E2 | Scale by the players in the place, or by everyone in the session (the request's literal \"in the game\")? | In the place, so a player fighting alone doesn't face four players' monsters. |\n| E3 | Personal loot, or one shared drop to fight over? | Personal: each player gets single player's drops. |\n\n## 13. Risks\n- **webrtc-rs on Windows (mingw):** `ring` builds C and assembly. Verify this first in C3; if it\n  won't build, escalate rather than drop the browser.\n- **Strict NATs:** some pairs of players won't connect without TURN (C6).\n- **Snapshot size grows with content:** measured at 41-175 KB; budget 300 KB, and leave caches\n  out with `#[serde(skip)]`.\n- **Big snapshots on the browser's main thread:** decoding about 1.5 MB of CBOR may hitch a\n  frame. Measure in C3, and spread the work over frames if needed.\n- **Promotion after a drop is approximate:** brains restart, and up to 5 s of far-off changes\n  can be lost. That's acceptable for a crash; graceful leaves hand over exactly.\n- **The MCP tool list is full:** keep to one `coop` tool with short help, and trim elsewhere if\n  `asset_errors.rs` fails.\n- **The bot's known stall** on level 4's seal (`docs/PROGRESS.md`, \"Open issues\") may block C2's\n  exit; fix it there if it does.\n\n## 14. Running it\n**On hold:** use this section only once the user takes co-op off hold (see the status at the\ntop).\n\nThe expected size is about 8,000-12,000 lines over six PRs (C0-C5); C6 waits on E1.\n1. **Before you start:** answer E1-E3 (§12) if you can, so the run doesn't stop on them.\n2. **Start fresh, don't compact.** Open a new session, or `/clear` this one, then give the\n   `/goal` below. This file carries everything the run needs, and a `/compact` summary of\n   earlier work would add tokens that every later call re-reads. A new cloud session builds\n   `pav` in its start hook.\n3. **Effort: extra (xhigh).** The design decisions are made here; what remains is a long,\n   careful engineering run with tool-checked exits. That suits xhigh. Max would mostly add cost,\n   and medium or high would risk cutting corners on ownership and resync edge cases.\n4. **The goal line:**\n\n```\n/goal Build co-op as docs/COOP_PLAN.md specifies: stages C0-C5 in order, one PR per stage merged when its checks pass, single player unchanged. Done when §10's targets hold in a four-player bot session and two native games and a browser tab play together over localhost WebRTC.\n```\n","numLines":653,"startLine":1,"totalLines":653}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011bE3EakJJzQqNEmCpNAFZk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
