{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9UwQvRBCRyGKnLDh8D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016CjAj32n9vFkHYdAveYomh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,90p crates/pav_core/src/figure.rs; cat docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md | sed -n 1,110p; cat docs/MATERIALS.md | sed -n 1,60p; grep -n \"material\" rooms/materials.toml | head -14; grep -n \"material\" crates/pav_core/src/room.rs | head","description":"Read figure system, object studio docs and materials docs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1327,"cache_read_input_tokens":261178,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1327},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"55110b6e-7438-40de-92a6-857c1f4d9a9b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:14.537Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9Uw83ozCjaBi6huzYk","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016CjAj32n9vFkHYdAveYomh":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall && sed -n 1,90p crates/pav_core/src/figure.rs; cat docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md | sed -n 1,110p; cat docs/MATERIALS.md | sed -n 1,60p; grep -n \"material\" rooms/materials.toml | head -14; grep -n \"material\" crates/pav_core/src/room.rs | head","description":"Read figure system, object studio docs and materials docs"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_016CjAj32n9vFkHYdAveYomh":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/_research/shardfall"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CjAj32n9vFkHYdAveYomh","type":"tool_result","content":"//! Imported figures: rigged characters from model libraries (KayKit's adventurers), drawn as\n//! rigid per-bone segments that follow the biped puppet's skeleton, so every clip, move and\n//! procedural walk the puppets play plays on them (docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md §4, S3b).\n//!\n//! A figure is two readable files in a library pack, both written by `asset_import`:\n//! - the body, `<pack>/<name>.json`: a format-2 prop of category `character` whose parts are\n//!   the segments, one mesh part each (`meshes/<name>_<segment>.json`), named after the\n//!   segment and placed in a standing display pose (what the Catalogue and previews show);\n//! - the rig, `<pack>/rigs/<name>.json` (`RigFile`): which of the model's bones each segment\n//!   takes, each segment's rest joint and tail (cm, the engine's frame: feet on the floor,\n//!   facing +z), and the look's proportions read from the bones (a `PuppetDef` fragment, m).\n//!\n//! A segment's mesh is written in its own frame: the origin at its joint, the torso's and the\n//! head's axes the character's, a limb's +y along the bone and +z the way its middle joint\n//! bends out (elbows back, knees forward), a foot's +y forward and +z up. Posing sets each\n//! frame from the puppet's `Skel`: the torso and head turn with the pelvis, chest and head; a\n//! limb's root follows its parent, its end the puppet's hand or ankle, and its middle joint is\n//! solved again at the figure's own (unequal) bone lengths; the body then stands with its\n//! lowest point where the puppet's is. Nothing is skinned: each segment moves whole.\n//!\n//! Sides: the puppet carries a clip's right side at +x (facing +z), a mirror image of the\n//! capture. A figure keeps its own mesh, so its limbs are matched to the puppet's by where they\n//! sit, not by name: the chain whose rest joint is at +x follows the puppet's index 1. A glTF\n//! character faces +z with its left side at +x, so it performs clips as the puppets do (both\n//! mirror images of the captures) and its motion agrees with the procedural walk.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, OnceLock, RwLock};\n\nuse glam::{Mat3, Quat, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\nuse crate::issue::{Code, Issue};\nuse crate::props::geom::Primitive;\nuse crate::props::{self, PropDef};\nuse crate::puppet::{PuppetDef, Skel};\n\n/// The segments of the engine's figure body plan, in drawing order. `L` and `R` are the rig's\n/// own sides (VRM's left and right).\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\npub enum Seg {\n    Hips,\n    Spine,\n    Chest,\n    Head,\n    UpperArmL,\n    LowerArmL,\n    HandL,\n    UpperArmR,\n    LowerArmR,\n    HandR,\n    UpperLegL,\n    LowerLegL,\n    FootL,\n    UpperLegR,\n    LowerLegR,\n    FootR,\n}\n\nimpl Seg {\n    pub const ALL: [Seg; 16] = [\n        Seg::Hips,\n        Seg::Spine,\n        Seg::Chest,\n        Seg::Head,\n        Seg::UpperArmL,\n        Seg::LowerArmL,\n        Seg::HandL,\n        Seg::UpperArmR,\n        Seg::LowerArmR,\n        Seg::HandR,\n        Seg::UpperLegL,\n        Seg::LowerLegL,\n        Seg::FootL,\n        Seg::UpperLegR,\n        Seg::LowerLegR,\n        Seg::FootR,\n    ];\n    pub const NAMES: [&'static str; 16] = [\n        \"hips\",\n        \"spine\",\n        \"chest\",\n        \"head\",\n        \"upper_arm_l\",\n        \"lower_arm_l\",\n        \"hand_l\",\n        \"upper_arm_r\",\n        \"lower_arm_r\",\n        \"hand_r\",\n# Object Studio\n\nObjects (props) are JSON documents of named parts. A command is one line, `tool key=value ...`\nor `{\"tool\":\"…\",\"args\":{…}}`; keep it on that line, document and all: a line break splits it\ninto broken commands.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. `assets find=words` finds a starting point: templates (BUILTIN/box, crate, bench, lantern),\n   library packs (LIB/pack/name, read-only: copy one; imported packs: docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md) and\n   saved objects (WORKSHOP/name). Rows are name cat size_cm tier mesh tok tags desc, best first\n   (tier: standard, checked or disk; mesh: % read-only mesh); a miss says `none` with a route;\n   filters `category=`, `tags=` (`@sit`: a socket), `tier=standard` (what every build has),\n   `similar=NAME`; `next` pages.\n2. Make the object and look at it in one go: a create command like the one below and, on the\n   next line, `asset_preview name=x views=true figure=true`. The create reply has size_cm and\n   check (what is wrong); the preview writes a sheet of four views beside a 1.8 m figure, the\n   image to look at (the reply's path; no capture needed).\n3. If the check or the sheet shows a fault, fix it in one more go: a patch (below) and a new\n   sheet. Otherwise stop; the first go is often the whole job.\n\n## A document\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"create\",\"name\":\"bench\",\"asset\":{\"format\":2,\"name\":\"bench\",\"unit\":\"cm\",\"description\":\"A wooden bench, its backrest leaning on the rear legs.\",\"materials\":{\"wood\":{\"color\":\"#bc8150\"},\"wood_dark\":{\"color\":\"#594635\"}},\"parts\":{\"seat\":{\"type\":\"rounded_box\",\"size\":[220,18,76],\"radius\":2.5,\"pos\":[0,80,0],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\"backrest\":{\"type\":\"rounded_box\",\"size\":[220,8],\"radius\":2.5,\"from\":[0,92,-38],\"to\":[0,141,-48],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\"leg_front\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[10,8],\"from\":[85,0,25],\"to\":[85,71,25],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"mirror\":\"x\"},\"leg_back\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[10,8],\"from\":[85,1,-26],\"to\":[85,141,-56],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"mirror\":\"x\"},\"crossbar\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[6,6],\"from\":[-80,31,-33],\"to\":[80,31,-33],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\"}},\"sockets\":{\"sit\":{\"pos\":[0,89,4]}}}}}\n```\n\nCentimetres; x right, y up, z forward (+z is the front); the origin is on the floor under the\ncentre. Part names: lowercase letters, digits, _ and -.\n\n- **Place** a part by pos (its centre); by from/to, the visible ends of a box, rounded_box,\n  cylinder, cone, tube or capsule (they set its length: a box takes size [across, thick]); or by\n  attach:\"seat\" (on its top; {\"to\":\"seat\",\"at\":\"front\"}: top, bottom, front, back, left, right)\n  plus offset. yaw, pitch, roll: degrees about y, x, z; +yaw turns +z toward +x, +pitch tips +z\n  down. Round parts stand along y; axis:\"x\" lays one along x.\n- **Shapes** (type; full sizes): box size; rounded_box size, radius (at most half the thinnest\n  side); sphere radius; ellipsoid size; cylinder radius, height; cone radius, radius_end, height;\n  capsule radius, height; tube radius, thickness, height; torus radius, minor_radius; wedge size,\n  peak (-1 back … 1 front, 0 a gable); lathe profile [[r,y],…]; extrude points [[x,y],…],\n  depth. arc (degrees) keeps part of a round shape, its middle toward facing (x, y, z, -x, -y,\n  -z).\n- **Colour.** Each part needs color (#rrggbb or a role: $floor $floor2 $wall $pillar $accent\n  $light) or mat: a key in materials ({color, look, emissive}, or a patterned library material\n  such as \"MAT/oak_planks\"), or a library name itself. Patterns, the library and your own\n  materials: docs/MATERIALS.md. look: cel (default), flat, lit, unlit, cutout. emissive 0-32\n  glows; above 0.5 it lights the room.\n- **Groups.** parts:{…} in a node makes a group: its children sit in its frame and inherit mat,\n  color, look, emissive and solid.\n- **Copies.** mirror:\"x\" (or [\"x\",\"z\"]) adds the reflection; array:{count, step:[x,y,z]} a row;\n  grid:{count:[a,b], step:[sa,sb]} a floor grid (plane:\"xy\": a wall); radial:{count, radius} a\n  ring round the part's position, each copy facing out (axis:\"z\", arc:180, start:-90: an arch).\n  All are centred. cycle goes inside one (array:{count, step, cycle:[\"wood\",\"wood_dark\"]}) and\n  gives copy i entry i mod n. Copies are named leg[0], mirror copies leg~x.\n- **More.** solid:false: not in the collider; hidden:true: collision only. light:{color, radius,\n  intensity} and fx:{preset} (fire, smoke, sparks, embers, steam, magic …) sit at a visible part.\n  chance, jitter and a group's pick:n vary copies by seed. sockets: sit, grip, grip2, tip, top,\n  hang, wall_mount. {\"ref\":\"BUILTIN/crate\",\"pos\":[…],\"scale\":0.5} reuses an asset.\n  {\"mesh\":\"kenney_mini_dungeon/chair\"} is a mesh part `asset_import` wrote: place, scale, mirror,\n  recolor:{\"0\":\"#rrggbb\"} or solid:false it; never edit its file.\n\nLimits: 256 nodes, 1,024 parts after copies, 4 lights, 16 mesh parts. `asset_edit action=schema [topic=node]` is\nthe field guide (also tags, category, spin, sway, segments, half, half_height, remap, source, fit,\nmesh, recolor; material base, roughness, metallic, map, turn, layers).\n\n## Edits\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"patch\",\"name\":\"x\",\"if_revision\":\"prop2-…\",\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"set\",\"part\":\"seat\",\"fields\":{\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"size\":[220,20,76]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"part\":\"arm\",\"value\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[6,20,60],\"pos\":[-104,99,0],\"mat\":\"wood\",\"mirror\":\"x\"}},{\"op\":\"remove\",\"part\":\"crossbar\"}]}}\n```\n\nif_revision is the last reply's revision. Ops target part (roof.slab in a group; parts:[…] for\nseveral), material or socket: set merges fields (\"array.count\":8 reaches in, null deletes); add\ntakes a whole part; also remove, rename, copy (to), mirror, array, expand (copies become parts),\nmove (by), scale (by), unit (to). One bad op rejects the batch; dry_run:true saves nothing; undo,\nredo. create refuses an existing name: action=replace resends a whole document, a patch just\nthe change.\nReplies carry revision, size_cm (width x height x depth), changed_parts and new findings.\naction=inspect returns the document (resolved=true: every part after copies).\n\n## Families (params and presets)\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"patch\",\"name\":\"fence\",\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"set\",\"fields\":{\"params.length\":{\"range\":[100,300],\"set\":{\"post.pos.0\":0.5,\"rail.size.0\":1}},\"params.pickets\":{\"range\":[3,15],\"int\":true,\"set\":{\"picket.array.count\":1}},\"params.broken\":{\"choices\":[\"no\",\"yes\"],\"set\":{\"picket.chance\":[1,0.6]}},\"presets.long\":{\"length\":300,\"pickets\":13}}}]}}\n```\n\nA range param sets each target to value × factor; a choices param sets each target to that\nchoice's value. The file's own values are the defaults, so a family is still a concrete asset:\na range's targets must agree on one default (within 0.5 or 0.5%; the check names the factor that\nfits), and the first choice must match what the file writes. Targets are part.field paths\n(group.child.size.1, rail.array.span.0, materials.wood.color), never type, ref, mesh or\nattach.to: attach, mirror, array.span and chance place what depends on a size. At most 8 params,\n8 targets each, 8 choices, 16 presets; a document set (above) adds them.\nPlace or show a variant by preset, knobs over it, or both: `asset_spawn name=x:long\nparams={\"broken\":\"yes\"}`, `asset_preview name=x:low` (params=, seed=), a room's\n`asset = \"LIB/core/x:low\"` with `params = { height = 70 }`. Example:\ndocs/asset_plan/examples/families/fence.f2.json.\n\n**Foundations.** `store/foundations.tsv` names a few standard props per archetype (table, seat,\nbed, barrel, container, crate, light, fire, weapon, tool, stall, shelter, gate, vehicle,\nvegetation) that transform into the rest; `assets shelf=prop` lists them first (LIB/core\nleaves) with their size, knobs and presets. Before making one of these, place a variant: a preset\n(`asset_spawn name=LIB/core/chair:stool`), knob values (`params={\"height\":45}`), a palette for\nits roles (`roles={\"$accent\":\"#2f6fb0\"}`) or a seed. Each row's transforms are tested examples\nand its `why` the technique to copy. Fork one when no knob reaches what you need. A copy of a\ncore asset under its own name (`copy from=LIB/core/table name=table`) is its next revision: it\nkeeps that asset's lineage, so publishing it back adds knobs without making it a fork of itself.\n\n## Check and the rest\n\n`asset_edit action=check name=x [target=WxHxD]` (cm) lists all findings and a cost line. Faults:\n# Materials\n\nA material is a surface recipe drawn per pixel: a base colour and look plus up to 3 pattern\nlayers (planks, brick, cobbles, noise …). No images, no UVs. Props wear them by name.\n\n## Names\n\n- Core, read-only: MAT/oak_planks, barrel_staves (oak planks upright), brick_wall, cobblestone,\n  mossy_stone, roof_shingles, awning_stripes, wrought_iron, gold_trim, rough_rock (cave and cliff\n  rock), checker_tiles, packed_earth (dirt, soil, paths), pb/gold, pb/iron, and the\n  dungeon set in theme colours: flagstones (stones in $floor and $floor2), dungeon_wall ($wall,\n  $floor dust at the foot), pillar_stone ($pillar drums), wall_cap (1 m coping stones).\n  `assets kind=material [find=wood]` lists them with layers and cost.\n- Yours: MAT/workshop/x, saved as assets/materials/workshop/x.json.\n\n## On a prop\n\n```json\n\"materials\":{\"wood\":\"MAT/oak_planks\",\"iron\":\"MAT/wrought_iron\",\"glow\":{\"color\":\"#ffcc66\",\"emissive\":3},\n \"rough\":{\"base\":\"MAT/cobblestone\",\"scale\":0.5,\"map\":\"world\"}},\n\"parts\":{\"lid\":{\"type\":\"cylinder\",\"radius\":30,\"height\":4,\"pos\":[0,92,0],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\n \"band\":{\"type\":\"tube\",\"radius\":31,\"thickness\":1,\"height\":5,\"pos\":[0,80,0],\"mat\":\"MAT/wrought_iron\"},\n \"stain\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[20,1,20],\"pos\":[0,0.5,0],\"mat\":\"wood\",\"color\":\"#6a4a2a\"}}\n```\n\n- materials: key → a library name, or a material written here (lengths in the asset's unit).\n- mat on a part, a group or the document: a key, or a library name directly. Groups pass it on;\n  the nearest mat decides, so a part wearing its own never takes an outer material's look or glow.\n- A part's own color keeps the patterns and replaces the base colour (stain above).\n- How patterns are laid (map): object (the part's own frame; default), axis (round parts:\n  cylinder, cone, capsule, tube, lathe: around and along the axis, so staves run up a barrel),\n  world (world space: neighbouring parts join seamlessly). A material's map overrides.\n- `check` reports unknown_material: an unknown name draws magenta until fixed.\n\n## In levels\n\nA theme in `game/themes.toml` names its materials: `floor_mat`, `wall_mat`, `pillar_mat`,\n`cap_mat` (core names only; all 8 themes use the dungeon set). The level's blocks wear them\nworld-mapped, so neighbouring blocks join; a block keeps its own colour as the base, and a layer's\n`$floor $floor2 $wall $pillar $accent $light` take the theme's colours (endless depths: the\nblended theme's). With a floor material the floor is laid in the first floor colour (no checker)\nand the material varies it. Ice keeps its own tiles. Drawing only: levels, packs and mechanics\nare the same with or without materials.\n\n`view.material_detail` (a view setting: `set path=view.material_detail value=off`): `full`\ndraws every layer, `first_layer` the first only, `off` plain colours. The game's graphics\nquality Medium or Low draws at most `first_layer`; Auto is Medium on phones and tablets, High\n(`full`) on desktops.\n\n## A material\n\n| field | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| base | MAT/name to start from; fields here override, layers replace its layers |\n| color | #rrggbb or a role ($floor $wall $accent …); required unless base |\n| look | cel (default), flat, lit, unlit |\n| emissive | 0-32 glow; above 0.5 lights the room |\n| roughness, metallic | 0-1; metallic adds a highlight band |\n| map | object, world or axis (above) |\n| scale | 0.1-10, multiplies every layer size |\n1:# Bounce & Friction Gallery: what the material numbers (restitution, friction, density) do.\n17:about = \"Drop five balls of rising bounciness, race four boxes down ramps of rising friction, launch yourself from three bounce pads, tip a seesaw, and shove ice, rubber and balls of density 10, 100 and 1000 to feel what the material numbers mean.\"\n41:what = \"Three material numbers, side by side. In the BOUNCE GALLERY five balls of restitution 0 to 0.95 fall from the same height; on the FRICTION RAMPS four boxes of friction 0 to 1.0 sit on 22 degree slopes; the ICE vs RUBBER lanes and the DENSITY 10 / 100 / 1000 balls show how hard things are to push. The LOW, MID and HIGH BOUNCE PADS and the SEESAW round it off.\"\n48:  \"Bounce pads are zones, not materials: anything that lands on one has its upward speed set to the pad's 10, 15 or 22 m/s (you get a quarter more if you hold jump), and gravity decides how high that goes.\",\n68:title = \"The material numbers go onto each object\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"3d85e1f5-f3da-43b5-976c-7ae79ded2ca8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:14.593Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"//! Imported figures: rigged characters from model libraries (KayKit's adventurers), drawn as\n//! rigid per-bone segments that follow the biped puppet's skeleton, so every clip, move and\n//! procedural walk the puppets play plays on them (docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md §4, S3b).\n//!\n//! A figure is two readable files in a library pack, both written by `asset_import`:\n//! - the body, `<pack>/<name>.json`: a format-2 prop of category `character` whose parts are\n//!   the segments, one mesh part each (`meshes/<name>_<segment>.json`), named after the\n//!   segment and placed in a standing display pose (what the Catalogue and previews show);\n//! - the rig, `<pack>/rigs/<name>.json` (`RigFile`): which of the model's bones each segment\n//!   takes, each segment's rest joint and tail (cm, the engine's frame: feet on the floor,\n//!   facing +z), and the look's proportions read from the bones (a `PuppetDef` fragment, m).\n//!\n//! A segment's mesh is written in its own frame: the origin at its joint, the torso's and the\n//! head's axes the character's, a limb's +y along the bone and +z the way its middle joint\n//! bends out (elbows back, knees forward), a foot's +y forward and +z up. Posing sets each\n//! frame from the puppet's `Skel`: the torso and head turn with the pelvis, chest and head; a\n//! limb's root follows its parent, its end the puppet's hand or ankle, and its middle joint is\n//! solved again at the figure's own (unequal) bone lengths; the body then stands with its\n//! lowest point where the puppet's is. Nothing is skinned: each segment moves whole.\n//!\n//! Sides: the puppet carries a clip's right side at +x (facing +z), a mirror image of the\n//! capture. A figure keeps its own mesh, so its limbs are matched to the puppet's by where they\n//! sit, not by name: the chain whose rest joint is at +x follows the puppet's index 1. A glTF\n//! character faces +z with its left side at +x, so it performs clips as the puppets do (both\n//! mirror images of the captures) and its motion agrees with the procedural walk.\n\nuse std::collections::BTreeMap;\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, OnceLock, RwLock};\n\nuse glam::{Mat3, Quat, Vec3};\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\nuse crate::issue::{Code, Issue};\nuse crate::props::geom::Primitive;\nuse crate::props::{self, PropDef};\nuse crate::puppet::{PuppetDef, Skel};\n\n/// The segments of the engine's figure body plan, in drawing order. `L` and `R` are the rig's\n/// own sides (VRM's left and right).\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]\npub enum Seg {\n    Hips,\n    Spine,\n    Chest,\n    Head,\n    UpperArmL,\n    LowerArmL,\n    HandL,\n    UpperArmR,\n    LowerArmR,\n    HandR,\n    UpperLegL,\n    LowerLegL,\n    FootL,\n    UpperLegR,\n    LowerLegR,\n    FootR,\n}\n\nimpl Seg {\n    pub const ALL: [Seg; 16] = [\n        Seg::Hips,\n        Seg::Spine,\n        Seg::Chest,\n        Seg::Head,\n        Seg::UpperArmL,\n        Seg::LowerArmL,\n        Seg::HandL,\n        Seg::UpperArmR,\n        Seg::LowerArmR,\n        Seg::HandR,\n        Seg::UpperLegL,\n        Seg::LowerLegL,\n        Seg::FootL,\n        Seg::UpperLegR,\n        Seg::LowerLegR,\n        Seg::FootR,\n    ];\n    pub const NAMES: [&'static str; 16] = [\n        \"hips\",\n        \"spine\",\n        \"chest\",\n        \"head\",\n        \"upper_arm_l\",\n        \"lower_arm_l\",\n        \"hand_l\",\n        \"upper_arm_r\",\n        \"lower_arm_r\",\n        \"hand_r\",\n# Object Studio\n\nObjects (props) are JSON documents of named parts. A command is one line, `tool key=value ...`\nor `{\"tool\":\"…\",\"args\":{…}}`; keep it on that line, document and all: a line break splits it\ninto broken commands.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. `assets find=words` finds a starting point: templates (BUILTIN/box, crate, bench, lantern),\n   library packs (LIB/pack/name, read-only: copy one; imported packs: docs/ASSET_IMPORT.md) and\n   saved objects (WORKSHOP/name). Rows are name cat size_cm tier mesh tok tags desc, best first\n   (tier: standard, checked or disk; mesh: % read-only mesh); a miss says `none` with a route;\n   filters `category=`, `tags=` (`@sit`: a socket), `tier=standard` (what every build has),\n   `similar=NAME`; `next` pages.\n2. Make the object and look at it in one go: a create command like the one below and, on the\n   next line, `asset_preview name=x views=true figure=true`. The create reply has size_cm and\n   check (what is wrong); the preview writes a sheet of four views beside a 1.8 m figure, the\n   image to look at (the reply's path; no capture needed).\n3. If the check or the sheet shows a fault, fix it in one more go: a patch (below) and a new\n   sheet. Otherwise stop; the first go is often the whole job.\n\n## A document\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"create\",\"name\":\"bench\",\"asset\":{\"format\":2,\"name\":\"bench\",\"unit\":\"cm\",\"description\":\"A wooden bench, its backrest leaning on the rear legs.\",\"materials\":{\"wood\":{\"color\":\"#bc8150\"},\"wood_dark\":{\"color\":\"#594635\"}},\"parts\":{\"seat\":{\"type\":\"rounded_box\",\"size\":[220,18,76],\"radius\":2.5,\"pos\":[0,80,0],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\"backrest\":{\"type\":\"rounded_box\",\"size\":[220,8],\"radius\":2.5,\"from\":[0,92,-38],\"to\":[0,141,-48],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\"leg_front\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[10,8],\"from\":[85,0,25],\"to\":[85,71,25],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"mirror\":\"x\"},\"leg_back\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[10,8],\"from\":[85,1,-26],\"to\":[85,141,-56],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"mirror\":\"x\"},\"crossbar\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[6,6],\"from\":[-80,31,-33],\"to\":[80,31,-33],\"mat\":\"wood_dark\"}},\"sockets\":{\"sit\":{\"pos\":[0,89,4]}}}}}\n```\n\nCentimetres; x right, y up, z forward (+z is the front); the origin is on the floor under the\ncentre. Part names: lowercase letters, digits, _ and -.\n\n- **Place** a part by pos (its centre); by from/to, the visible ends of a box, rounded_box,\n  cylinder, cone, tube or capsule (they set its length: a box takes size [across, thick]); or by\n  attach:\"seat\" (on its top; {\"to\":\"seat\",\"at\":\"front\"}: top, bottom, front, back, left, right)\n  plus offset. yaw, pitch, roll: degrees about y, x, z; +yaw turns +z toward +x, +pitch tips +z\n  down. Round parts stand along y; axis:\"x\" lays one along x.\n- **Shapes** (type; full sizes): box size; rounded_box size, radius (at most half the thinnest\n  side); sphere radius; ellipsoid size; cylinder radius, height; cone radius, radius_end, height;\n  capsule radius, height; tube radius, thickness, height; torus radius, minor_radius; wedge size,\n  peak (-1 back … 1 front, 0 a gable); lathe profile [[r,y],…]; extrude points [[x,y],…],\n  depth. arc (degrees) keeps part of a round shape, its middle toward facing (x, y, z, -x, -y,\n  -z).\n- **Colour.** Each part needs color (#rrggbb or a role: $floor $floor2 $wall $pillar $accent\n  $light) or mat: a key in materials ({color, look, emissive}, or a patterned library material\n  such as \"MAT/oak_planks\"), or a library name itself. Patterns, the library and your own\n  materials: docs/MATERIALS.md. look: cel (default), flat, lit, unlit, cutout. emissive 0-32\n  glows; above 0.5 it lights the room.\n- **Groups.** parts:{…} in a node makes a group: its children sit in its frame and inherit mat,\n  color, look, emissive and solid.\n- **Copies.** mirror:\"x\" (or [\"x\",\"z\"]) adds the reflection; array:{count, step:[x,y,z]} a row;\n  grid:{count:[a,b], step:[sa,sb]} a floor grid (plane:\"xy\": a wall); radial:{count, radius} a\n  ring round the part's position, each copy facing out (axis:\"z\", arc:180, start:-90: an arch).\n  All are centred. cycle goes inside one (array:{count, step, cycle:[\"wood\",\"wood_dark\"]}) and\n  gives copy i entry i mod n. Copies are named leg[0], mirror copies leg~x.\n- **More.** solid:false: not in the collider; hidden:true: collision only. light:{color, radius,\n  intensity} and fx:{preset} (fire, smoke, sparks, embers, steam, magic …) sit at a visible part.\n  chance, jitter and a group's pick:n vary copies by seed. sockets: sit, grip, grip2, tip, top,\n  hang, wall_mount. {\"ref\":\"BUILTIN/crate\",\"pos\":[…],\"scale\":0.5} reuses an asset.\n  {\"mesh\":\"kenney_mini_dungeon/chair\"} is a mesh part `asset_import` wrote: place, scale, mirror,\n  recolor:{\"0\":\"#rrggbb\"} or solid:false it; never edit its file.\n\nLimits: 256 nodes, 1,024 parts after copies, 4 lights, 16 mesh parts. `asset_edit action=schema [topic=node]` is\nthe field guide (also tags, category, spin, sway, segments, half, half_height, remap, source, fit,\nmesh, recolor; material base, roughness, metallic, map, turn, layers).\n\n## Edits\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"patch\",\"name\":\"x\",\"if_revision\":\"prop2-…\",\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"set\",\"part\":\"seat\",\"fields\":{\"mat\":\"wood_dark\",\"size\":[220,20,76]}},{\"op\":\"add\",\"part\":\"arm\",\"value\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[6,20,60],\"pos\":[-104,99,0],\"mat\":\"wood\",\"mirror\":\"x\"}},{\"op\":\"remove\",\"part\":\"crossbar\"}]}}\n```\n\nif_revision is the last reply's revision. Ops target part (roof.slab in a group; parts:[…] for\nseveral), material or socket: set merges fields (\"array.count\":8 reaches in, null deletes); add\ntakes a whole part; also remove, rename, copy (to), mirror, array, expand (copies become parts),\nmove (by), scale (by), unit (to). One bad op rejects the batch; dry_run:true saves nothing; undo,\nredo. create refuses an existing name: action=replace resends a whole document, a patch just\nthe change.\nReplies carry revision, size_cm (width x height x depth), changed_parts and new findings.\naction=inspect returns the document (resolved=true: every part after copies).\n\n## Families (params and presets)\n\n```json\n{\"tool\":\"asset_edit\",\"args\":{\"action\":\"patch\",\"name\":\"fence\",\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"set\",\"fields\":{\"params.length\":{\"range\":[100,300],\"set\":{\"post.pos.0\":0.5,\"rail.size.0\":1}},\"params.pickets\":{\"range\":[3,15],\"int\":true,\"set\":{\"picket.array.count\":1}},\"params.broken\":{\"choices\":[\"no\",\"yes\"],\"set\":{\"picket.chance\":[1,0.6]}},\"presets.long\":{\"length\":300,\"pickets\":13}}}]}}\n```\n\nA range param sets each target to value × factor; a choices param sets each target to that\nchoice's value. The file's own values are the defaults, so a family is still a concrete asset:\na range's targets must agree on one default (within 0.5 or 0.5%; the check names the factor that\nfits), and the first choice must match what the file writes. Targets are part.field paths\n(group.child.size.1, rail.array.span.0, materials.wood.color), never type, ref, mesh or\nattach.to: attach, mirror, array.span and chance place what depends on a size. At most 8 params,\n8 targets each, 8 choices, 16 presets; a document set (above) adds them.\nPlace or show a variant by preset, knobs over it, or both: `asset_spawn name=x:long\nparams={\"broken\":\"yes\"}`, `asset_preview name=x:low` (params=, seed=), a room's\n`asset = \"LIB/core/x:low\"` with `params = { height = 70 }`. Example:\ndocs/asset_plan/examples/families/fence.f2.json.\n\n**Foundations.** `store/foundations.tsv` names a few standard props per archetype (table, seat,\nbed, barrel, container, crate, light, fire, weapon, tool, stall, shelter, gate, vehicle,\nvegetation) that transform into the rest; `assets shelf=prop` lists them first (LIB/core\nleaves) with their size, knobs and presets. Before making one of these, place a variant: a preset\n(`asset_spawn name=LIB/core/chair:stool`), knob values (`params={\"height\":45}`), a palette for\nits roles (`roles={\"$accent\":\"#2f6fb0\"}`) or a seed. Each row's transforms are tested examples\nand its `why` the technique to copy. Fork one when no knob reaches what you need. A copy of a\ncore asset under its own name (`copy from=LIB/core/table name=table`) is its next revision: it\nkeeps that asset's lineage, so publishing it back adds knobs without making it a fork of itself.\n\n## Check and the rest\n\n`asset_edit action=check name=x [target=WxHxD]` (cm) lists all findings and a cost line. Faults:\n# Materials\n\nA material is a surface recipe drawn per pixel: a base colour and look plus up to 3 pattern\nlayers (planks, brick, cobbles, noise …). No images, no UVs. Props wear them by name.\n\n## Names\n\n- Core, read-only: MAT/oak_planks, barrel_staves (oak planks upright), brick_wall, cobblestone,\n  mossy_stone, roof_shingles, awning_stripes, wrought_iron, gold_trim, rough_rock (cave and cliff\n  rock), checker_tiles, packed_earth (dirt, soil, paths), pb/gold, pb/iron, and the\n  dungeon set in theme colours: flagstones (stones in $floor and $floor2), dungeon_wall ($wall,\n  $floor dust at the foot), pillar_stone ($pillar drums), wall_cap (1 m coping stones).\n  `assets kind=material [find=wood]` lists them with layers and cost.\n- Yours: MAT/workshop/x, saved as assets/materials/workshop/x.json.\n\n## On a prop\n\n```json\n\"materials\":{\"wood\":\"MAT/oak_planks\",\"iron\":\"MAT/wrought_iron\",\"glow\":{\"color\":\"#ffcc66\",\"emissive\":3},\n \"rough\":{\"base\":\"MAT/cobblestone\",\"scale\":0.5,\"map\":\"world\"}},\n\"parts\":{\"lid\":{\"type\":\"cylinder\",\"radius\":30,\"height\":4,\"pos\":[0,92,0],\"mat\":\"wood\"},\n \"band\":{\"type\":\"tube\",\"radius\":31,\"thickness\":1,\"height\":5,\"pos\":[0,80,0],\"mat\":\"MAT/wrought_iron\"},\n \"stain\":{\"type\":\"box\",\"size\":[20,1,20],\"pos\":[0,0.5,0],\"mat\":\"wood\",\"color\":\"#6a4a2a\"}}\n```\n\n- materials: key → a library name, or a material written here (lengths in the asset's unit).\n- mat on a part, a group or the document: a key, or a library name directly. Groups pass it on;\n  the nearest mat decides, so a part wearing its own never takes an outer material's look or glow.\n- A part's own color keeps the patterns and replaces the base colour (stain above).\n- How patterns are laid (map): object (the part's own frame; default), axis (round parts:\n  cylinder, cone, capsule, tube, lathe: around and along the axis, so staves run up a barrel),\n  world (world space: neighbouring parts join seamlessly). A material's map overrides.\n- `check` reports unknown_material: an unknown name draws magenta until fixed.\n\n## In levels\n\nA theme in `game/themes.toml` names its materials: `floor_mat`, `wall_mat`, `pillar_mat`,\n`cap_mat` (core names only; all 8 themes use the dungeon set). The level's blocks wear them\nworld-mapped, so neighbouring blocks join; a block keeps its own colour as the base, and a layer's\n`$floor $floor2 $wall $pillar $accent $light` take the theme's colours (endless depths: the\nblended theme's). With a floor material the floor is laid in the first floor colour (no checker)\nand the material varies it. Ice keeps its own tiles. Drawing only: levels, packs and mechanics\nare the same with or without materials.\n\n`view.material_detail` (a view setting: `set path=view.material_detail value=off`): `full`\ndraws every layer, `first_layer` the first only, `off` plain colours. The game's graphics\nquality Medium or Low draws at most `first_layer`; Auto is Medium on phones and tablets, High\n(`full`) on desktops.\n\n## A material\n\n| field | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| base | MAT/name to start from; fields here override, layers replace its layers |\n| color | #rrggbb or a role ($floor $wall $accent …); required unless base |\n| look | cel (default), flat, lit, unlit |\n| emissive | 0-32 glow; above 0.5 lights the room |\n| roughness, metallic | 0-1; metallic adds a highlight band |\n| map | object, world or axis (above) |\n| scale | 0.1-10, multiplies every layer size |\n1:# Bounce & Friction Gallery: what the material numbers (restitution, friction, density) do.\n17:about = \"Drop five balls of rising bounciness, race four boxes down ramps of rising friction, launch yourself from three bounce pads, tip a seesaw, and shove ice, rubber and balls of density 10, 100 and 1000 to feel what the material numbers mean.\"\n41:what = \"Three material numbers, side by side. In the BOUNCE GALLERY five balls of restitution 0 to 0.95 fall from the same height; on the FRICTION RAMPS four boxes of friction 0 to 1.0 sit on 22 degree slopes; the ICE vs RUBBER lanes and the DENSITY 10 / 100 / 1000 balls show how hard things are to push. The LOW, MID and HIGH BOUNCE PADS and the SEESAW round it off.\"\n48:  \"Bounce pads are zones, not materials: anything that lands on one has its upward speed set to the pad's 10, 15 or 22 m/s (you get a quarter more if you hold jump), and gravity decides how high that goes.\",\n68:title = \"The material numbers go onto each object\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016CjAj32n9vFkHYdAveYomh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
