ub"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012fuCH5dP4c4RvBRFJztLYW","type":"tool_result","content":"  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n\nThe hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\ntime fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with\nits own colours, and its name, blurb, facts and keys sit over them. Under it\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\n5:entries in the library, the cc-matrix repository and outside facts, and with\n115:    creative decisions alone.\n135:The owner decided both on Oct 29: no video, and servers prepared in the\n169:left alone. `Math.random` is seeded by the hub before the game's\n222:   left alone.\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n332:  one; an edited copy is reported and left alone. Batch by folder, then spot\n398:  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n404:  live (42 checks), and both advisors show nothing from our migrations.\n431:Then the hub alone, no game changes:\n487:  first. Both that and watcher seats are schema and seat-colour changes\n544:| 3 | WebGPU-only games: drop \"no fallback\" in cc-matrix, or rely on `hub:webgpu`? | Both work; dropping it lets every friend play every new game |\n593:- **The versus example, rewritten to own its player**: bots through the\n22:- **First place is a win**; in a team result (a shared world's `finish` with\n38:The host picks a format in the lobby:\n45:| **Endless** | when the host ends it | most points |\n51:The host's own picks come first: **Up next** in the lobby, the games the host\n53:Random Mode deals. The host picks from every game that seats the players\n56:players yet keeps its place for a later round. A session hosted from a game's\n59:state says `picked: true`, and its card reads *The host's pick*. A pick is\n69:Parallel and ghost games, where each player has a world of their own, far\n70:outnumber the shared worlds, so the deal keeps them as two decks and\n71:alternates between them while both have games, starting with a shared world.\n87:The host can narrow the deal in the lobby, before the first round or back in\n100:line. They travel in the session state, so a seat that takes over as host\n113:   20 (the default) or 30 seconds, as the host sets it in the lobby, before\n134:The host can jump ahead (**Next game now**), skip a game, or end the session.\n140:Parallel and ghost games report `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`.\n149:The host's page also feeds each player's last progress to the round\n150:(`round.progress(id, score)`): a player still in a parallel or ghost round\n173:| hub | `welcome` | `me, host, players, mode, seed, limitMs, round, splash?, limits, server?` | once; a client of 1.3.0 or later keeps any field the hub adds |\n180:| game | `progress` | `score, label?` | parallel and ghost; about once a second (the page drops any closer than half a second) |\n182:| game | `result` | `time` or `score`, `stats?`, `awards?` | parallel and ghost; once |\n183:| game | `finish` | `standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards?` | shared world; the host; once |\n190:For a game tagged parallel or ghost, the page's address carries the round's seed\n192:the game's own scripts run. A game still tagged race plays as ghost but keeps\n206:| `{k:'t', …}` | clock requests to the host | the WebRTC mesh |\n350-- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n351-  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n352-  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n353:  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n354-  *Play on this screen*.\n355-\n356-### The Couch — `/couch`\n357-\n358-The hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\n359-time fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"e7932b35-2f20-474a-95f2-a3df06f49047","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:28:32.295Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n\nThe hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\ntime fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with\nits own colours, and its name, blurb, facts and keys sit over them. Under it\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n\nTwo scripts keep thousands of games on the protocol without editing them by\nhand:\n\n```bash\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\n5:entries in the library, the cc-matrix repository and outside facts, and with\n115:    creative decisions alone.\n135:The owner decided both on Oct 29: no video, and servers prepared in the\n169:left alone. `Math.random` is seeded by the hub before the game's\n222:   left alone.\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n332:  one; an edited copy is reported and left alone. Batch by folder, then spot\n398:  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n404:  live (42 checks), and both advisors show nothing from our migrations.\n431:Then the hub alone, no game changes:\n487:  first. Both that and watcher seats are schema and seat-colour changes\n544:| 3 | WebGPU-only games: drop \"no fallback\" in cc-matrix, or rely on `hub:webgpu`? | Both work; dropping it lets every friend play every new game |\n593:- **The versus example, rewritten to own its player**: bots through the\n22:- **First place is a win**; in a team result (a shared world's `finish` with\n38:The host picks a format in the lobby:\n45:| **Endless** | when the host ends it | most points |\n51:The host's own picks come first: **Up next** in the lobby, the games the host\n53:Random Mode deals. The host picks from every game that seats the players\n56:players yet keeps its place for a later round. A session hosted from a game's\n59:state says `picked: true`, and its card reads *The host's pick*. A pick is\n69:Parallel and ghost games, where each player has a world of their own, far\n70:outnumber the shared worlds, so the deal keeps them as two decks and\n71:alternates between them while both have games, starting with a shared world.\n87:The host can narrow the deal in the lobby, before the first round or back in\n100:line. They travel in the session state, so a seat that takes over as host\n113:   20 (the default) or 30 seconds, as the host sets it in the lobby, before\n134:The host can jump ahead (**Next game now**), skip a game, or end the session.\n140:Parallel and ghost games report `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`.\n149:The host's page also feeds each player's last progress to the round\n150:(`round.progress(id, score)`): a player still in a parallel or ghost round\n173:| hub | `welcome` | `me, host, players, mode, seed, limitMs, round, splash?, limits, server?` | once; a client of 1.3.0 or later keeps any field the hub adds |\n180:| game | `progress` | `score, label?` | parallel and ghost; about once a second (the page drops any closer than half a second) |\n182:| game | `result` | `time` or `score`, `stats?`, `awards?` | parallel and ghost; once |\n183:| game | `finish` | `standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards?` | shared world; the host; once |\n190:For a game tagged parallel or ghost, the page's address carries the round's seed\n192:the game's own scripts run. A game still tagged race plays as ghost but keeps\n206:| `{k:'t', …}` | clock requests to the host | the WebRTC mesh |\n350-- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n351-  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n352-  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n353:  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n354-  *Play on this screen*.\n355-\n356-### The Couch — `/couch`\n357-\n358-The hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\n359-time fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012fuCH5dP4c4RvBRFJztLYW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
