{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCx7GM8BLUxzj7rxaTZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V7zBZVQWhp1TrsRtH9WJMW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/PLAN.md","limit":330},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4189,"cache_read_input_tokens":559180,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4189},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"19ce9d7d-f9a4-4447-a9ac-134b6419134c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:11:35.772Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCx6xzx5KcJdc8WkfJi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01V7zBZVQWhp1TrsRtH9WJMW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/PLAN.md","limit":330}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V7zBZVQWhp1TrsRtH9WJMW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an over-the-shoulder action-horror game on the Pavilion engine\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this without the person who wrote it. Every decision here is already made, so don't reopen one unless something in this plan is impossible. If it is, make the smallest change that keeps the design and note it for your Engine Feedback.\n4\t\n5\t- **The game** is what this plan calls your game. You pick its title the way your TASK.md says, a playful riff on the source's name. A riff, not the name itself. No text in this plan depends on the title.\n6\t- **`tide`** is the engine key for the game: scene name, code folder, asset pack. It never appears on screen.\n7\t- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder. `engine/` is the engine clone inside it.\n8\t- **[V]** marks something the planner tested on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the real engine. Everything else is design you'll be first to run.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 1. The game in one page\n13\t\n14\t**Genre and perspective:** third-person, over-the-shoulder action-horror. You plant your feet to aim a laser sight, a crowd closes in, and precise shots set up melee follow-ups. Resources are tight, a trader keeps turning up, and set-pieces come one after another. The camera stays behind the right shoulder for the whole campaign, with scripted cuts for scenes.\n15\t\n16\t**Premise.** October 1979, North Atlantic. **Cass Harrow** (she/her), a deadpan Senior Field Adjuster for Ironbridge Mutual Marine & General, lands on **Saltmarrow**, a fog-bound fishing island, to close one claim: the Vane & Daughters Fine Tinned Fish cannery has reported a total loss. The islanders are polite, tidy and homicidal. They have taken the Brine, which seeds **saltroot**, a parasitic barnacle that lives in the marrow and rewrites its host. The island's tins are the delivery system, and ten million of them sail on the morning tide unless the owner signs the manifest. Cass has to find the owner, **Mrs. Ottoline Vane** (she/her, 74, unimpressed by everything), get her off the island, and stop the shipment. She does it while the Harbormaster fines her over the town tannoy for every crime she commits, and something very large waits under the harbor: **the Underwriter**.\n17\t\n18\t**Tone.** A straight-faced B-movie pulp thriller. The horror is real, the dialogue is cheesy on purpose, and nobody winks at the camera.\n19\t\n20\t**Pillars**\n21\t1. **Stop. Breathe. Shoot.** Aiming plants your feet. Every shot is a choice about where the crowd will be in two seconds.\n22\t2. **Shots open doors for boots.** Where you hit decides how the enemy reacts, and each reaction offers a melee follow-up. Good players string these together into chains.\n23\t3. **The box is a puzzle and the trader is a friend.** Items are shapes in a grid. Cash, ammo and space are always slightly short. The blue lamp means you can breathe.\n24\t4. **Always one more thing.** No two chapters play alike: siege, factory, escort on a rising tide, cliff ride, lighthouse, sea monster.\n25\t5. **Paperwork is the joke, the barnacle is the threat.** Fines, forms and one-liners run alongside real menace.\n26\t\n27\t**What ships**\n28\t- A five-chapter campaign, about 50 minutes first time through.\n29\t- *Claims Rush*, a 1 to 4 player co-op score mode that plays in AI Hub sessions and alone.\n30\t- Attract mode, dev mode, settings, saves, and a full 10-foot UI.\n31\t- All content is data-driven, so the rest of the game could be built with the same tools (§16).\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## 2. Originality rules (hard rules)\n36\t\n37\tThe game may share genre and mechanics with the source: planted over-the-shoulder aiming, body-part reactions, melee follow-ups, grid inventory, a travelling trader, weapon tuning, treasure combining, escort, crowd control. It must **not** recreate the source. Do not use any of these:\n38\t\n39\t- Names, characters, creatures, organisms, factions or places from the source. No secret-agent rescuing a president's daughter. No Spanish village, castle or military island. No head-burst parasite reveal.\n40\t- The chainsaw man, the church bell that calls enemies away, the lake monster fought from a boat, a knife-fight rival, the attaché case, typewriter saves, herbs, or the trader's famous lines.\n41\t- Its music, its logo, its look, or any of its dialogue, including its well-known jokes.\n42\t\n43\tEverything in this plan already has its own replacement (saltroot and barnacle whips, the foghorn and the walk into the sea, the tackle box, the shortwave logbook, the brass-helmeted Old Fathoms, the hook-and-winch Flenser). If you add content, invent it, and don't borrow from the source to fill a gap.\n44\t\n45\t---\n46\t\n47\t## 3. Verified facts and traps\n48\t\n49\tRead this section twice. Each item cost the planner real time.\n50\t\n51\t### Pins and build times [V]\n52\t```sh\n53\tgit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall.git engine && git -C engine checkout 49ef2ba\n54\tmkdir -p tools/hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n55\t  curl -fsS -o tools/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n56\t```\n57\t- Rust 1.98.1 and the wasm32 target are installed. `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129 matches `Cargo.lock`.\n58\t- From `engine/`: `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=8 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav` takes 4 min the first time and about 1 min after a core change.\n59\t- `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=8 scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene tide --title \"<title>\" --description \"<line>\" --theme-color \"#0e1620\" --out <path>` takes 2 min the first time and about 1 min after, and gives an 11.4 MB page.\n60\t- Native sim speed was about 21,000 ticks/s in a test room with 17 entities and no AI. Expect less with crowds, but still far above real time, so a scripted run through the whole game stays practical.\n61\t- Adding a crate dependency (for example `js-sys` to `pav_app`) makes `--locked` fail until a plain `cargo build` has updated `Cargo.lock`. `build-web.sh` always uses `--locked`.\n62\t- Read `engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It's short and accurate. Ignore its \"Report back\" format: your TASK.md's feedback questions replace it.\n63\t\n64\t### Browser and hub\n65\t- **Chromium flags [V]. Use exactly these everywhere:**\n66\t  `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`\n67\t  - The shorter set in TASK.md's computer notes (`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) kills the whole GPU channel as soon as 3 or more WebGPU copies share a browser. The log shows `SharedImageStub: Unable to create shared image`, then `A valid external Instance reference no longer exists`, then a wgpu `unwrap` panic and `RuntimeError: unreachable`, which fails the hub's \"no errors\" check. It looks like a game bug and isn't.\n68\t  - With the full set, the engine's starter plus a JS stand-in for game logic **passed** the harness on `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&play=8000` at 2, 3 and 4 players, at 4 with `scenario=host-leave`, and at 2 with `scenario=server`. Each run took 10 to 20 s, and `ready()` fired within 2 s of load.\n69\t- **Reading the server's port from a script [V]:** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` block-buffers its \"port\" line on a pipe, so `readline()` hangs forever. Use `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`. Stop it by the PID you started.\n70\t- `python3 -I` can't import Playwright here [V]. Run your own test scripts without `-I`.\n71\t- **Software rendering is slow [V]:** about 1.5 fps at 1280×720 and 5 fps at 640×360 with the bare starter, and 2 to 3 fps at 640×360 with a dressed harbor scene. The sim catches up at most 8 ticks per frame (`simhost.rs`), so a browser runs at 10 to 40 ticks/s against the real 60. **Never play the game through in a browser.** Play it through natively (`pav`, `cargo test`). Browser tests only check boot, input routing, UI and the hub, at 640×360.\n72\t- **First frame can take over 14 s to show on software rendering [V].** Hub-check's \"draws its screen\" test screenshots after about 7 s and needs 1,200 bytes more than a blank page, so a blank canvas fails it. Keep a DOM splash (title text on dark) over the canvas until the first frame has really presented (`queue.on_submitted_work_done`). This also helps real players.\n73\t- WebGPU canvases do appear in headless screenshots with these flags [V], and the stub pad reaches `gilrs` [V]: the log says `gamepad connected`, and the engine's guide switches to gamepad.\n74\t- `pack_single_html.py` refuses wasm-bindgen snippets. **No `inline_js` and no `#[wasm_bindgen(module=...)]`.** Put JS in `web/index.html` and bind with `#[wasm_bindgen] extern \"C\" { #[wasm_bindgen(js_namespace = __hub)] fn poll() -> String; }`.\n75\t- `session.js` goes in unmodified, as the first script right after `<meta charset>`, exactly one copy. The packer needs exactly one `<title>`, `<meta name=\"description\">`, `<meta name=\"theme-color\">`, manifest link and `</body>` in `web/index.html`.\n76\t- Harness key presses are synthetic and dispatched to `doc.activeElement` or `body`, so handle the card's Enter in a `window` listener in JS, not through winit (§11).\n77\t- Handle `time-up` in JS: the sim may be running at 1 fps and the host has only 5 s to call `finish()`.\n78\t\n79\t### Engine traps\n80\t- **Disk-only packs don't exist in the browser [V].** `LIB/quaternius_medieval_village/*` and `LIB/kenney_mini_dungeon/*` work in `pav` but show a red \"Room file errors\" panel in the build. Use only `BUILTIN/*`, `LIB/core/*`, `LIB/kaykit_adventurers/*` and your own shipped pack (§14.2).\n81\t- **An emissive part above 0.5 lights the room [V].** A tiny laser dot at `emissive: 6` turned the whole harbor red. Small glows stay at 0.4 or below. Only real lamps go higher.\n82\t- **`[view]` per area.** With tilt below 30° the view cuts away everything in front of the player unless you set `\"cutaway.front_cut\" = false`, and also `\"cutaway.height_cut\" = false` and `\"cutaway.fade\" = false`.\n83\t- **Unknown block fields in TOML are ignored silently [V].** A legend's `material` did nothing until patched (§14.1 E1).\n84\t- `NavGrid` only treats blocks with tops near y=0 as floor. Upper floors need E8.\n85\t- A game's `[view]` only re-applies when `(room id, view_serial)` changes, and a rebuilt standalone room is always id 0. Bump `view_serial` on travel or the look won't change.\n86\t- `InputFrame` is `Copy`, so `cmd` must stay `Copy`.\n87\t- `Sim` keeps rewind history. Set `history_seconds = 0` in your `build`.\n88\t- `Sim::raycast` excludes the player only. Bullets test bodies with your own capsule code (§5.2).\n89\t- KayKit figures are chibi-proportioned [V]. Don't use them for people in this game.\n90\t- Puppets default to `eyes_to_camera = 0.6`, a cheat for high cameras that slides the eyes toward the viewer. Set it to 0 on every look: this camera sits behind the head.\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## 4. How it plays\n95\t\n96\t### 4.1 Controls\n97\t\n98\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard and mouse |\n99\t|---|---|---|\n100\t| Move | Left stick (half = walk, full = jog) | W A S D |\n101\t| Run | Hold ✕ while moving | Hold Shift |\n102\t| Look | Right stick | Mouse (pointer lock) |\n103\t| Aim (plants feet) | Hold L2 | Hold right mouse |\n104\t| Fire | R2 | Left mouse while aiming |\n105\t| Knife | L1 | F, or left mouse when not aiming |\n106\t| Interact and follow-up | ✕ | E, or left-click the highlighted thing under the crosshair |\n107\t| Reload | □ | R |\n108\t| Sidestep (while aiming or moving) | ○ | Space |\n109\t| Quick turn | Left stick back + ○ | Q |\n110\t| Throwable | R1 (aim first for an arc) | G |\n111\t| Tackle Box | △ | Tab or I |\n112\t| Map | Create | M |\n113\t| Quick weapons | D-pad up, left, right | 1, 2, 3, plus mouse wheel to cycle |\n114\t| Mrs. Vane: follow or wait | D-pad down | C |\n115\t| Swap shoulder | R3 | V |\n116\t| Pause | Options | Esc |\n117\t\n118\t- ✕ does the prompted action on press. With no prompt showing, holding it runs.\n119\t- The hub owns Create + Options held together: ignore Options while Create is held.\n120\t- In `gilrs`, `LeftTrigger` is L1 and `LeftTrigger2` is L2. Read L2 and R2 as analog with a 0.35 threshold.\n121\t- Prompts show the action's glyph for the device used last (§12.2). Show a device change after a 0.3 s stick-noise filter.\n122\t- When a menu is open, the D-pad or left stick moves focus, ✕ confirms, ○ goes back, and L1/R1 change tabs. The mouse hovers to move focus and clicks to activate. On keyboard, arrows or WASD move, Enter or E confirms, Esc goes back.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2 Feel numbers (defaults, all sliders in dev mode, §13.2)\n125\t\n126\t| Thing | Value |\n127\t|---|---|\n128\t| Walk, jog, run | 1.9, 3.4, 5.2 m/s (no firing while running) |\n129\t| Aim in, aim out | 0.18 s, 0.12 s |\n130\t| Move speed while aiming | 0 (planted) |\n131\t| Yaw while aiming, stick | 95°/s max, ease-in curve (stick²), 40% of that within 3° of a head (aim assist) |\n132\t| Mouse look | 0.12°/count, no acceleration, no smoothing while aiming |\n133\t| Sidestep | 0.38 s, 1.6 m, invulnerable for the first 0.22 s, 1.2 s cooldown |\n134\t| Quick turn | 180° in 0.25 s |\n135\t| Camera, moving | right-shoulder offset 0.42 m, arm 2.3 m, eye height 1.55 m, FOV 58° |\n136\t| Camera, aiming | offset 0.5 m, arm 1.25 m, FOV 42°, 0.14 s ease |\n137\t| Camera pitch | −50° to +60° |\n138\t| Laser | muzzle to first hit, thin unlit red beam, dot at hit. 0.25° figure-8 sway. +0.6° bloom per shot, recovers in 0.35 s |\n139\t| Hit-stop | 0.04 s on head hit, 0.08 s on kick, 0.25 s at 30% speed on the last kill of an encounter |\n140\t| Rumble | shot 0.1 s, kick 0.15 s, damage 0.3 s (`vibrationActuator.playEffect` from JS, guarded) |\n141\t\n142\tThe camera spring-arm uses `Sim::raycast` from the shoulder to the ideal eye and pulls in 0.15 m before any hit. Below 0.7 m of arm, swap to the other shoulder.\n143\t\n144\t### 4.3 What the player can and can't do\n145\t\n146\tTeach these limits with one prompt each, the first time they matter:\n147\t- You can't move while aiming. You can't fire while running. Reloading is interrupted by a sidestep.\n148\t- You can't jump. Climbing, vaulting and dropping are contextual, with a prompt.\n149\t- You can't save anywhere: saves happen at shortwave radios.\n150\t- Kicks need a reeling enemy. The knife always works but barely hurts.\n151\t\n152\t**How to Play**, in the pause menu, is 6 big one-idea cards: Aim, Follow-ups, Box, Trader, Mrs. Vane, Saves.\n153\t\n154\t---\n155\t\n156\t## 5. Combat\n157\t\n158\t### 5.1 Hero\n159\t100 HP, shown as a ring gauge. Damage causes a 0.4 s flinch, then 0.8 s of invulnerability. At 0 HP, the screen says **POLICY LAPSED** with *Reinstate* (retry from checkpoint), *Load Log*, *Quit*.\n160\t\n161\t### 5.2 Hit detection\n162\tHitscan rays, except harpoon, thrown items and flare, which are projectiles.\n163\t- Each ray goes from the muzzle to the camera ray's hit point.\n164\t- Test static world with `Sim::raycast`, and bodies with capsules built from `pav_core::puppet::biped_skel(def, &anim)`: head sphere, torso, two arms, two legs. Creatures get authored sphere lists.\n165\t- This is deterministic because it derives from sim state.\n166\t\n167\t### 5.3 Body-part reactions\n168\t\n169\t| Part | Damage mult | Reaction (any hit dealing ≥ 8 cumulative in 1 s) | Follow-up offered |\n170\t|---|---|---|---|\n171\t| Head | ×2, plus a 12% crit that does ×5 | **Reel** 1.6 s, hands to face | **Roundhouse** ✕: 30 dmg, 2.4 m radius, 200° front arc, knocks down everyone hit |\n172\t| Leg | ×0.75 | **Kneel** 1.4 s | **Ledger Slam** ✕: 45 dmg to the target, knockdown |\n173\t| Arm | ×0.75 | Interrupts the swing, 30% chance to drop the tool (then unarmed) | none |\n174\t| Torso | ×1 | Short flinch | none |\n175\t| Knocked down | ×1 | Down 2.0 s | **Finish** ✕ knife: ×3 knife damage, ignores armor |\n176\t\n177\tFollow-ups have a 2.5 m reach, 0.5 s of hero invulnerability and a one-liner chance of 20%. The prompt floats at the target's head. That chain (head shot → kick → crowd down → finish) is the core of the game. Make it feel good first.\n178\t\n179\t### 5.4 Weapons\n180\tDamage assumes 40 HP for a basic enemy. Box size is grid cells W×H.\n181\t\n182\t| Weapon | Box | Mag | s/shot | Reload | Damage | Notes | Source |\n183\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n184\t| Issue .38 | 3×2 | 10 | 0.38 | 1.5 s | 10 | Accurate, crits | Start |\n185\t| Trench Pump | 6×2 | 6 | 0.95 | 0.55 s/shell | 7 pellets × 8, 6° cone | Full reel at ≤ 6 m, falls off past 8 m | Harbor Office, Ch1 |\n186\t| Stitcher | 5×2 | 30 | 0.09 | 2.2 s | 3.5 | Climbs, shreds Bloats, poor stagger | £4,000, Ch2 |\n187\t| Seal Rifle | 7×1 | 5 | 1.30 | 2.6 s | 38, pierces 2 | R3 toggles ×3 zoom while aiming | Ch4 hut, or £5,500 from Ch3 trader |\n188\t| Harpoon Gun | 6×2 | 1 | n/a | 1.8 s | 70 | **Pins** target to the wall behind if within 3 m. Walk over a spent harpoon to retrieve it | Ch3 crypt |\n189\t| Flare Pistol | 2×2 | 1 | n/a | 1.6 s | 6 + burn 20/s for 3 s | Blinds 6 m radius 3 s, instantly kills a Rooted bulb, lights fog | Gift, Mrs. Vane, Ch3 |\n190\t| Filleting Knife | none | n/a | 0.45 | n/a | 6 | Always on L1. Breaks crates. No ammo | Start |\n191\t\n192\t**Throwables** (1×2, stack 3):\n193\t- *Stick*: dynamite, 4 m radius, 90 dmg, 2.2 s fuse.\n194\t- *Magnesium*: stuns 8 m radius for 3 s, kills Rooted.\n195\t- *Oil Lamp*: 3 m fire patch, 5 s, 15 dmg/s.\n196\t\n197\t**Tune-ups at the trader**, three tracks per weapon:\n198\t\n199\t| Track | L1 | L2 | L3 |\n200\t|---|---|---|---|\n201\t| Damage | +20% | +45% | +80% |\n202\t| Reload | −15% | −30% | −45% |\n203\t| Capacity | +30% | +60% | +100% |\n204\t\n205\t- Prices: £800, £1,600, £3,200, × weapon factor (.38 ×1, Pump ×1.2, Stitcher ×1.2, Rifle ×1.5, Harpoon ×1.5, Flare ×1).\n206\t- All three tracks maxed unlocks an **Exclusive** for £6,000: .38 *Hair Trigger* (crit ×2 chance), Pump *Choke* (3° cone), Stitcher *Drum* (mag ×2), Rifle *Straight Pull* (0.6 s/shot), Harpoon *Reel* (auto-retrieves), Flare *Over-and-Under* (mag 2).\n207\t- The final Whaling Cannon is a level prop, not an item.\n208\t\n209\t### 5.5 Enemies\n210\t\n211\tBipeds use the puppet plus worn props (§14). Attack tokens: at most 3 enemies attack at once (Rank changes this), and the rest circle at 2.5 to 4 m and shuffle, so crowds surround you rather than pile up.\n212\t\n213\t| Enemy | Plan | HP | Speed | Attack | Behaviour |\n214\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n215\t| **Parishioner** | biped | 40 | 1.4, runs 3.0 past 12 m at 30% | Tool swing, 0.55 s windup, 1.6 m, 14 dmg. Grab from side or back (hold, others hit you, mash ✕ to break) | Base enemy. Starts on a chore (gutting, mending nets, praying). Yell alerts 15 m. Rank ≥ 6: sidesteps if laser held on it > 0.7 s past 5 m (15%). Breaks doors in 2 kicks, climbs ladders, vaults windows |\n216\t| **Net-Wife** | biped | 35 | 1.2 | Thrown net, 9 m: slows 70% for 2.5 s, mash to tear | Keeps 6 m away. Priority target |\n217\t| **Tinner** | biped | 35 | 1.3 | Lobs lit fish-oil tins, 12 m arc, 2 s fuse, 3 m radius, 35 dmg | Shoot the tin in hand: blows up on them and neighbours |\n218\t| **Rooted** | biped + whip chain | 60 (bulb ×3) | 1.6 | Barnacle whip, 3 m sweep, 22 dmg | A \"dead\" Parishioner may burst into this (§7 sets chance per chapter). Stands back up, chest splits, 3 to 5 verlet whips with a pale bulb. Flare or Magnesium kills at once. On death, spawns 1 Scuttler at Rank ≥ 5 |\n219\t| **Scuttler** | spider | 15 | 4.5 | Leap, 8 dmg | Small and fast. Shotgun or kick |\n220\t| **Sea Hound** | quadruped | 25 | 6.0 | Bite, 10 dmg | Packs of 3 to 5, circle, then rush in pairs |\n221\t| **Bloat** | blob | 30 | 0.9 | Bursts in 3.5 m: 30 dmg, poison cloud 4 s, 8 dmg/s | Shoot it far away. Blast hurts other enemies |\n222\t| **Bailiff** | biped, large | 150 + 4 barnacle plates (30 each) | 1.3 | Anchor-chain sweep, 30 dmg, 3.2 m | Plates block damage and reel until shot off. Kick staggers only after plates are gone |\n223\t\n224\t**Dynamic Rank** (hidden, 1 to 10, starts at 4):\n225\t- Rises with accuracy and no-damage streaks, falls with deaths and low health.\n226\t- Scales enemy damage (×0.75 to ×1.3), tokens (2 to 4), sidestep chance and Rooted chance (±10%).\n227\t- Dev mode shows and can override it.\n228\t- *New Claim* offers three difficulties: **Assistant** (Rank locked ≤ 3), **Adjuster** (default), **Senior Adjuster** (Rank starts at 7, enemy HP ×1.25).\n229\t\n230\t### 5.6 Bosses\n231\t\n232\t**The Flenser** (Mr. Grice from Gutting) (Ch2, returns in Ch5 and as Claims Rush's final wave). 3 m tall biped, rubber apron, iron mask.\n233\t- Walks with a ratcheting winch on its back. You hear it first: a loud, unmistakable clack-clack-clack.\n234\t- Hook-on-chain thrown 12 m: on hit, drags you to it for a Gut (80 dmg unless you mash ✕ in 1.2 s, then 20).\n235\t- HP 900; only the belly tank takes full damage, and the rest is ×0.3.\n236\t- Shooting the belt lantern sets it alight and stuns 3 s.\n237\t- Ch2 ending: lure it onto the press platform and hit the button, or deplete it. The press kill pays £3,000.\n238\t\n239\t**The Bell-Hermit** (Ch3 boss, flooded crypt, HP 2,200). A cathedral-sized hermit crab (beetle plan, scale 2.6) wearing the cracked chapel bell as its shell.\n240\t- *Phase 1:* charges along the crypt, shells off pillars. Shots at the bell spark and do nothing, except they ring it, and enough rings (6) agitate it into a slam.\n241\t- Shoot the eyes (×2) to make it rear up. Shoot the hanging iron clapper chain when it's under the clapper: the clapper strikes the bell, it reels out of the shell for 6 s, and the soft abdomen takes ×3.\n242\t- *Phase 2 (below 50%):* water rises to shin depth (wading ×0.7), Scuttlers spill from the shell, pillars are gone. Kill by repeating the expose loop, or finish with sticks into the open shell.\n243\t\n244\t**The Underwriter** (Ch5 finale, flooded Quay at night). See Ch5, §7. Bulbs 6 × 400 HP, then the mouth takes 3 Whaling Cannon charges.\n245\t\n246\t---\n247\t\n248\t## 6. Economy and items\n249\t\n250\t### 6.1 The Tackle Box (grid inventory)\n251\tA tin fisherman's box with fold-out trays. It starts 10×6, upgrades to 12×7 (£3,000) and 14×8 (£8,000). Every item is a rectangle W×H that may rotate 90°. Weapons, ammo, healing and treasures all take space. A separate **Key Items** page holds no-space items (keys, fuses, forms). If a pick-up won't fit, the box opens with the item on the cursor: place it, discard something, use something, or leave it.\n252\t\n253\t| Item | Box | Stack | Effect or value |\n254\t|---|---|---|---|\n255\t| .38 rounds | 1×1 | 30 | |\n256\t| Shells | 1×1 | 12 | |\n257\t| Stitcher mag | 1×1 | 60 rounds | |\n258\t| Rifle rounds | 1×1 | 10 | |\n259\t| Harpoon | 2×1 | 3 | |\n260\t| Flares | 1×1 | 4 | |\n261\t| Sea Moss | 1×2 | 1 | +30 HP |\n262\t| Iodine | 1×2 | 1 | Combine only |\n263\t| Tincture (Moss + Iodine) | 1×2 | 1 | Full heal |\n264\t| Strong Tincture (Moss + Moss + Iodine) | 1×2 | 1 | Full heal, +10 max HP once per Tincture |\n265\t| Tinned Kippers | 2×1 | 1 | +50 HP (\"Best before: ask again later.\") |\n266\t| Pearl | 1×1 | 3 | £800 |\n267\t| Gold Tooth | 1×1 | 5 | £300 |\n268\t| Scrimshaw | 1×2 | 1 | £1,800 |\n269\t| Ambergris | 2×2 | 1 | £4,000 |\n270\t| Sextant parts (Frame + Mirror + Scale) | 1×1 each | | £600 each, £7,500 combined |\n271\t\n272\t**Combining** is *Combine* on one item, then pick another (no drag-and-drop required).\n273\t\n274\t### 6.2 Drops\n275\tEnemy drop roll: 40% nothing, 25% cash (£100 to £300), 28% ammo for a carried weapon (weighted to whatever you're lowest on), 7% Sea Moss, doubled when under 35% HP. Crates and barrels roll the same table at +20% drop. Campaign total cash is about £40,000, enough to fully tune about 2 weapons, which forces a choice. Picking up is automatic when walking over cash and ammo; other items need ✕ so they can't be taken by accident.\n276\t\n277\t### 6.3 Old Fathoms (trader)\n278\tA man in a full brass diving helmet and canvas suit, who walks up out of the water whenever an electric-blue storm lamp is lit. There are 6 lamps: Net Lane, Loading Dock, Halfway Hut, the Cliff Road hut, lighthouse floor 2, and the Quay before the Underwriter. Nobody ever sees how he got there. Music changes to his warm concertina waltz (§15); enemies never spawn near a lit lamp. Three tabs: **Buy**, **Sell**, **Tune**. Each shows at most 6 big rows with a 3D turntable of the selected item (§12.4).\n279\t\n280\t**Tin Gulls.** 15 tin gull targets on posts around the island, shot on sight. Fathoms pays £500 each, and all 15 unlocks a free Exclusive. Treasure Charts (£1,000 per chapter) mark treasures on the map.\n281\t\n282\t### 6.4 Saves\n283\tA **shortwave radio** in safe spots: *Log Report* saves. Autosave at each checkpoint and chapter start. Save JSON goes to `localStorage` (versioned): area, checkpoint, box grid, weapons and tracks, cash, flags per area (crates broken, pickups taken, encounters cleared), Rank, stats, settings. Three manual slots plus one auto. `Continue` loads the newest. In a hub session the client hands you empty storage, and you save nothing.\n284\t\n285\t### 6.5 Chapter report\n286\tAfter each chapter: time, accuracy, kills, follow-ups, deaths, cash, then a grade (S to D). After Ch5: the Claim Settlement screen, total grade, and unlocks: the *Chapters* menu entry, *Claims Rush* costume colours, the second-run *Actuary* difficulty.\n287\t\n288\t---\n289\t\n290\t## 7. Campaign\n291\t\n292\tEach area is one room file under `engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/tide/levels/`. Sizes are in metres. Treat the beats as a script and the layouts as firm guidance. Keep areas within about 110 m on a side: fog at 46 m hides the edges.\n293\t\n294\t**Chapter data** (chance a killed Parishioner becomes Rooted): Ch1 0%, Ch2 15%, Ch3 25%, Ch4 35%, Ch5 45%.\n295\t\n296\t**Standard area kit:** ASCII `[layout]` + `[game]` numbers + `[[encounter]]` + `[[object]]`/`[[npc]]`, see §16.\n297\t\n298\t### Chapter 1: Notice of Loss (`quay`, dawn fog, 85×65 m, about 13 min)\n299\t```\n300\t   N\n301\t  ###########  ##########  ########        # = buildings   ~ = sea\n302\t  #NET LOFT#=  #OFFICE S#  #CHAPEL #       . = cobbles     = = planks\n303\t  ###.....##    ##.......   ###....        T = blue lamp   S = radio\n304\t   ...HOUSE..  ...SQUARE.......            G = gate north to Cannery Road\n305\t   . NET LANE ...(statue)...  G            P = mail pier\n306\t  ##..T.####..........#####\n307\t  ~~~~===========~~~~~~~~~~~~\n308\t  ~~~~~~~~~CUSTOMS====P===~~~   (pier runs south-east)\n309\t```\n310\t1. **Mail pier.** Skipper Bram's boat drops Cass and leaves. Radio with Tull. Teach move, look, run. Foghorn far off.\n311\t2. **Customs Shed.** A Parishioner with their back turned, gutting. \"Anything to declare?\" Teach aim, shoot, head shot, kick prompt. The **Tackle Box** on the counter: box tutorial with a too-big item. First radio (save). First cash.\n312\t3. **Net Lane.** Narrow lane under drying nets. Shoot the rope to drop a fish rack on a group (trap teach). Crates, first Tin Gull, first Tinner on a roof (teach shooting the tin).\n313\t4. **The House on Net Lane.** Enemies pour in. Push the dresser against the door (hold ✕), they kick through anyway, vault out of the window, kick the ladder off the roof. Tannoy wakes up: Pell's first citation.\n314\t5. **First blue lamp.** Old Fathoms walks up the slip. Buy, Sell, Tune tutorial with a free tune-up credit.\n315\t6. **Harbor Square siege.** 40×35 m plaza, statue of St. Orran with a herring, tannoy horns on poles, two ladders to a roof walkway, doors that open and close. Gates lock on entry. Waves: up to 8 at a time, about 22 total, Tinner on roof, Net-Wife late. Ends at 18 kills or 3:00.\n316\t   - **The foghorn sounds. Every Parishioner stops, sets down their tool, turns and walks calmly into the sea.**\n317\t   - Cass: \"Either that's the tide or the union.\"\n318\t   - Build this moment carefully. It's the game's first big image.\n319\t7. **Harbor Office.** Trench Pump on the wall rack, radio, Pell's ledger, Cannery Gate key. Pell over the desk radio: full introduction. Gate north. Chapter report.\n320\t\n321\t### Chapter 2: Fine Tinned Fish (`cannery`, 70×55 m, two floors, machine light, about 13 min)\n322\tReuse conveyor zones (`zone = { kind = \"conveyor\", facing, speed }`, carries characters and props).\n323\t1. **Yard.** Stacked crates, Hounds. Tin stacks fall if shot. Trader at the Loading Dock (Stitcher for sale).\n324\t2. **Gutting Floor.** Long conveyors with Parishioners on the line, a moving hook-rail that hits for 20 (dodge or lure enemies under it), catwalks above with Net-Wives. Power is out, so doors are shut.\n325\t3. **Boiler Room puzzle.** Find 3 fuses (one in Retort Room, one on the catwalk, one on a corpse in Cold Corridor), fit them in the board: Key Items flow. When the third goes in, lights come up, machinery starts, **first Rooted reveal** on the body you killed a minute ago.\n326\t4. **The Flenser.** Ratchet sound through the walls. It hunts you through Boiler, Corridor and Retort. Retort Room has 6 pressure tanks: shoot a valve to scald anything near it (25 dmg + stun). You can't kill it here easily. Pell: \"Mr. Grice from Gutting will see you now.\"\n327\t5. **Press Room fight.** Lure the Flenser onto the press plate, hit the button (✕). Or shoot it down.\n328\t6. **Cold Store.** Mrs. Vane sitting on a crate, in a fur coat, handbag on knee. \"You're late.\" Escort starts. She has the manifest; Pell needs her signature. Way out: the causeway. Radio. Chapter report.\n329\t\n330\t### Chapter 3: Turning Tide (`causeway` 110×24 m and `crypt` 40×40 m, overcast dusk, rain, about 13 min)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"501e7141-3737-4a8e-9cac-eb3584714d8d","uuid":"a1ae876b-90d8-4c37-a7b2-106ec253ec4f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:11:35.968Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211956__x710a06/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an over-the-shoulder action-horror game on the Pavilion engine\n\nYou are building this without the person who wrote it. Every decision here is already made, so don't reopen one unless something in this plan is impossible. If it is, make the smallest change that keeps the design and note it for your Engine Feedback.\n\n- **The game** is what this plan calls your game. You pick its title the way your TASK.md says, a playful riff on the source's name. A riff, not the name itself. No text in this plan depends on the title.\n- **`tide`** is the engine key for the game: scene name, code folder, asset pack. It never appears on screen.\n- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder. `engine/` is the engine clone inside it.\n- **[V]** marks something the planner tested on this computer on 2026-11-04 with the real engine. Everything else is design you'll be first to run.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n**Genre and perspective:** third-person, over-the-shoulder action-horror. You plant your feet to aim a laser sight, a crowd closes in, and precise shots set up melee follow-ups. Resources are tight, a trader keeps turning up, and set-pieces come one after another. The camera stays behind the right shoulder for the whole campaign, with scripted cuts for scenes.\n\n**Premise.** October 1979, North Atlantic. **Cass Harrow** (she/her), a deadpan Senior Field Adjuster for Ironbridge Mutual Marine & General, lands on **Saltmarrow**, a fog-bound fishing island, to close one claim: the Vane & Daughters Fine Tinned Fish cannery has reported a total loss. The islanders are polite, tidy and homicidal. They have taken the Brine, which seeds **saltroot**, a parasitic barnacle that lives in the marrow and rewrites its host. The island's tins are the delivery system, and ten million of them sail on the morning tide unless the owner signs the manifest. Cass has to find the owner, **Mrs. Ottoline Vane** (she/her, 74, unimpressed by everything), get her off the island, and stop the shipment. She does it while the Harbormaster fines her over the town tannoy for every crime she commits, and something very large waits under the harbor: **the Underwriter**.\n\n**Tone.** A straight-faced B-movie pulp thriller. The horror is real, the dialogue is cheesy on purpose, and nobody winks at the camera.\n\n**Pillars**\n1. **Stop. Breathe. Shoot.** Aiming plants your feet. Every shot is a choice about where the crowd will be in two seconds.\n2. **Shots open doors for boots.** Where you hit decides how the enemy reacts, and each reaction offers a melee follow-up. Good players string these together into chains.\n3. **The box is a puzzle and the trader is a friend.** Items are shapes in a grid. Cash, ammo and space are always slightly short. The blue lamp means you can breathe.\n4. **Always one more thing.** No two chapters play alike: siege, factory, escort on a rising tide, cliff ride, lighthouse, sea monster.\n5. **Paperwork is the joke, the barnacle is the threat.** Fines, forms and one-liners run alongside real menace.\n\n**What ships**\n- A five-chapter campaign, about 50 minutes first time through.\n- *Claims Rush*, a 1 to 4 player co-op score mode that plays in AI Hub sessions and alone.\n- Attract mode, dev mode, settings, saves, and a full 10-foot UI.\n- All content is data-driven, so the rest of the game could be built with the same tools (§16).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Originality rules (hard rules)\n\nThe game may share genre and mechanics with the source: planted over-the-shoulder aiming, body-part reactions, melee follow-ups, grid inventory, a travelling trader, weapon tuning, treasure combining, escort, crowd control. It must **not** recreate the source. Do not use any of these:\n\n- Names, characters, creatures, organisms, factions or places from the source. No secret-agent rescuing a president's daughter. No Spanish village, castle or military island. No head-burst parasite reveal.\n- The chainsaw man, the church bell that calls enemies away, the lake monster fought from a boat, a knife-fight rival, the attaché case, typewriter saves, herbs, or the trader's famous lines.\n- Its music, its logo, its look, or any of its dialogue, including its well-known jokes.\n\nEverything in this plan already has its own replacement (saltroot and barnacle whips, the foghorn and the walk into the sea, the tackle box, the shortwave logbook, the brass-helmeted Old Fathoms, the hook-and-winch Flenser). If you add content, invent it, and don't borrow from the source to fill a gap.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Verified facts and traps\n\nRead this section twice. Each item cost the planner real time.\n\n### Pins and build times [V]\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall.git engine && git -C engine checkout 49ef2ba\nmkdir -p tools/hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n  curl -fsS -o tools/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n```\n- Rust 1.98.1 and the wasm32 target are installed. `wasm-bindgen` 0.2.129 matches `Cargo.lock`.\n- From `engine/`: `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=8 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav` takes 4 min the first time and about 1 min after a core change.\n- `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=8 scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene tide --title \"<title>\" --description \"<line>\" --theme-color \"#0e1620\" --out <path>` takes 2 min the first time and about 1 min after, and gives an 11.4 MB page.\n- Native sim speed was about 21,000 ticks/s in a test room with 17 entities and no AI. Expect less with crowds, but still far above real time, so a scripted run through the whole game stays practical.\n- Adding a crate dependency (for example `js-sys` to `pav_app`) makes `--locked` fail until a plain `cargo build` has updated `Cargo.lock`. `build-web.sh` always uses `--locked`.\n- Read `engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It's short and accurate. Ignore its \"Report back\" format: your TASK.md's feedback questions replace it.\n\n### Browser and hub\n- **Chromium flags [V]. Use exactly these everywhere:**\n  `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`\n  - The shorter set in TASK.md's computer notes (`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`) kills the whole GPU channel as soon as 3 or more WebGPU copies share a browser. The log shows `SharedImageStub: Unable to create shared image`, then `A valid external Instance reference no longer exists`, then a wgpu `unwrap` panic and `RuntimeError: unreachable`, which fails the hub's \"no errors\" check. It looks like a game bug and isn't.\n  - With the full set, the engine's starter plus a JS stand-in for game logic **passed** the harness on `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&play=8000` at 2, 3 and 4 players, at 4 with `scenario=host-leave`, and at 2 with `scenario=server`. Each run took 10 to 20 s, and `ready()` fired within 2 s of load.\n- **Reading the server's port from a script [V]:** `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` block-buffers its \"port\" line on a pipe, so `readline()` hangs forever. Use `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`. Stop it by the PID you started.\n- `python3 -I` can't import Playwright here [V]. Run your own test scripts without `-I`.\n- **Software rendering is slow [V]:** about 1.5 fps at 1280×720 and 5 fps at 640×360 with the bare starter, and 2 to 3 fps at 640×360 with a dressed harbor scene. The sim catches up at most 8 ticks per frame (`simhost.rs`), so a browser runs at 10 to 40 ticks/s against the real 60. **Never play the game through in a browser.** Play it through natively (`pav`, `cargo test`). Browser tests only check boot, input routing, UI and the hub, at 640×360.\n- **First frame can take over 14 s to show on software rendering [V].** Hub-check's \"draws its screen\" test screenshots after about 7 s and needs 1,200 bytes more than a blank page, so a blank canvas fails it. Keep a DOM splash (title text on dark) over the canvas until the first frame has really presented (`queue.on_submitted_work_done`). This also helps real players.\n- WebGPU canvases do appear in headless screenshots with these flags [V], and the stub pad reaches `gilrs` [V]: the log says `gamepad connected`, and the engine's guide switches to gamepad.\n- `pack_single_html.py` refuses wasm-bindgen snippets. **No `inline_js` and no `#[wasm_bindgen(module=...)]`.** Put JS in `web/index.html` and bind with `#[wasm_bindgen] extern \"C\" { #[wasm_bindgen(js_namespace = __hub)] fn poll() -> String; }`.\n- `session.js` goes in unmodified, as the first script right after `<meta charset>`, exactly one copy. The packer needs exactly one `<title>`, `<meta name=\"description\">`, `<meta name=\"theme-color\">`, manifest link and `</body>` in `web/index.html`.\n- Harness key presses are synthetic and dispatched to `doc.activeElement` or `body`, so handle the card's Enter in a `window` listener in JS, not through winit (§11).\n- Handle `time-up` in JS: the sim may be running at 1 fps and the host has only 5 s to call `finish()`.\n\n### Engine traps\n- **Disk-only packs don't exist in the browser [V].** `LIB/quaternius_medieval_village/*` and `LIB/kenney_mini_dungeon/*` work in `pav` but show a red \"Room file errors\" panel in the build. Use only `BUILTIN/*`, `LIB/core/*`, `LIB/kaykit_adventurers/*` and your own shipped pack (§14.2).\n- **An emissive part above 0.5 lights the room [V].** A tiny laser dot at `emissive: 6` turned the whole harbor red. Small glows stay at 0.4 or below. Only real lamps go higher.\n- **`[view]` per area.** With tilt below 30° the view cuts away everything in front of the player unless you set `\"cutaway.front_cut\" = false`, and also `\"cutaway.height_cut\" = false` and `\"cutaway.fade\" = false`.\n- **Unknown block fields in TOML are ignored silently [V].** A legend's `material` did nothing until patched (§14.1 E1).\n- `NavGrid` only treats blocks with tops near y=0 as floor. Upper floors need E8.\n- A game's `[view]` only re-applies when `(room id, view_serial)` changes, and a rebuilt standalone room is always id 0. Bump `view_serial` on travel or the look won't change.\n- `InputFrame` is `Copy`, so `cmd` must stay `Copy`.\n- `Sim` keeps rewind history. Set `history_seconds = 0` in your `build`.\n- `Sim::raycast` excludes the player only. Bullets test bodies with your own capsule code (§5.2).\n- KayKit figures are chibi-proportioned [V]. Don't use them for people in this game.\n- Puppets default to `eyes_to_camera = 0.6`, a cheat for high cameras that slides the eyes toward the viewer. Set it to 0 on every look: this camera sits behind the head.\n\n---\n\n## 4. How it plays\n\n### 4.1 Controls\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick (half = walk, full = jog) | W A S D |\n| Run | Hold ✕ while moving | Hold Shift |\n| Look | Right stick | Mouse (pointer lock) |\n| Aim (plants feet) | Hold L2 | Hold right mouse |\n| Fire | R2 | Left mouse while aiming |\n| Knife | L1 | F, or left mouse when not aiming |\n| Interact and follow-up | ✕ | E, or left-click the highlighted thing under the crosshair |\n| Reload | □ | R |\n| Sidestep (while aiming or moving) | ○ | Space |\n| Quick turn | Left stick back + ○ | Q |\n| Throwable | R1 (aim first for an arc) | G |\n| Tackle Box | △ | Tab or I |\n| Map | Create | M |\n| Quick weapons | D-pad up, left, right | 1, 2, 3, plus mouse wheel to cycle |\n| Mrs. Vane: follow or wait | D-pad down | C |\n| Swap shoulder | R3 | V |\n| Pause | Options | Esc |\n\n- ✕ does the prompted action on press. With no prompt showing, holding it runs.\n- The hub owns Create + Options held together: ignore Options while Create is held.\n- In `gilrs`, `LeftTrigger` is L1 and `LeftTrigger2` is L2. Read L2 and R2 as analog with a 0.35 threshold.\n- Prompts show the action's glyph for the device used last (§12.2). Show a device change after a 0.3 s stick-noise filter.\n- When a menu is open, the D-pad or left stick moves focus, ✕ confirms, ○ goes back, and L1/R1 change tabs. The mouse hovers to move focus and clicks to activate. On keyboard, arrows or WASD move, Enter or E confirms, Esc goes back.\n\n### 4.2 Feel numbers (defaults, all sliders in dev mode, §13.2)\n\n| Thing | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Walk, jog, run | 1.9, 3.4, 5.2 m/s (no firing while running) |\n| Aim in, aim out | 0.18 s, 0.12 s |\n| Move speed while aiming | 0 (planted) |\n| Yaw while aiming, stick | 95°/s max, ease-in curve (stick²), 40% of that within 3° of a head (aim assist) |\n| Mouse look | 0.12°/count, no acceleration, no smoothing while aiming |\n| Sidestep | 0.38 s, 1.6 m, invulnerable for the first 0.22 s, 1.2 s cooldown |\n| Quick turn | 180° in 0.25 s |\n| Camera, moving | right-shoulder offset 0.42 m, arm 2.3 m, eye height 1.55 m, FOV 58° |\n| Camera, aiming | offset 0.5 m, arm 1.25 m, FOV 42°, 0.14 s ease |\n| Camera pitch | −50° to +60° |\n| Laser | muzzle to first hit, thin unlit red beam, dot at hit. 0.25° figure-8 sway. +0.6° bloom per shot, recovers in 0.35 s |\n| Hit-stop | 0.04 s on head hit, 0.08 s on kick, 0.25 s at 30% speed on the last kill of an encounter |\n| Rumble | shot 0.1 s, kick 0.15 s, damage 0.3 s (`vibrationActuator.playEffect` from JS, guarded) |\n\nThe camera spring-arm uses `Sim::raycast` from the shoulder to the ideal eye and pulls in 0.15 m before any hit. Below 0.7 m of arm, swap to the other shoulder.\n\n### 4.3 What the player can and can't do\n\nTeach these limits with one prompt each, the first time they matter:\n- You can't move while aiming. You can't fire while running. Reloading is interrupted by a sidestep.\n- You can't jump. Climbing, vaulting and dropping are contextual, with a prompt.\n- You can't save anywhere: saves happen at shortwave radios.\n- Kicks need a reeling enemy. The knife always works but barely hurts.\n\n**How to Play**, in the pause menu, is 6 big one-idea cards: Aim, Follow-ups, Box, Trader, Mrs. Vane, Saves.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Combat\n\n### 5.1 Hero\n100 HP, shown as a ring gauge. Damage causes a 0.4 s flinch, then 0.8 s of invulnerability. At 0 HP, the screen says **POLICY LAPSED** with *Reinstate* (retry from checkpoint), *Load Log*, *Quit*.\n\n### 5.2 Hit detection\nHitscan rays, except harpoon, thrown items and flare, which are projectiles.\n- Each ray goes from the muzzle to the camera ray's hit point.\n- Test static world with `Sim::raycast`, and bodies with capsules built from `pav_core::puppet::biped_skel(def, &anim)`: head sphere, torso, two arms, two legs. Creatures get authored sphere lists.\n- This is deterministic because it derives from sim state.\n\n### 5.3 Body-part reactions\n\n| Part | Damage mult | Reaction (any hit dealing ≥ 8 cumulative in 1 s) | Follow-up offered |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Head | ×2, plus a 12% crit that does ×5 | **Reel** 1.6 s, hands to face | **Roundhouse** ✕: 30 dmg, 2.4 m radius, 200° front arc, knocks down everyone hit |\n| Leg | ×0.75 | **Kneel** 1.4 s | **Ledger Slam** ✕: 45 dmg to the target, knockdown |\n| Arm | ×0.75 | Interrupts the swing, 30% chance to drop the tool (then unarmed) | none |\n| Torso | ×1 | Short flinch | none |\n| Knocked down | ×1 | Down 2.0 s | **Finish** ✕ knife: ×3 knife damage, ignores armor |\n\nFollow-ups have a 2.5 m reach, 0.5 s of hero invulnerability and a one-liner chance of 20%. The prompt floats at the target's head. That chain (head shot → kick → crowd down → finish) is the core of the game. Make it feel good first.\n\n### 5.4 Weapons\nDamage assumes 40 HP for a basic enemy. Box size is grid cells W×H.\n\n| Weapon | Box | Mag | s/shot | Reload | Damage | Notes | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Issue .38 | 3×2 | 10 | 0.38 | 1.5 s | 10 | Accurate, crits | Start |\n| Trench Pump | 6×2 | 6 | 0.95 | 0.55 s/shell | 7 pellets × 8, 6° cone | Full reel at ≤ 6 m, falls off past 8 m | Harbor Office, Ch1 |\n| Stitcher | 5×2 | 30 | 0.09 | 2.2 s | 3.5 | Climbs, shreds Bloats, poor stagger | £4,000, Ch2 |\n| Seal Rifle | 7×1 | 5 | 1.30 | 2.6 s | 38, pierces 2 | R3 toggles ×3 zoom while aiming | Ch4 hut, or £5,500 from Ch3 trader |\n| Harpoon Gun | 6×2 | 1 | n/a | 1.8 s | 70 | **Pins** target to the wall behind if within 3 m. Walk over a spent harpoon to retrieve it | Ch3 crypt |\n| Flare Pistol | 2×2 | 1 | n/a | 1.6 s | 6 + burn 20/s for 3 s | Blinds 6 m radius 3 s, instantly kills a Rooted bulb, lights fog | Gift, Mrs. Vane, Ch3 |\n| Filleting Knife | none | n/a | 0.45 | n/a | 6 | Always on L1. Breaks crates. No ammo | Start |\n\n**Throwables** (1×2, stack 3):\n- *Stick*: dynamite, 4 m radius, 90 dmg, 2.2 s fuse.\n- *Magnesium*: stuns 8 m radius for 3 s, kills Rooted.\n- *Oil Lamp*: 3 m fire patch, 5 s, 15 dmg/s.\n\n**Tune-ups at the trader**, three tracks per weapon:\n\n| Track | L1 | L2 | L3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Damage | +20% | +45% | +80% |\n| Reload | −15% | −30% | −45% |\n| Capacity | +30% | +60% | +100% |\n\n- Prices: £800, £1,600, £3,200, × weapon factor (.38 ×1, Pump ×1.2, Stitcher ×1.2, Rifle ×1.5, Harpoon ×1.5, Flare ×1).\n- All three tracks maxed unlocks an **Exclusive** for £6,000: .38 *Hair Trigger* (crit ×2 chance), Pump *Choke* (3° cone), Stitcher *Drum* (mag ×2), Rifle *Straight Pull* (0.6 s/shot), Harpoon *Reel* (auto-retrieves), Flare *Over-and-Under* (mag 2).\n- The final Whaling Cannon is a level prop, not an item.\n\n### 5.5 Enemies\n\nBipeds use the puppet plus worn props (§14). Attack tokens: at most 3 enemies attack at once (Rank changes this), and the rest circle at 2.5 to 4 m and shuffle, so crowds surround you rather than pile up.\n\n| Enemy | Plan | HP | Speed | Attack | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Parishioner** | biped | 40 | 1.4, runs 3.0 past 12 m at 30% | Tool swing, 0.55 s windup, 1.6 m, 14 dmg. Grab from side or back (hold, others hit you, mash ✕ to break) | Base enemy. Starts on a chore (gutting, mending nets, praying). Yell alerts 15 m. Rank ≥ 6: sidesteps if laser held on it > 0.7 s past 5 m (15%). Breaks doors in 2 kicks, climbs ladders, vaults windows |\n| **Net-Wife** | biped | 35 | 1.2 | Thrown net, 9 m: slows 70% for 2.5 s, mash to tear | Keeps 6 m away. Priority target |\n| **Tinner** | biped | 35 | 1.3 | Lobs lit fish-oil tins, 12 m arc, 2 s fuse, 3 m radius, 35 dmg | Shoot the tin in hand: blows up on them and neighbours |\n| **Rooted** | biped + whip chain | 60 (bulb ×3) | 1.6 | Barnacle whip, 3 m sweep, 22 dmg | A \"dead\" Parishioner may burst into this (§7 sets chance per chapter). Stands back up, chest splits, 3 to 5 verlet whips with a pale bulb. Flare or Magnesium kills at once. On death, spawns 1 Scuttler at Rank ≥ 5 |\n| **Scuttler** | spider | 15 | 4.5 | Leap, 8 dmg | Small and fast. Shotgun or kick |\n| **Sea Hound** | quadruped | 25 | 6.0 | Bite, 10 dmg | Packs of 3 to 5, circle, then rush in pairs |\n| **Bloat** | blob | 30 | 0.9 | Bursts in 3.5 m: 30 dmg, poison cloud 4 s, 8 dmg/s | Shoot it far away. Blast hurts other enemies |\n| **Bailiff** | biped, large | 150 + 4 barnacle plates (30 each) | 1.3 | Anchor-chain sweep, 30 dmg, 3.2 m | Plates block damage and reel until shot off. Kick staggers only after plates are gone |\n\n**Dynamic Rank** (hidden, 1 to 10, starts at 4):\n- Rises with accuracy and no-damage streaks, falls with deaths and low health.\n- Scales enemy damage (×0.75 to ×1.3), tokens (2 to 4), sidestep chance and Rooted chance (±10%).\n- Dev mode shows and can override it.\n- *New Claim* offers three difficulties: **Assistant** (Rank locked ≤ 3), **Adjuster** (default), **Senior Adjuster** (Rank starts at 7, enemy HP ×1.25).\n\n### 5.6 Bosses\n\n**The Flenser** (Mr. Grice from Gutting) (Ch2, returns in Ch5 and as Claims Rush's final wave). 3 m tall biped, rubber apron, iron mask.\n- Walks with a ratcheting winch on its back. You hear it first: a loud, unmistakable clack-clack-clack.\n- Hook-on-chain thrown 12 m: on hit, drags you to it for a Gut (80 dmg unless you mash ✕ in 1.2 s, then 20).\n- HP 900; only the belly tank takes full damage, and the rest is ×0.3.\n- Shooting the belt lantern sets it alight and stuns 3 s.\n- Ch2 ending: lure it onto the press platform and hit the button, or deplete it. The press kill pays £3,000.\n\n**The Bell-Hermit** (Ch3 boss, flooded crypt, HP 2,200). A cathedral-sized hermit crab (beetle plan, scale 2.6) wearing the cracked chapel bell as its shell.\n- *Phase 1:* charges along the crypt, shells off pillars. Shots at the bell spark and do nothing, except they ring it, and enough rings (6) agitate it into a slam.\n- Shoot the eyes (×2) to make it rear up. Shoot the hanging iron clapper chain when it's under the clapper: the clapper strikes the bell, it reels out of the shell for 6 s, and the soft abdomen takes ×3.\n- *Phase 2 (below 50%):* water rises to shin depth (wading ×0.7), Scuttlers spill from the shell, pillars are gone. Kill by repeating the expose loop, or finish with sticks into the open shell.\n\n**The Underwriter** (Ch5 finale, flooded Quay at night). See Ch5, §7. Bulbs 6 × 400 HP, then the mouth takes 3 Whaling Cannon charges.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Economy and items\n\n### 6.1 The Tackle Box (grid inventory)\nA tin fisherman's box with fold-out trays. It starts 10×6, upgrades to 12×7 (£3,000) and 14×8 (£8,000). Every item is a rectangle W×H that may rotate 90°. Weapons, ammo, healing and treasures all take space. A separate **Key Items** page holds no-space items (keys, fuses, forms). If a pick-up won't fit, the box opens with the item on the cursor: place it, discard something, use something, or leave it.\n\n| Item | Box | Stack | Effect or value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| .38 rounds | 1×1 | 30 | |\n| Shells | 1×1 | 12 | |\n| Stitcher mag | 1×1 | 60 rounds | |\n| Rifle rounds | 1×1 | 10 | |\n| Harpoon | 2×1 | 3 | |\n| Flares | 1×1 | 4 | |\n| Sea Moss | 1×2 | 1 | +30 HP |\n| Iodine | 1×2 | 1 | Combine only |\n| Tincture (Moss + Iodine) | 1×2 | 1 | Full heal |\n| Strong Tincture (Moss + Moss + Iodine) | 1×2 | 1 | Full heal, +10 max HP once per Tincture |\n| Tinned Kippers | 2×1 | 1 | +50 HP (\"Best before: ask again later.\") |\n| Pearl | 1×1 | 3 | £800 |\n| Gold Tooth | 1×1 | 5 | £300 |\n| Scrimshaw | 1×2 | 1 | £1,800 |\n| Ambergris | 2×2 | 1 | £4,000 |\n| Sextant parts (Frame + Mirror + Scale) | 1×1 each | | £600 each, £7,500 combined |\n\n**Combining** is *Combine* on one item, then pick another (no drag-and-drop required).\n\n### 6.2 Drops\nEnemy drop roll: 40% nothing, 25% cash (£100 to £300), 28% ammo for a carried weapon (weighted to whatever you're lowest on), 7% Sea Moss, doubled when under 35% HP. Crates and barrels roll the same table at +20% drop. Campaign total cash is about £40,000, enough to fully tune about 2 weapons, which forces a choice. Picking up is automatic when walking over cash and ammo; other items need ✕ so they can't be taken by accident.\n\n### 6.3 Old Fathoms (trader)\nA man in a full brass diving helmet and canvas suit, who walks up out of the water whenever an electric-blue storm lamp is lit. There are 6 lamps: Net Lane, Loading Dock, Halfway Hut, the Cliff Road hut, lighthouse floor 2, and the Quay before the Underwriter. Nobody ever sees how he got there. Music changes to his warm concertina waltz (§15); enemies never spawn near a lit lamp. Three tabs: **Buy**, **Sell**, **Tune**. Each shows at most 6 big rows with a 3D turntable of the selected item (§12.4).\n\n**Tin Gulls.** 15 tin gull targets on posts around the island, shot on sight. Fathoms pays £500 each, and all 15 unlocks a free Exclusive. Treasure Charts (£1,000 per chapter) mark treasures on the map.\n\n### 6.4 Saves\nA **shortwave radio** in safe spots: *Log Report* saves. Autosave at each checkpoint and chapter start. Save JSON goes to `localStorage` (versioned): area, checkpoint, box grid, weapons and tracks, cash, flags per area (crates broken, pickups taken, encounters cleared), Rank, stats, settings. Three manual slots plus one auto. `Continue` loads the newest. In a hub session the client hands you empty storage, and you save nothing.\n\n### 6.5 Chapter report\nAfter each chapter: time, accuracy, kills, follow-ups, deaths, cash, then a grade (S to D). After Ch5: the Claim Settlement screen, total grade, and unlocks: the *Chapters* menu entry, *Claims Rush* costume colours, the second-run *Actuary* difficulty.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Campaign\n\nEach area is one room file under `engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/tide/levels/`. Sizes are in metres. Treat the beats as a script and the layouts as firm guidance. Keep areas within about 110 m on a side: fog at 46 m hides the edges.\n\n**Chapter data** (chance a killed Parishioner becomes Rooted): Ch1 0%, Ch2 15%, Ch3 25%, Ch4 35%, Ch5 45%.\n\n**Standard area kit:** ASCII `[layout]` + `[game]` numbers + `[[encounter]]` + `[[object]]`/`[[npc]]`, see §16.\n\n### Chapter 1: Notice of Loss (`quay`, dawn fog, 85×65 m, about 13 min)\n```\n   N\n  ###########  ##########  ########        # = buildings   ~ = sea\n  #NET LOFT#=  #OFFICE S#  #CHAPEL #       . = cobbles     = = planks\n  ###.....##    ##.......   ###....        T = blue lamp   S = radio\n   ...HOUSE..  ...SQUARE.......            G = gate north to Cannery Road\n   . NET LANE ...(statue)...  G            P = mail pier\n  ##..T.####..........#####\n  ~~~~===========~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  ~~~~~~~~~CUSTOMS====P===~~~   (pier runs south-east)\n```\n1. **Mail pier.** Skipper Bram's boat drops Cass and leaves. Radio with Tull. Teach move, look, run. Foghorn far off.\n2. **Customs Shed.** A Parishioner with their back turned, gutting. \"Anything to declare?\" Teach aim, shoot, head shot, kick prompt. The **Tackle Box** on the counter: box tutorial with a too-big item. First radio (save). First cash.\n3. **Net Lane.** Narrow lane under drying nets. Shoot the rope to drop a fish rack on a group (trap teach). Crates, first Tin Gull, first Tinner on a roof (teach shooting the tin).\n4. **The House on Net Lane.** Enemies pour in. Push the dresser against the door (hold ✕), they kick through anyway, vault out of the window, kick the ladder off the roof. Tannoy wakes up: Pell's first citation.\n5. **First blue lamp.** Old Fathoms walks up the slip. Buy, Sell, Tune tutorial with a free tune-up credit.\n6. **Harbor Square siege.** 40×35 m plaza, statue of St. Orran with a herring, tannoy horns on poles, two ladders to a roof walkway, doors that open and close. Gates lock on entry. Waves: up to 8 at a time, about 22 total, Tinner on roof, Net-Wife late. Ends at 18 kills or 3:00.\n   - **The foghorn sounds. Every Parishioner stops, sets down their tool, turns and walks calmly into the sea.**\n   - Cass: \"Either that's the tide or the union.\"\n   - Build this moment carefully. It's the game's first big image.\n7. **Harbor Office.** Trench Pump on the wall rack, radio, Pell's ledger, Cannery Gate key. Pell over the desk radio: full introduction. Gate north. Chapter report.\n\n### Chapter 2: Fine Tinned Fish (`cannery`, 70×55 m, two floors, machine light, about 13 min)\nReuse conveyor zones (`zone = { kind = \"conveyor\", facing, speed }`, carries characters and props).\n1. **Yard.** Stacked crates, Hounds. Tin stacks fall if shot. Trader at the Loading Dock (Stitcher for sale).\n2. **Gutting Floor.** Long conveyors with Parishioners on the line, a moving hook-rail that hits for 20 (dodge or lure enemies under it), catwalks above with Net-Wives. Power is out, so doors are shut.\n3. **Boiler Room puzzle.** Find 3 fuses (one in Retort Room, one on the catwalk, one on a corpse in Cold Corridor), fit them in the board: Key Items flow. When the third goes in, lights come up, machinery starts, **first Rooted reveal** on the body you killed a minute ago.\n4. **The Flenser.** Ratchet sound through the walls. It hunts you through Boiler, Corridor and Retort. Retort Room has 6 pressure tanks: shoot a valve to scald anything near it (25 dmg + stun). You can't kill it here easily. Pell: \"Mr. Grice from Gutting will see you now.\"\n5. **Press Room fight.** Lure the Flenser onto the press plate, hit the button (✕). Or shoot it down.\n6. **Cold Store.** Mrs. Vane sitting on a crate, in a fur coat, handbag on knee. \"You're late.\" Escort starts. She has the manifest; Pell needs her signature. Way out: the causeway. Radio. Chapter report.\n\n### Chapter 3: Turning Tide (`causeway` 110×24 m and `crypt` 40×40 m, overcast dusk, rain, about 13 min)","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":959}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V7zBZVQWhp1TrsRtH9WJMW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
